      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>' PLEASANT 'PARAGRAPH&amp;</head>
        <p>' PLEASANT 'PARAGRAPH&amp;</p>
        <p>THE safest way to approach a mule is to go the other way around the earth. A CHEERFUL A DVERTISICMENT. --" Wanted, a young woman who can cook and dress the chil- dren.' Poor little dears!</p>
        <p>"CAN you help me to re-cover my child?" asked the poor woman. "Is your child lost ? " "Oh no. His clothes are worn out." FIRST COUNTRY BOY: " Wer be 'e a-going Jim? Seccnd Country Boy : "Ain't a-going no.wheres." F. C. B. : "Yes you be." S. C. B.: "No I bain't, I be a-coming back." "BILLY, I believe IM in love I" "What makes you thine to, Tommy ?'' "Cause I washes my neck and com`)s my hair without being told." " WELL, air, what- do you think of my new drama?" " Splendid. 'Lie villains in particular are admirably portrayed. The very words they utter are stolen ? ' Wato's TO BLAME ?—Wife (to her husband) : "I say, my dear,, how badly the tailor has put this button on your waistcoat I This is the fifth tune I have had to sew it on again." EMPLOYER : "Mr. Redink, you got off yesterday afternoon under the plea of being ill; I saw you afterwards going to the races, and you didn't appear to be at all unwell." " Employe : " You ought to have seen me after the second race, sir, I was bad enough then!" "WHAT to you," she asked, in dreamy accents, "is the most beautiful thing about the suottet?" He thought for some time, and that!. replied, "It reminds me of supper." WISHING to pay his friend a compliment, a Fentleman re narked: "I hear you have a very industrious wife." " Yes," replied his friend, with a melancholy smile, "she is never idle. She is always finding something for me to do." A GENTLEMAN, in jumping off a tramcar, fell, and mile I into the gutter. While brushing the dirt off his clothes a little girl ran up to him and said, "Mister, please do it again. Mamma didn't see you that time.'' MISTRESS : " Norah, you must always sweep behind the doors." New Servant : " Yes'rn ; I always does. It's the easiest iv ay of gettin' the durrit out of sight." IT is said that man in a savage state does not have the toothache. We are more inclined to the belief that any man who has the toothache is in a savage state. Docrott : "How .is the baby?" Mrs. Jinke : "Offal had, sir. Last night the poor little thing was took dreffle. First she would clinch her hands, and then she would say A-h-b,' just like a human being." A LADY who advertised for a girl "to do light house-work" received a letter from an applicant who said her health demanded sea air, and asked where the lighthouse was situated. MANY a man who thinks he can rule the world changes his mind when a wasp gets after him. Mits. LAKESIDE "I don't see why you can't marry Mr. Flatpuree." Daughter: "Let me please myself this time, ma, and I'll try to please you the next time." SCENE—dodoes house. Little boy (at the front door): "Is the doctor in! 'Cause, if he is, I want to see him at once." Servant : "He's not in." Little boy: "Well, jest as soon as he gets home, you tell him to corns over to our house and take that baby away he left last week. It's in the way." THERE is a girl who boasts of having a watch that keeps correct time. She was heard to remark not long ago, as she pulled it out, "If the sun isn't over the hill in a minute and a-half he will be late." "Ir'ealways a relief to me when it comes time to pay Susan, said Mrs. Howeskeep. "Why ?" inquired her husband. "Because it is the only time when I feel positive that she doesn't employ me." " GOOD-MORNIN', Mrs. Bryan. An' how is your brother-in-law, Mr. McCafferty, to-day ? Very bad indade, Miss Corcoran—very bad ludade. Sorra a bite does lie ate except what he drinks." CUSTOMER: "I say, Mr. Shortweight, that cheese is full of holes." Shortweighta "Yes, that's the way it comes." Customer: "Well,' don't want to pay for half-a- pound of holes." IN A TERRIBLE CONDITIOV. —Wife "Where are you going, John? It's getting very late." Husband (who has been reading a patent-medicine almanac) : " " I'm going to see a doctor if I live to find one." "IT seems to me that you ask a pretty good price for this parrot." "Yes, but you know he belonged to one of the best families." "How do you know?" "lie always talks when anyone begins to sing." A usweevaraer clergyman was one day talking with him landlord on the personality of the devil. The gentleman remarked : "I should like to see the devil." "Can't you wait, my good follow ? " was the reply. mint (to bird fancier): "The parrot I bought yesterday awears like a trooper in Spanish." Bird Fancier; " Well, sir, you can't exp:ot the bird to master Eaglish in a week." LAD : "And what does your father do ?" Little Girl : "Oh, papa is a doctor." Lady: " Indeed I suppose h practices a great deal, does he not?" Little Girl: "Oh, no; tie doesn't practice any more. He knows how now." IMPATIENT GUEST (at seaside): " Where in thunder is that waiter ?" Head Waiter : "Was he 43, sir?" Impatient Guest: "lie may have been 42 when I gave the order, but he's 50 by this time." LITTLE FLAXF.N HAIR : "Papa, it's raining." Papa (annoye( fly work in hand) : " Well, let it rain.' Little Flaxen Hair (timidly): "I was going to." SMALL SON: "Pa, what is a bachelor?" Hen- pecked parent: "A bachelor is a man who has resisted all endeavours on the part of woman to render his life miserable through marriage." A MISLINDICRSTANDING.—" I thought you adver- tised that there were long walks to be had from this hotel?" "No; I said the neighbourhood was -renowned for its tramps." Wire, INDEED. Aunt Janet: " What do you call that ? ' Nephew from the city : "It's a trouser stretcher." Aunt Janet : "A trouser stretcher ! Why don't you get your trousers big enough, so's you don't have to stretoh 'ern?" "NOTHING can make a woman so superlatively happy as to have a baby of her own to kiss," ex- claimed Mrs. Mcßride rapturously, as she fondled her first horn. "My dear," rep:ied her husband pityingly, "you can never know the unutterable my of being 'Next' in a crowded barber's shop on a Saturday night." A LITTLE boy entered a surgery the other day. On his catching sight of the doctor, he whispered quietly in his ears : "Please, sir, mother wants to know is measleB catching?' " Tell yoer mother, of course, they are," replied the doctor. The boy, not being satisfied with this, exclaimed : "Please, -sir, mother wants to know what you will give her to spread them about the ? Lly si4er's got 'em awful r' " WHAT was Helen crying about, Polly ?" asked Folly's mamma, as ;the little one came in from the playground. "She dug a great big hole in the garden, and her mamma wouldn't let her take it into the house with her," said Polly. THE only way of solving the problem, "Is marriage a failure ?" is to try it. It reminds us of the story anent the toadstool and the mushroom. How can you tell a mus'a.oona from a toadstool! By eating it. If it is a toadstool you die; if it is a mushroom, you don't. AN art Critic, deccribing a recent collection of bric-a-brac, says "The visitor's eye will be struck on entering the room, with a porcelain umbrella." Coe:gunman; mother (i.o governese) : "Mies Smith, don't, let Alfred an I Jennie sit down on the damp grass for fear they should catch cold. When they are tired you can sit down and take them on your lap." "MARRIAGES are made in heaven," quoth Miss Antique. "Then there is some chance for you yet," was the cruel response of her younger sister. YOUNG MAN (I 0 provision merchant) : "Your daughter and I, air, have agreed to row down the river of life together." Provision merchant (sar- castically) "Have you got any provisions on board!" Young man "NO, sir; considering your business, we thought the victualling depart- ment was more in your line !" WHAT Is IT ?—Masher : "You have been look ing straight at me for the last five minutes. What is it ?" Farmer • " That's just what I was wonder- ing."</p>
