        <head>'CAPITAL" CHAT.</head>
        <p>'CAPITAL" CHAT.</p>
        <p>There is a discussion now proceeding .about the origin of the smock frock of the -agricultural labourer. There can be little doubt that it comae from the Arab. The serape worn in Mexico and the poncho of the South American are similar, and they were exported from Spain, which in turn derived them from the Moors. -Mr. Rental'. who, deplores the disappearance of the smock from Essea,ie struck with the resemblance of it to the" tunicles " worn by deacons in our churches prior to the Reforma- tion. His ingenious theory that the -economical ecclesiastics sold their cast-off robes,and that the peasantry bought and copied the elaborate needle- work about the yoke,cannot for a moment be enter- tained. The smock exists in all_countries among the peasantry. It is merely an evolution of what would be close to the primeval attire. A sackositb a hole for the head and two for the aransovould be among the earliest devices for a wardrobe. The Roman toga was a sample which most probably was adopted from the East, and to-day the Arab wears a covering which differs very slightly from the smock frock of the agricultural labourer.</p>
        <p>Some more Eastern potentates are sending their sons to be educated in England. To-day Prince Svasta Sobhon, stepbrother of the King cf ,Siam. arrives in London, bringing with him two sons of the Ring, who are to ne placed in a public school. The Prince will take the opportunity affordellby his vieit to confer with the British and iFrenah Governments upon the question of neutral terri- tory.</p>
        <p>scene: The Underground Railway. Three.ladiss enter a crowded carriage. A gentleman gallantly rises, "Allow me to offer my seat to the eldest of the ladies.' Not one of them budges,and the gentle- man resumes his place.</p>
        <p>Baltimore has recently been celebrating with Much pomp the 25th anniversary—the Jubilee, as our cousins term it— of the American Cardinal Gibbon's entry into the episcopate. The occasion was one of exceptional interest. The cathedral in which the ceremony was performed was the first Roman Catholic cathedral ever built in the United States. Within its walls the Carninal was baptised, for he is a Baltimorean by birth. A phonographic message from Pope Leo 2C.111. formed, it can be understood, not the least interesting feature of the dinner which in the evening celebrated the morn- ing's ceremonial, one thatrhad been attended by all the Archbishops of America and by the Papal dele- gate, Mgr. Satolli.</p>
        <p>Cardinal G;bbons, who is just 59, has not unitly been termed the Prince of the Church in America, In Baltimore he is a favourite even with his theo- logical opponents. Socially he is a well-known figure. As a Temperance reformer he is almost as warm a zealot as Cardinal Manning, whose ascetic- ism he to a slight extent follows, but he is more a ma % of the world and in 'the world than the late Archbishop of Westminster. He is regarded as a brilliant diplomat, and has handled the difficulties which in America beset the Boman Catholic Church, with a skill that to a.great extent explains how it is that not a few of his admirers have permitted themz:elves to dream of the possibility of an American-born Pope occupying the throne of St. Peter.</p>
        <p>Custance, who steered Thormonby, Lord Lyon, George Frederick to victory in the Derby, promises shottly a volume of " Riding Recollections and Hunting Stories." This should be interesting, for Custance is one of the few jockeys that could ride. Paradoxical as it may seem, nevertheless it is true that the majority of our famous jockeys are not. horsemen. Let a horse give a bit of a buck, or bolt and jump at the rails, and where are the riders? On tl.eir backs. But Custance could, and did, as Adam Gordon, tin) first and best poet ot the horse, wrote, '• sit the pick of the Quorn." He was a good man to bounds, during the time be was riding upon the flat, and few, if any, jockeys can boast of this re- putation, For" years he was among the first flight of Leicestershire, and though " Brooksby "has so fully and so picturesquely covered- the period over which Custance's hunting reminiscences wander, nevertheless there is much that is interesting which is unrecorded.</p>
        <p>There was a tremendous crush at the Padereweki recital en Tuesday, the total receipts just touching four figures. It .was the Polish virtuoso's first appearance in London this season, and the anxiety to hear him was, it possible, more pronourk:ed than ever. Ills prqgramme began with Bach's" uhroma- tic Fantasia," and the first part further comprised Beethoven's sonata in D minor, op. 31, and Schu- mann's sonata in F sharp minor, op. 11—both magnificently played and rapturously applauded. In a lengthy (Chopin selection M. Paderewski was heard quite at his best. Ile gave the balltidi in F with some marvellous effects of contrast, and fol- lowed this up by creating a genuine sensation with the etude in A minor from op. 25, the rendering of which furnished a memorable tour de force. Among the remaining items there was one novelty, an air with variations from M. Paderewski's "English Suite," a work still in manuscript, but to be pub- lished shortly. The air turned out to be "Home, Sweet Home," and the variations, seven in number, are extremely ingenious and difficult. The audi- ence, however, did not appear to be greatly impresed, and displayed much more enthusiasm alter a Liszt " Rhapsody "later on.</p>
        <p>But for the presence of Mr. Pickard the meeting of Liberal members of Parliament held on Wednes- day afternoon at the National Liberal Club would have gone much further in its expression of sym- pathy with the miners and its endorsement of their demand for a minimum wage. Opinion was, prac- tically, unanimously on the side of the men, not only in the present acute crisis but also in their fundamental contentions. Two amendments tend- ing towards nationalisation were ruled out of order (evidently tr. Hutton thought a London County. Councillor could teach members of Parliament something in the despatch of business), but these will come up for consideration again. The adop- tion of a definite course of action was postponed until after the joint conference, at the express wish of Mr. Pickard, who himself preserved a complete reticence. There will be a fresh meeting next Wed- nesday, when if peace be not made there will be more complete freedom of speech, and when a plan of campaign will probably be arranged. Meanwhile Mr. Conybeare and other Radical members main- tain their freedom of action, and one of the first sittings of Parliament will almost certainly witness a motion for adjournment to call attention to the whole subject.</p>
        <p>_ • Sir Henry Loch, whose natne has been much brought to the fore of late in connection with the present troubles in Matabeleland is an official who has literally grown grey in the service of hiecoun- try. Sir Henry was yet a young man when his truly awful expeliences out in China with the mis- sion of Lord Elgin—the grandfather of the present Governor-General of India—left their traces deep upon his face and figure. In a ZOW little-known volume he published many years ago,he has modest- ly told his fearful sufferings when placed in an iron cage by his captors and half-starved for weeks. It was only by the kindly intervention of one of the higher officials in chargethat be escarbd—by a few moments—the sentence of death despatched from headquarters.</p>
        <p>Since those distant days—it is 35 years ego—Sir Henry's lines have been cast in less risky places, viz., as Lieutenant-Governot of the little Home- Ruled Isle of Man, where he passed nearly 20 un- eventful years, and later in the more distinguished pcsition of Governor of Victoria, and still more re- cently in the same capacity at the tape, in all three of which latter posts he had been most ably assisted in his social duties by his charming wife, the twin sister of the late Lord Lytton.</p>
        <p>"City's CocoA. has, in a remarkable degree, those natural elements of sustenauce which give the system endur- ance and hardihood, with a steady action that renders It a m it acceptable and reliable beverage."—Hea/t/i.</p>
        <p>Mr. Carter Harrison, the Mayor of Chicago, who was so brutally murdered, mai a big-bodied, much-bewhis- kered, long-haired man, and with a smile so honest that he made you feel at ease in a moment. Mr. Harrison had travelled over the world, talk-A French and German like a native, and was a graduate of Yale. Men the Czar of Russia is on a visit to his fatherand mether-in-law, the King and Queen of Dmmark,nothing delights him more than to wheel the little children of the Princess Waldemar of Denmark about in their baby carriage.</p>
        <p>gibe -Royal Laundresses sRy Critchley's " STARCH GLOSS is unequalled for impartintr a beautitni finish to starched I nen, &amp;c., either cold or bolliag water starch. Mr. —, Corn- wall. says My wife will not finish our linen without-your "Starch Gloss." Front Mrs. —, Bradford : "Dear alt.— am pleased to inform you that I have tried many so-called Starch Glazes and Polishes, but find none equal to yours." It should be used in every house. Bold at Id., 3d., and 6d., by moat starch sellers. If not in stock will yr:nulls It. T. CRIT,NILEY, BY.sokburn, F.:rr!ctcr.—Ad-1.</p>
