      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <p>THE EXAMINER. SATURDAY,, NOVEMBER 4, 1893.</p>
        <p>little summer villa at Rosenau, and when there she has five o'clock tea on the lawn every day. For tea she has, amongst other things, large [Marl potatoes "In their jackets !" a homely tat c which will rather surprise those who will persist in regarding Her Royal Highness as the essence of stiff, starched formality.</p>
        <p>Cocky-leekie (a Scotch dish.) A gallon and a half of water is put into a broth pan and allowed to boil, a pair of good plump fowls are trussed, and put in with agessert spoonftll of salt.,Leeks are cut up finely, and added in great quantity,"as they are the predomioating feature in this broth; other veget- ables, such as parsley, and,a little turnip cut finely, are sometimes added. This is boiled for two hours, the fowls are then taken out, cut into joints of a convenient size, and returned to the broth, pepper and salt to taste being added. It is boiled again for a quarter of an hour, and is then ready to serve. The leeks are put in In such quantity as to make the broth quite thick.; but if so may be not liked, breadcruinbs are often added for thickening. It is served with boiled potatoes and bread.</p>
        <p>ILIAC rt</p>
        <p>CHAPTER Ix. ONB NIGHT'S MYSTERY.</p>
        <p>PARTED BY FATE;</p>
        <p>OR THE MYSTERY OF BLACK-TOR LIGHTHOUSE.</p>
        <p>BY LAURA JEAN LIBBEY,</p>
        <p>Author of ."lone, the Pride of the. Mill," Au., &amp;a.</p>
        <p>As he spoke he threw back the folds of the heavy cloak which enveloped his fair captive. The blaze of strong light for an instant nearly blinded Verlie. Then, tids her eyes became accustomed to the dazzling light, she found that she was in a high, rooky cavern. Rough slab benches were ranged around the walls' and formed Its only article of furniture, and upon these benches lounged a score of men—a fierce, dark, bloodthirsty-looking crow, from whose nauti- cal air Verlie at once knew thorn to be sailors. All were armed with pistols and daggers stuck in their belts, And wore heavy black crape masks pulled low over their swarthy faces. With a f3eling of sick terror, Verlie staggered back against the wall, as the dreadful conviction that she bad fallen into a den of pirates or smug- glers forced itself upon her mind. In a few words her captor explained the situation to the men, adding, thoughtfully 3 "As she witnessed the duel, and in that partieu!ar locality, our safety demanded that she must be bound by an oath of silence, ere she is permitted to go free." " D.3n't let her go, captain. She will turn Informer," shouted every one present, simultane- " Silence I" said the man whom they addressed as captain, with a sudden flash of his eye, and in a tone that made the boldest quail. "You have no right to make me your prisoner," panted Verlie, indignantly, "and I demand my freedom. I can gee you are a cowardly set of law- breakers, and I know you must be the pirates who infest the harbour, and for whose apprehen- sion suoh large rewards have been offered by the authorities; but to waylay and entrap defenceless girls is a crime which ought to be too mean for even outlaws."</p>
        <p>A tierce murmur ran through the cavern at Ver. lie'a daring words ; but no one dared expreaa his anger aloud. . _ _</p>
        <p>" Allow me to set you right; fair lady," replied her captor, with a cynical smile. "We did not entrap you. You threw yourself into our power. And" (here he lowered hie voice to a significant whisper) "let me beg of you to use more respect- ful language when speaking of us. There is a spirit in my men that your words will be apt to arouse, and which I may find it difficult to subdue I fun sorry it is not in my power to comply with your request. Neither I nor my men would feel safe in this retreat afterwards if we let you go free.</p>
        <p>"In short, it is my painful duty to imform you that you must remain with us, at least until such time as this harbour cavern is deserted by us, unless as I said before, you are willing to take a solemn oath nevet to reveal what you have seen or heard this night. Will you take that oath 71 "No," cried Verlie, indignantly.; "not to save myself from deatli, much less imprisonment, would I see you escape the punishment of the law you so richly deserve. You, above all, should face the gallows, for I saw you take a unman life to-night. "The very moment I am free, that same moment I will inform upon you, and tell them the entrance to your retreat must be somewhere in the rear or the ruins of the old stone church in the graveyard. I will consider myself doing an injustice to the world in ridding it of a band of robbers and mur- derers."</p>
        <p>_ In a single moment, as if pandemonium had sud- denly let loose, each map VMS on his feet With a volley of oaths, and, in a twinkling, a score or more drawn revolvers glistened in the garish lamp- light. "Lower your weapons, comrades," commanded the handsome young chief, grimly. "I will attend to this defiant little beauty." The lovely blue eyes flashed him a look of scorn; the golden, curly head crested itself proudly. "Do not touch me !" she cried, as he grasped her arm firmly. "Out of my way !" he cried, harshly, to the men who would have barred his exit. "I will give her into Hagar's hand for the present. Before we disperse to-night we will settle her future. You will follow me," he said, not unkindly, as he turned to Verlie.</p>
        <p>She had no choice bat to follow, but the first few steps became so painful she dropped at his feet in a deep BWOOO. Raising her quickly in his arms, he bore her swiftly through a long narrow passage, and into an inner apartment, whore an old colored woman sat busily sewing. "Lor', Mame Rupert t" she cried, "what in tho world has yo' got thar ?" He pushed impatiently past her, and laid the slight, girlish figtire in his arms hastily down on a rude couch fashioned out of buffalo robes and blankets' that occupied the further corner. Black Hagar hobbled after him with all the curi- osity of her race.</p>
        <p>"I'll 'ten' dat ar chile, Marse Rupert!" she cried. "Mn she dead or in a faint like f"</p>
        <p>"She has fainted," he replied, briefly. " fetch her too, quick 'nun'. You men don't un'stan' seal 'fairs."</p>
        <p>" lie quick about it, then," he retorted, imps., tiently. as he turned on his heel. An exclamation from Black Ilagar quickly arrested his steps. " Oh, Lor' 7" she cried nut in astonishment, gazing down on the wlsite, upturned face, upon which the fitful rays of the swinging oii-lamp glim- mered fitfully, "this young gal am as lovely as a born angel. There'll baa scene whon—"</p>
        <p>"Never mind making comments; attend to your own affairs," sharply exclaimed the man whom she addressed as " Marse Rupert." "Set about restoring her if you can. She has a badly sprained ankle. The deuce and all would be to pay if I were obliged to c:til in a doctor. I'll come this way in the course of an hour and see how she is."</p>
        <p>When he returned to the cavern again he found Black Hagar still working over her patient. "It am no use, Alarse Rupert," she declared. "The pain o' this liar foot has set her clar delarus. She can't be brung too; you'll have to fetch a doctor, shu'." "A doctor 1" he cried, staggering back and gnashing his teeth in impotent rage. "It isn't to be thought of; no one must see the girl here. Why, that would ruin us, you old fool. Send for a doctor, indeed, to spread the startling discovery of this underground cavern that leads to the har- bour, and that, too, in the very heart of the city Why, woman, you must be mad." She had been vainly trying to restore the lovely young stranger to consciousness, but all her efforts seemed futile. The little white hands grew icy cold under her touch, the stronv cordial she had forced be- ween the girl's white lips failed to draw back the fleet- ing breath, and the•lovely face, framed in its sheen of golden hair, grew marble white under her frightened gaze. "It's life or death, Marse Rupert," declared Black Hagar ; "her life am in yo' hands. Poor thing, it am a pity to let her die, she am so young an' fair."</p>
        <p>An hour later a all, beardad man stood upon the steps of Dr. Keith's residence. There came such a thundering kneel at the front•door, and, directly after, such a peal at the office-bell, as made OA dooka Liwri Lie bed and grind</p>
