      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>LADUS' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADUS' LETTER.</p>
        <p>DEAREST NELLIE, There seems to be a strong feeling for jackets and coats this autumn.; but it is far from probable that they will replace the ever-popular cloaks and capes, which are so much more in kespingswith the present style of dress. Jackets for the 'autumn will I _think be half-length and tight-fitting. Double•breasted jackets are much in fashion with three buttons, but the baequee are not long. They are trimmed in quite amovel fashion with half-inch wide silk braid in treble sets,. headed by narrow bands of Astrachan.</p>
        <p>Capes will also be worn; they are in every shape and style, though invariably small. They will be made of cloth, velvet, velveteen, black plush, and, in lad, of a great variety of material. The trimmings will be as varied as the stuffi used for making. As the season progre-aes the brown and black combination will _undoubtedly obtain vogue, and many of the black gowns will be elaborately trimmed either with brown fur or brown velvet. Blending of colours as -well as combination of materials are indicated, and the success of gowns will largely depend on the skill with which these harmonies are effected. Velvet devid us the honours with satin and mit IS as a favourite trimming for cloth and woollen rlstariala.</p>
        <p>daresay you have noticed what an ageing effect the fashion of parting the hair straight down the middle of the bead has upon NVJMeII who :follow this mode. In PAria jewellery there is nothing very new, though it is lovely, tasteful, and full of .fancy as ever. The arrangements for the hair in the shape of birds' wings, feathers, or --sprays are moto fascinating. -o.se feels that even a hideous wornsa coitfed properly, and with 000 of these ornaments placed amidst 8)(I curls or waves must needs look if not pretty at any rAte die. .tinguiehed. In the art of furbishing her head the Englishwoman is still many miles behind the Parisian.</p>
        <p>'GI the newest coiffures one can be arranged Irons a fair amount of hair, or, if the natural hair is short and scanty, the centre loop O.Ln be made from this, with a very small, light, frizetto to keep St firm, and one large rouleau curl placed top and bottom may he spread out to reach to the aide„ and lightly fastened with tiny hairpins. These curls in a small size cost about 21. each, or very large ones .3a. and 4s. They are easily rolled up, and, if waved and the ends "fizzed" before curl- ing up, will spread out to seine length, and can be pinned on as described. The double skirt still continues in great favour. It consists of two deep flounces, the upper one coming down from the waist and just overlapping the other. You can see the treble skirt, formed of three superposed flmnces over a foundation skirt. If, however, the skirt. is plain, it is trimmed in circles, three or four narrow flounces put on at equal distances, or else bands of embroidery, lace insertion, zephyr, velvet, or moir4 ribbon, passe- menterie or galloon put on in the game way. —lt the material is wide enough, there is only one seam at the back; if not, there is one on each side ail well, and for narrow-width fabrics, we have the umbrella skirt, formal of a nuaLper of gored widths, all tapering up to the waist.</p>
        <p>A delightful hat I saw recently wa4 somewhat of the boat shape, made in black silt beaver, faced with black satin; round the crown ii a scarf of reseda green velvet, tied with long ends at the back, not too long, but just scffiiently long to give it a special character, and at each side of the front nestles a convincing eltuter of purple•tinted dahlias ; truly this is a great success. Another bat, which may be justly written down as a success also, is of golden brown beaver, with folds of petunia velvet drawn through rings of jet; at either side of the brim are clusters of purple-tinted roses, from which emerge two black quills. An otter-hued beaver bat—and beaver, you will notice, is eminently fashionable in millinery—is lined with black felt, and trimmed with a mass of black ostrieh feathers ; four of these standing erect in the front, while two more are arranged at each aide to droop on the hair. A very pretty felt hat can be stylishly trimmed with ribbon bows in black, navy, or brown. It can be worn with the hair fixed up in the style described above.</p>
        <p>A great advance has been made M the style of children's dresses, and one o; the prettiest of the neweat fanhiona i 5 au "Arrow" Trylntle, made of win- ter tweed, piped with dark plush, and finiahed with fancy corda at the top of the eacque, the waist being outlined with a plaiu band in front. T:e collar is charmingly shaped, e itia a fall frill at the bark. A girl of nine years w!) &amp;Id- find this cloak wry suitable. The accquil i;g,ure is clad in a winter stockinette kyrle cloth ei.antle, nominated the " Tarff." The collar is bordered with soft racoon. At the Lack a tenti-htit holds the pleats to the waist. A pcliasa in cteam fancy ffeecy, buttonci down the front with pearl buttons, has cape, sleeves, and hood haniat, bardered sith white fox. The muff is of the wee fur.</p>
        <p>Yours lovingly,</p>
        <p>VEs:us.</p>
        <p>Miss Emily Penrose is amongst those appointed to lecture during the coming winter under the auspices of the London Society for the Extension of University Teaching. _ ___</p>
        <p>At the World's Congress of Religions the Rev. Dr. Augusta Chaplain was among those who spoke at the opening service. The degree of D.D. has recently been conferred upon this lady by an American university. The Empress Eugenie is looking very mdch aged of late, and the look of melancholy sadness seems to have deepened on her face. The villa which she has had built at Cap Martin will be quite ready for ocoupation in November when it is probable that the Empress will go there for a stay of several weeks. At a ball in Melbourne where a number of young ladies were hostesses, the dainties on the supper table were all homemade, having been contributed by the girls themselves. The intrinsiC excellence of the dishes was proved by the justice done to them, and the taste with which they were arranged made them all the more attractive. Will English girls take the hint? The Duke and Duchess of York are agreed in disliking any unnecessary formalities, and during their recent appearances in Edinburgh and York delighted everyone with their affability and simple manners. In both cities they shook hands and chatted gaily with quite a large number of people, and won golden opinions from all. The Reoirss of Edinburgh is very fond of a certain</p>
