      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>Secrtt of a Publican's Chemist.</head>
        <p>Secrtt of a Publican's Chemist.</p>
        <p>Dr. Gordon Stable's once fell in with a " publican's chemist" at Sheerness. In a private room he revealed to the doctor secrets that astmishad ,him. The chemist placed in front of him five little glasses containing spirits ot wine.and taking from a case five little phials,he added a portion of their contents to each and lo ! they were changed into brandy, rum, gin,Scotch and Irish whisky! The brandy in this country, says " Science Siftings," is nearly all bad, and too new, and contains much fusil oil. It is this oil that attacks the digestion and brain, and "gives you a head in the morning." _T.emetnber that a large quantity of raw grain Scotch whisky leaves this country every year for France, and returns as brasdy. Remember, too, that good brandy is cheap at any rea- sonable price, but it should he used as a anedicine more than anything else.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>Blackberriee.</head>
        <p>Blackberriee.</p>
        <p>Many people like apples and brambles mixed. The subjoined is condensed from " The Spinning Wheel " : —Blackberries cooked in any way are one of the greatest treats that can be provided for children, and, like treacle, form useful medicines for their little ailments. The fruit (for jam) spould be picked clean and sin dry weather. To every pound add lb. course brawn sugar, and let them simmer for nearly an hours keeping it stirred well the whole time. Put into pots, and keep in a dry place. The fruit for wines (one of the nest and most agreeable), must be ripe, dry, and sound.. Put 5 quarts with a pint of elderberries picked from the stem, to every two gallons of water in an earthenware pan. Soak all night ; the next day rub the fruit through a wind seive until all the juice is extracted, washing the pulp again with some of the watm Then to every gallon put 41bs of best Demerara sugar. Barrel, but lay bung lightly. Do not use anything to ferment it. In three days add a bottle of brandy to every 4 gallons,then bung it close, but leave Vie peg out at top for a few days. Keep the wine fsr three years. Jelly-mould flavoured with blackberry juice is delicious.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>True Physical Rest.</head>
        <p>True Physical Rest.</p>
        <p>There is an old story of ;.n Indian and a "pale face." who, after ,a long day's journey, lay down in a deserted cabin at nightfall to rest. The Indian, wrapping him- self in his blanket, stretched himself on the fluor of the cabin, with his feet to the fire and was soon asleep.. His companion, meantime,has espied a feather bed in another room, and congratulating himself on his discovery, jumped in and was also soon in a doze. With the first rays of the morning light ths Indian rose refreshed, and ready for the day's task. Be went to arouse his comrade; when 10, he found him dead from the exhaustion of the previous day. Luxurious repose is never true to physical rest. To enjoy that blessing to its fullest extent, free- dom from restraint must be allowed every part of the body. A firm surface is required—one that will tend to keep the body stretched out at full length, that the lungs and heart may feel no sense of restriction by compression of the chest walls, and that the blood may have uninter- rupted COOII6B in every direction. We should never be guilty of supposing that the person whom we saw sitting in a chair, with his chin pressing on his sunken chest, was enjoying true physizal rest, no matter how fast asleep he might appear to be. The tendency of the body to gravitate toward the lowest part of a feather-bed is beyond remedy. Lu this position the whole body isoiton so curled up that no one part is free from constriction. The chest-walls are caved in and the whole body suffers from the conseqnent lack of proper oxygenation of the blood and the restriction which is placed upon its general circulation. The blood moves sluggishly, and as a re.,:ult the condition of " fat and flabby is superinduced. This condition is never likely to follow the constant use of a firm hair mattress, for the blood has na chance to get dropsical from too sluggish a circelati-m. Perfect physical repose, like perfect physical activity, is depen- dent upon a proper equilibrium of the bodily functions during slumber. The story of the Indian and the white man might easily have been founded on fact.—" Youth's Companion."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>WHY THE LEAVES TREMBLED.</head>
        <p>WHY THE LEAVES TREMBLED.</p>
        <p>While Mr. Hudson was in Patagonia he became Much interested in a scout by the name of Sosa, who was famous for. the almost preternatural keenness of his senses. In most other respects he was as Mr. Hudson says, a degenerate being. In particular, he was an inveterate horse-thief—a fact which was largely over- looked by the authorities on account of the exceecliug value of his services in times of Indian warfare. In 1861 Sosa had found it prudent to disappear for a season, and in the company of five or six other gauchos—also offenders against the law, who had fled to the refuge of the desert—he amused himself by hunting ostriches along the Rio Colorado. On the twelfth of March the hunters were camping beside a grove of willows in the sad about nine o'clock that evening, while they were seated about the fire roasting their ostrich meat, Sosa suddenly sprang up and held his hand high above his head for some moments. "There is not a breath of wind," he said, "and yet the leaves of the trees are trembling. What can this portend ?" The other men stared at the trees, but could see no motion, and began to laugh and jeer at Sosa. Presently he sat clown again, remarking that the trembling had ceased; but for the rest of the evening he seemed greatly disturbed in his mind. He remarked repeatedly that such a thing had never happened t:) him before. He could feel a breath of wind befere the leaves felt it, and there had been no wind. He feared it was a sign of some disaiter that was about to overtake the party. The disaster was not for them. On that evening an earthquake destroyed the distant city of Mendoza, crushing twelve thousand people to death in its fall. That the subterranean wave extended east to the Plata and southward into Patagonia was afterward known ; for in the cities of Rosario and Buenos Ayres clocks stopped, and a slight shock was experienced in Carmen on the Rio Negro.</p>
        <p>London letter carriers find a good many empty purses in letter boxes. Pickpockets think it safer to drop them there than on tie pavements. What does the Church and Stage Guild think of this ? asks the " Musical Standard ":—A young lady who is playing a small part in " A Gaiety Girl," recently obtained an engagement to sing in the choir of a London church. She did not mention what her profession was. Directly the authorities of the church dbc)vered that she was an actress, she W3S promptly dismissed.</p>
      </div>
