      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>GREAT LOSS OF LIFE.</head>
        <p>GREAT LOSS OF LIFE.</p>
        <p>---- , 'Hardly a day goes by without one can see in broad 'letters, on the bnil.etins, "Great loss of life." " Appal- ling disaster." What with fire-traps in Lor.don ; reek- or car dessness at sea; cyclones in America; land-slides in ; 'and volcanoes in Italy, no city, no countEy, no continent seems exempt. Then, is, how- ever, in most instances, a summary judicial inquiry and "e.ey effort is made to minimise the chances for a re- Petition as well as alleviate the suffering victims. There is one source of danger which claims hundreds 'lf victims in all lands, and which, if taken by statistics, Causes more deaths than any one of the above disasters, and with regard to which, strange to say, there is a general apathy. That is, the danver resulting from Ignoring the k; gnthbeaseeriousness of a cold settling in the kidneys. fickle,appetite ; nervousne,'s, sleepless- Usslangout-; a slight puffiness of the eyelids; a stitch under the shoulder-blades, and occasional brick-dust deposit in the urine are the forerunners of kidney dis- ;aBe, which, if nut arrested, will prove fatal. Mrs. Warners Hatfield Peverel, near Witham, says I used Safe Cure for !fright's Disease of the Kidneys, tr:.rn which I was a victim. Before commencing with p medicine I could only raise myself up in bed with help. I not only suffered the symptoms above men- vinoned, but from shortness of breath, dropsical swelling, Crelsing general weakness, and ursemic convulsions. on after taking Warner's Safe Cure I was able to get p or an hour or two, for the first time in four months. ti.fsgress was uninterrupted, and my perseverancA. with is remedy resulted in effecting a complete cure.'</p>
