      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>-- ~4 sPecial meeting of the Warrington Town Coun- Cu, was held on Tuesday evening,for the purpose of pleting the business of the municipal year. i•nere were present the Mayor, Aldermen J. Ham- rni J. Platt, G. Blackhurst, R. W. Francomb, T. H. _utton, J. C. Holmes, J. E. Wright ; Councillors R. Fairclough, G. Flumpton, Dr. Cannel!, L. Green- J. Smethurst, J. Hill R. Heaton, Shaw Green, if Garner, J. Ashton,S. J. Beaman, T. Flood, A. Br7lvne, W. J. Shaw, T.llutchinson, J. Muir, and W. },°!tun ; Messrs. J. Lyon Whittle (town clerk), airhurst (borough treasurer),and Long.lin (borough treasurer).</p>
        <p>RESIGNATION OF ALDEREAN CUANDLEY. Bef°ra the commencement of the business of the 'Celan Aue, the Mayor said that he regretted to report that to an Chandley had resigned the office of alderman IrD'ue borough. Ile had sent the following letter : Dear Mr, Mayor.—l re beg to place in your hands my t: a isign on of the office of alderman for one of the stens of the borough of Warrington. It only re- Zins for me to express my deep thanks to those me 8 of the Council at whose hands I have received such 7lgnal kindness,and to all those officials who have given .9° such courteous assistance." Alderman Chandley en- rsed the usual shilling and the Mayor announced that "e vacancy would be shilling, on the 9th November.</p>
        <p>INSURING OF CORPORATION PROPERTY. ),„1-"lintillOr GREENING asked if all th; property had ;711 tn insured against fire.—Alderman FRANCO3II3 replied EALTR at it bad. COMMITTEE.—THE SUPERVISION OF LODGING-</p>
        <p>HOUSES. vieTrile adoption of the minutes of this committee,which e reported in tile " Examiner" last week, were -;'mled by Alderman SUTTON, seconded by Councillor Alderman FRANCOMB unwed as an amendment, that ion! resolution ..That it be an instruction to the Inspec- iocUf lodging-houses to submit a report of his visits to the6\mg-houses to the Medical Officer of Health,and that tru in_.,24edical Officer of Health be empowered to give such sctions as he may consider necessary In the cases rePorted to him •, and that he report to this committee w.cases which in his judgment ought to be proceeded "11, before the magistrates, be omitted, as it gave" the 61%ncal Officer power to prosecute. (" No, no. ') He oicin that for many years he had been a member of the ti Sanitary Committee. The inspectors then, visited le anYth'lodging-houses regularly, and when they found co mg out of order, it was reported to the sumMittee. The lodging-house keeper was then cotirnre„mled before the committee, and if it was stuered a trifling complaint, the committee re- ;;43 Inanded him, and the matter ended. But if it was a talkl°llB c°mplaint, the committee ordered the case to be Pii befcre the magistrates. He did not think it right inedical Officer should have this power,as it gave too -"'eh power to him, oput him in an unpleasant as Positio I and a . n• t w a excellent thing that the officer should ron able to put kimselt behind the committee, for if any °i was prosecuted before the maE,dstrates, and the d leer was spoken to about it, he could say, "I have to art• The case was reported to my Committee, and ter due consideration, they ordered it to be taken be- re the mag,istiates." It was wrong to put an official gtioto such a position as the proposed resolution would do. 1, re than that, lie didn't think it was fair to the C„0, uneillor CANNELL seconded the amendment. 'l6dern3an Surforr said that the Health Committee had COMP to this resolution without serious considera-, 0.11,n• A good deal of the time of the Committee was -en taken ul3 in investigating matters which they con- could Sideredvery properly be investigated by the medi- haealibtoeffitrhcrmeidf tcstake to put it as Alderman Francomb did, o i eaiofficer 1 before the meeting. It was alto- S medical lal of health would be empowered te order the cases to be taken before the magistrates, be- anse it was not so. The medical officer would inquire the cases and should he consider them sufficiently vvroic'illst he ;mid report them to the Committee who d then consider them. There were many cases re- 11).°rted which the Committee considered ought not to be t%Prnor.ted and their time ought not to be taken up with 1-790 n a division being taken, there were for the ttriendment : Aldermen Francomb and Platt, Councillors Pairclough, Heaton, Bolton, Garner, Dr. Cannell, jan-1 and Smethurst, 9 ; against, Alderman Harrison, Sutton and Blackhurst, Councillors Shaw Green, e the eing, Beaman, Plumpton, Mod, and Ashton, 10 ;. tha alnendment was therefore lost, and the minutes of tiveominittee were then carried unanimously. INO AND SEWERAGE COMMITTEE.-.-THE UNEMPLOYED This r QUESTION. WBl-nrnMittee's minutes were moved by Aldermani i.,111'. He said that owing to the extreme poverty n wo —wn, the Committee had done what it could to find go 11, tor the men who were out of work. There was a anumber of men employed in paving the streets,and C visit round the town that he had made that day, Wag%U.ld saY that the men were fairly earning their u..,...Councillor HEATON seconded the adoption of the nnutes. tistttir:Ceneistlusaietlreilet orPLumrrox called attention to the state of • and GoMlden-street. The houses in that Were very damp, and the persons who lived in the off had complained to him. The plaster was falling CII thle Walls to about a yard above the ground. Some latosveede.Property owners were anxious to have the streets d le thought if the chairman could see his way sor:ernmence this work, it would find employment to c of the men out of work in tho Whitecross Ward. oc: b -street at the far end also required something done t it. quirednenlor SuAw GREEN Said that Buckley-street re- Ald repaving as it was in a very bad condition. ._errnan WRIGHT said this was the first intimation he a Ita(yad of Monk-st reet and Goulden-street being in well 411_9, U del; enctle v i i °en • He would however promise that they the Committees attention, as also Cart- -street and and Bucklev-street. "le minutes were passed unanimously. The SCHOOL ATTENDANCE. repor, m. mutes of this Committee which contained the tase or the inspector, from which it appeared that 30 iedia had been taken before the magistrates, and fines Dioveled, in 29 cases, and one boy sent to the Clio, were to th" bY Councill'or SHAW GREEN, who called attention aubadenia:unsuiya.l report of the Committee just published, s.ecended by Councillor PLUMPTON, and carried The • MUSEUM COMMITTEE. follow.llllßUtes of this Committee which reported the vvTg gifts :—Drawings by Thomas Robson, from Cr osi ham Robson ; ten Kaffir weapons, from Mrs. John peitrl‘ed ; Parliamentary papers (18), from Mr. IL Pler- -IPart 3 ; reproductions of drawings by old masters, p0i.... !catalogue of birds, vol. I. ; catalogue of snakes, bus,' catalogue of madreporaria, vol. 1., from ~he feeea'ses of the British Museum ; portrait of Huskisson, and Mr. IV. Howe • were moved by Councillor Greening alc, seconded by Coil' 'minor Plumpton and carried unani- usiy. This Co CEMETERY COMMITTEE. raAt ommittee reported that the interments for the born our weeks had been : Adults,29 ; minors, 74; still- premature, 7. Corresponding period last year The nits; 35 ; minors, 62 ; stillborn and premature, 10. corresrecelPts tn. the past four weeks were £BB 125., Tb Ponding period last year, £BO 7s. seee s‘irninutes were moved by Alderman Harrison and bY Councillor Ashton and carried. Tb TOWN MALL, PARK AND BATHS COMMITTEE. repo -e minutes of this Committee which contained the tt r'f the batlikeeper, from which it appeared that i 23'ilneine from the baths for the past four weeks was kslittecu.o;, and for the corresponding period last year rwriereemoved by Councillor Bolton, seconded by at thAalderman FRANCOMB asked how often the water was a „uged in the Laths. He asked that question because •s•ircitielnan told him that it was only changed once a /tad_ If this was not correct, hi n would like it to .be Public what was the actual truth. `l'e°uncillor BOLTON said that the baths were changed le itic.7aasfound necessary. They were changed at week.s minutes were passed.</p>
        <p>The trc MARKETS COMMITTEE. the • of this Committee whith repotted that Lacil,ncomo from all sources during the past four weeks R.33D°eien £344 163. 6d., corresponding period last year 'B' 7d, increase £l2 14s. 11d., were passed on the</p>
        <p>motion of the MAYOR seconded by Alderman BLACK- HURST. GAS COMMITTEE. This Committee's minutes which were published in last week's Examiner vp•ro passed on the motion of Cauricillor R. T. FameLotain, seconded by Councillor HEATON, with the excoepticai of the paragraph propesing that automatic gas tn ters he supplied to all consumers applying for them. These had it yet been tested. RIVERS COMMITTEE. Councillor FAIRCLOUGH iii moving, the confirmation of the minutes of this committee, which were published in sur last issue, said that everything was going on satis- factorily with regard tr. the river. Councillor BOLTON 13ECOOLled the motion which was carried. FIRE ENGINE COMMITTEE. The minutes contained no item of public interest. Councillor HUTCHINSON, in moving their adoption, said the new steam fire engine was expected at the end el next month. The committee had applied time after time for it, but it appeared that the makers had been exceptionally busy. Councillor Dr. CANSTELL seconded the motion, which was carried. WATER COMMITTEE.—WATER FOR ORFORD. At a meeting held on the 9th October, it was resolved that mains be laid and a simply of water be furnished for Orford, the Warrinet in Rural Sanitary Authority having agreed to guarantee the payment of £35 annually. On the motion of Alderman FnaNcosin, seconded Alderman PLATT, the minutes were passed without comment. GENERAL PURPOSES COMMITTEE.—THE SHIP CANAL. At a meeting held on the 23rd October, the Town Clerk submitted the conditions proposed to be submitted 1 to the Manchester Ship Caral Company, and upon which the Corporation would agree to an extension of time 1 for the completion of the Wa rington works, which were 1 approved, when it was resolved that the subject of the works to be constructed at Warrington under the Man- chester Ship Canal Acts be referred to the Parliamentary Committee, with instructions to arrange with the com- pany the terms upon which such works shall be carried out. These minutes were also agreed to without discussion, on the motion of the MAYOR, seconded by the DEPUTY, MAYOR. , VOTE OF THANES TO THE MAYOR.—COMELIMENTS TO MRS. GREENALL. ' Alderman Hanntsois, according to the printed agenda, should have moved :—" That the best thanks of this Council be presented to Edward Greenall, Esq., alder- man of this borough, for the satisfactory manner in which he has discharged the responsible duties devolv- ing upon him as Mayor and chief magistrate of this , borough during this his second year of office, and for the , unvarying courtesy and impartiality displayed by him when presiding over the deliberations of the Council and in his intercourse with its members." He wished to sug- gest that his old friend, Alderman Holmes, should pro- pose this resolution. (Hear' hear.) He had done so for many years past because of his seniority as a member of the Council. Alderman HotAxs said that he had proposed it so many times that he had nothing fresh to say about it, but Alderman Harrison might have done so. However, after this preamble, he had great pleasure in proposing the resolution. During the whole course of his experi- ence he did not think he ever knew a Mayor who had filled the office better. Alderman Greenall had also been ably supported by Mrs. Greenall. No Mayor had been better supported by his wife, and he thought they must include the name of the Mayoress in the resolution. (Hear, hear.) He had pleasure in moving a vote of thanks to the Mayor. (Applause.) Alderman HARRISON seconded tha motion and said he could not till them how pleased he was that Alder- man Holmes had been goed enough to move the vote of thanks, although his pleasure had been enhanced by listening to the graceful remarks which the Alderman had made. There were three kinds of Mayors. There was what was called the Benedict Mayor,to which Alder- man Greenall belonged. There was also the wifeless Mayor, who might perhaps, be entitled to a great deal of sympathy, for it was by the inexorable decrees of nature that he had been deprived of his sheet anchor. The next class was the bachelor Mayor, he had little or no sympathy for the bachelor Mayor—(laughter)—because he stead not be a bachelor, but if he preferred single- blessedness and suffered any disadvantage when he was elevated to a municipal dignity, they could not sym- pathise very much with him. He regarded the bachelor mayor as a sort of /usus natures—a sort of one-legged monstroSitf. (Laughter) He would not like to be a one-legged monstrosity. (Renewed laughter.) The Mayor, however was to be congratulated upon belong- ing to the first class he had spoken of, and he had been nobly seconded during his year of office by his good wife _(applause)who had assisted by her graceful demean- our in everything that appertained to the lighter and more elegant duties belonging to the office. Great credit was due to her, and the Mayor was to be greatly con- gratulated, for she belonged to that bright galaxy of rare and radiant married ladies who, as the poet says. Give to earth its charm. And to life its zest. (Applause.) He begged to add to the printed resolution the words, " Not forgetting the able assistance rendered by Mrs. Greenall, the Mayeress,in various ways, as well as for having played the part of hostess at the Town Hall with such rare grace and dignity as to leave noth- ing to be desired." (Applause.) Councillor SHAW GREEN supported, and said that per- sonally he felt that the conduct of the Mayor had been exceptirnally praiseworthy. He had performed his duties under very trying circumstances. His conduct during the epidemic of smallpox, and the trouble and affliction they were passing through now, showed that he had a great and kind heart,and that he was endowed with a great deal of sympathy for the suffering and un- fortunate people. Unless he had had such an excellent wife,it would have been impossible for him to have gone through such trying ordeals so well ; indeed, their con- duct showed that they had two excellent persons in the 1 Mayor and Mayoress. (Applause.) ,i Alderman BRANCOMB endorsed everything that had been said. He believed the Mayor had done all in his power to perform his duties well and satisfactorily, and as far as the Mayoress was concerned, she had done her , best to help the Mayor, and to make herself as useful as possible to the people of the town. (Hear, hear.) Alderman FILANCOTIII put the resolution to the meet- ing, and it was carried with acclamation. The MAYOR, in responding, said he had no set speech that night,but he thanked them very much for the kind way in which they had proposed and carried the resolu- tion, and also for introducing his wife's name into it. As Alderman Harrison had said, she was a great help to him, ar,d he hoped she would be long spared to him. ' (Applause.) It would be their endeavour as long as they were in the position they now occupied, to do their duty as far as they could. (Applause.) The Council then rose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>MESSRS. CR,OSFIELD AND SONS' " CONSTANCE " IN COLLISION.</head>
        <p>MESSRS. CR,OSFIELD AND SONS' " CON- STANCE " IN COLLISION.</p>
        <p>-- At the Liverpool County Court on Saturday, be- fore his Honour Judge Collier and Captains E. R. Peel and Andrew Pearson, nautical assessors, the action brought by Messrs. Mawson, of Barrow-in- Furness, owners of the steamship Galgorm Castle, against Messrs. Crosfield and Sons, owners of the steambarge Constance, for damages stated at £3OO, sustained bye collision which occurred in the Alex- andra Dock, on the 18th July last,was resumed and concluded. Mr. Maurice Hill (instructed by Messrs. llill, Dickinson, and Hill) appeared on behalf of the plaintiffs, and Mr. W. F. Taylor (instructed by Messrs. Wilson and Cowie) represented the defendants. It ap neared that on the day in question the Galgorm Castle was coming through the Langton Dock en her way to the Alexandra Dock 'discharging berth. Owing to the dock being blocked she lav ;topped alongside the tender Magnetic, of the White Star Line, when the Constance I struck her. The allegations was that the force of the blow caused the Galgorrn Castle to hit the Magnetic, the ' propeller of which cut a hole in her under the water line. The vessel was found to be sinking, and was at once hauled off to the small river craft branch with a six to eight feet higher sill in the Hornby Dock, where 8114 settled down. In hastening to enter this shallow look,the footbridge which was in process of being opened was carried away by the Galgorm Castle. The evidence throughout the trial, which lasted two days, was of a very con dieting character. The plaintiffs urged, and were supported in their contention by several Dock Board officials, the Galgorm Castle could not have safely con- tinued her course in view of the position of other vessels in the deck, and it was necessary for her to reverse and stop alongside the Magnetic. on the other hand the defendants pleaded that this stoppage was unnecessarv, and the fact of the Galgorm Castle reversing helped t cause the damage. It was also sought to prove that the blow given by the Constance could not have been of sufficient force to cause the Galgorm Castle to be driven on to the Magnetic 's piopeller. as there was no mark on her side at the place of contract. Against this the plain- tiffs proved that a cork fender was burst into pieces,and the protection of this fender prevented any serious mark being made on the side of the Galgorm Castle. Counsel on both sides, at the conclusion of the evidence, argued the case at considerable length, the result being that his Honour, after consulting with the nautical assessors, found for the plaintiffs the amount of damages, t -Tether with the consequential damages in carrying away the footbridge being referred for assessment to the registrar and one of the assessors. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
