      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>EXAMINER; SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 4, 18.93.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER; SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 4, 18.93.</p>
        <p>A MONMOUTEISHIRE MIRACLE. WONDERFUL OCCURRENCE AT TIN TERN.</p>
        <p>TRUTH STRANGER THAN FICTION</p>
        <p>From the "Chepstow Advertiser," Sept. 23, 1393. When we received information of a remarkable cure affected by a certain widely advertised specific, we determined to investigate for.ourselves tho remarkable statements made,, and to expose them without fear or favour if (as we were quite prepared to find, from their extraordinary character) they were unfounded. On Wednesday, September 6th, therefore, a " Chepstow Advertiser " reporter journeyed to Tintern—it must be confessed in a sceptical frame of mind, having always been averse to what we deemed " quackery."</p>
        <p>Inquiry was first made of independent persons, and showed that our "subject" belonged to a respectable farnily,and that the case was a bona fide one of absolute paralysis in the right side, and further that from some cause or other the patient had recovered not only the power of speech, which had forsaken her, but also the use of her limbs. We proceeded to Cliff Cottage, Tintern, the residence of Mrs. Mary Phillips,—a neat little whitewashed and flower-surrounded habitation, not far from the famous Abbey. Cliff Cottage is situated in a most healthy position, some two or three hundred feet above the highway, on an almost perpendicular slope, and has a glorious outlook. We were invited inside, and there, in a cleanly and comfortable room, sat Mrs. Phillips, busy with her needle, her mother (Mrs. Williams), and, near the door., her daughter Elizabeth, a young woman aged 24 years, looking in good health, though somewhat delicate. We explained the reason of our visit, and inquired if Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People had, as report stated, been of any benefit to her family. Both mother and daughter appeared to have implicit faith in their efficacy, and were as one in ascribing to that medicine the wonderful result of the younger woman's recovery from an attack of paralysis which had rendered her helpless and speechless, Mrs. Williams also concurring in the instance of her grand-daughter the pills had been of the greatest value in effecting a recovery. The history of the case, according to the threefold testimony of Mrs. Williams, Mrs. Phillips, and the young woman herself, is as follows :-- On the Ist of ,Tanuary last, Mrs. Phillips as she got up in the morning, gave her daughter Elizabeth a call, and proceeded downstairs to light the fire. Presently she heard a sound as though of loud laughter, whereupon she went to the bottom of the stairs, and inquired what was the matter. Mrs. Williams (an old lady whose hair is whitened by the snows of eighty winters) thought Eliza- beth and her little brother were playing, and giving vent to their amusement in laughter,and accordingly answered hr daughter to that effect. Laughter undoubtedly was heard, but subsequent events showed that it was not the joyous and hearty peal of exuberant mirth—it was the hysterical laugh of a stricken girl ; a girl seized sudeenly and in a moment with one of the greatest infirmities which can befall the human race—" Taken in a laugh- ing fit," as her mother described it. Shortly after the noise was heard, Mrs. Williams had occasion to enter her grand-daughter's bedroom, and there, to her intense alarm-and horror, she beheld Elizabeth lying prone on the floor, upon her right side, " stiff, cold, and speech- less," having been stricken with paralysis, which re- sulted in the loss of all power in her right arm and leg, and, for some three weeks, her voice was gone also. She was attended by a local doctor (who bears in the sur- rounding district the reputation, Of being an extremely clever physician), and also consulted a medical man at Aberdare. She was given up by those gentlemen, who informed that medicine was of no use to her, and time alone could effect any change in her unhappy condition. Mrs. Phillips saw an advertisement in one of the news- papers relating to the marvellous cures through the in- strumentality of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. Accordingly, with a mother's natural solicitude for her daughter's sad condition, and endowed with the" hope that springs eternal in the human breast," she procured some for her daughter. After taking four boxes Eliza- beth felt much better, and sh,e persevered in taking the pills, with the eminently satisfactory result that she has so far recovered as to be able to work with her sewing machine, and her health continues to improve. Truly a living testimony to the efficacy of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People are recognised as one ef the greatest modern medicines, a perfect blood builder and nerve restorer, curing) such a disease as Rheumatism, Neuralgia, Partial Paralysis, Lecomotor Ataxy, St. Vitus' Dance, Nervous Headache, Nervous Prostration and the tired feeling resulting thereform, decoases depending upon Humours in the Blood, such as Sctofula, Chronic Erysipelas, Sec. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills restore pale and sallow complexions to the glow of health, and are a specific for all the troubles peculiar to the female sex, while in the case of men they effect a radical cure in all cases arising from mental worry,over- work, or excesses of whatever nature. These Pills are manufactured by the Dr. Williams' Medicine Company of 46,1101b0rn Viaduct, London,Eng• ' land (and of Brockville, Ontario, Canada, and Schenec- tady, New York), and are sold only in boxes bearing the firm's trade mark and wrapper at 2s. 9d. a box, or six boxes for 13s. 91 A Descriptive Pamphlet sent free by post on application. Bear in mind that Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People are never sold in bulk, or by the dozen or hundred, for any dealer who offers sub- stitutes in this form is trying to defraud you and should be avoided. Ask ycur dealer • for Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, and refuse all imitations and sub- stitutes. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills may be had of all chemists, or direct by post from Dr. Williams' Medicine Company from the above address. The price at which these pills are sold makes a course of treatment comparatively inex- pensive as compared with other remedies or medical treatment. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT. THURSDAY.</head>
        <p>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT. THURSDAY.</p>
        <p>The House of Commons reassembled On Thursday. Mr. BUXTON, anSwering a question put to Mr. Paul, said the views which Sir F. CARRINGTON had expressed in an interview with a news agency's representative as to the necessity for the extermination of the Matabeles were his own. He had been sent out in a purely military capacity, and would have nothing to do with the politics of the question. Mr. H. FOWLER moved the second reading of the Local Government (England and Wales) Bill. Dealing with objections which had been made to the Bill, he said the Government wished to retain to every parish the right of having permanently a parish meeting, no matter what its population was. lie should be disposed to say that the County Council should have power to give a Parish Council to any parish, no matter what its popu- lation was and that they should have power to group parishes, which exceeded the limit of population, with the consent of the parishes. The Governtrient would not stand by the limit of 300, but would be willing to listen to any amendment, and would be guided in the main by the judgment oh the House. He denied the allegiation that ilia Bill was one for the spoliation of the Church of England, and said the Government would be prepared to introduce amendments in it which would set at rest the doubts of many friends of the Church. They could not abandon the provisions of the Bill relat- ing, to district Councili, holding that they were as im- portant as those relating to Parish Councils. Mr. LONG in the course of his criticism of the measure suggested that that part of the Bill relating to the Poor Law should be dropped and introduced next session. Sir C. Dir.xn, Sir F• POWELL, Mr.F. S. STEVENSON, Mr. E. STANHOPE, a-nd others took part in the debate, which was adjourned at midnight.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>GAMEKEEPER KILLS A POACHER.</head>
        <p>A GAMEKEEPER KILLS A POACHER.</p>
        <p>A coroner's inquiry into the circumstances attending the death of a miner named Battey, who died from in- juries to the head said to have been inflicted by a game- keeper named Fcarns, was held at Ward Green, near Barnsley, on Thursday. Batty and his brother were out walking and met Fearns and two other gamekeTers. The latter appear:, to have found a gun close to where the brothers were, and after some words it is alleged that Fearns struck Battey over the head with the barrel of the gun, fracturing his skull so badly that he suc- cumbed to the injury on Tuesday last. In a conscious interval he stated that Fearns deliberately struck him without provocation. A verdict of manslaughter was r turned against Fearns.</p>
        <p>RUPTURE CURED.—S. J. SHERMAN, Hernia Specialist, 64, Chancerv-lane, London, sends his book on rupture post ree. Branch Chambers, 25, King-. , Manchebter.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>WIDNES TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>WIDNES TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The last monthly meeting for the year was held at the Town Hall on Monday afternoon, under the presidency of Alderman F. IL Gossage, J.P., Mayor. There were present Aldermen Tilley, Tirnmis, Sadler, Wareing and Williamson, Councillors Barlow, Shuttleworth,.Christie, Bullough, F. Neil, G. I. Neil, Davies, Owens, Quinn, Singleton, O'Donnell, Shores, Gough, Carney, W. W. Gossage, Smith and Ifodg- kinson, with Oppenheim (town clerk), Craik (assist- ant clerk); Hoyle (assistant), Carr (engineer), Sin- clair (surveyor), O'Keeffe (medical officer), Lowe (inspector).</p>
        <p>POOTIPATIC OBSTRUCTION. The highway Committee ieportcd with regard to Bradley footpath—that the Borough Surveyor having produced plan of footpath showing, an obstruction there- to, and reported that the footpath in question was an ancient footpath, it was resolved that the Town Clerk institute legal proceedings against the person or persons causing the obstruction.—Alderman Sadler moved the confirmation of the minutes. In reply to Councillor Shores the Town Clerk said the Committee had considered and had partially adopted the byelaws of the Public Health Amendment Acts. They were await- ing a conference with the builders in the town before coming to a final decision.—Passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>THL CHARGES AGAINST THE HOSPITAL.</head>
        <p>THL CHARGES AGAINST THE HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>The Health Committee submitted the following reso- lution.—Beselved unanimously that this Committee hav- ing exhaustively inquired into and fully considered the statements laid before them by various persons, lately patients in the Infectious Diseases Hospital, the medical gentlemen in attendance upon such persons, the matron, and other persons, and having heard Mr. Alderman Williamson and Mr. Councillor Quinn, the mover and seconder of the amendment referring the minute of this Committee of the 15th September ult., back to this Com- mittee, confirm their previous resolution and find that the charges made are baseless and without foundation. —Councillor G. I. Neil in moving the confirmation of the minutes said he had no doubt the Council would be pleased the Committee had come to the same conclusion as before that the charges were groundless, and he main- tained that the Committee ought not to have been put to this enormous expense.—Councillor Smith seconded the motion. He considered that the inquiry had placed Alderman Williamson in a very questionable position in the minds of the Council when theyremembered the way in which he brought forward these base charges which were calculated to bring disgrace upon an excellent in- stitution, the medical attendants, the Health Committee and an excellent matron and staff, and that all the evi- dence he could bring proved a miserable failure. It looked very much like a trumped up affair from begin- ning to end. He thought Alderman Williamson should be called upon to withdraw the insinuations he had made, especially with respect to the "bogus" inquiry and calling the Health Committee "cut throat."—Alder- man Williamson said he should advise Mr. Smith next time he wrote a speech to send it to the newspaper. He went on to say that he mentioned the matter to the Chairman of the Committee two months before he brought it before the Council, and as he (Mr. Neil) did not take any action he thought it his duty to bring the subject before the Council. He considered that the inquiry right through completely vindicated his action. (A laugh.) Any gentleman who would read carefully through the reports without prejudice would be under-the impre§- sion that he had done nothing but what he would have done had the same things come under his notice. As to the cost of the inquiry the whole Council were as much to blame as himself.—(Mr. Christie : Speak for yourself, please.)—He was speaking for himself. The Council at the last meeting took the responsibility of the inquiry, and he thought the ratepayers would judge the money well spent, if it had been the means of saving lives or of I mein; more care to be taken of the patients who were sent to the hospital in the future. Had the inquiry been, a public one,he believed the opinion of the public would completely exonerate him for the action he had take]. Any future case coining, under his notice, instead of brining it before the Health Committee, he should send the evidence to the Local Government Board, and he would then get an inquiry that would be unbiassed.— Councillor F. Neil said what took place at the second inquiry simply corroborated the evidence at the first in- quiry, consequently,the expense was upon the shoulders of Alderman Williamson. lie characterised Alderman Williamson's statements with regard to the result of the inquiry as very unfair indeed.—Councillor Quinn did not think Councillor Smith was justified in bringing the charge he had against Alderman Williamson.—Council- lor G. I. Neil said he had no intention to again discuss the hospital inquiry.—Mr. Quinn here rose to ask a fur- ther question, when the Chairman peremptorily said : Sit down,please ; I will not have two gentlemen on their feet at the same time.—Councillor Quinn said the con- densation of the report by the committee was mislead- ing.—The minutes were passed, Alderman Williamson and Councillor O'Donnell voting against the resolution.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>HEALTH RETURNS.</head>
        <p>HEALTH RETURNS.</p>
        <p>The Medical Officer reported that during the past month there were 73 births and 29 deaths being at the respective rates of 44.40 and 17 64 per 1,000. There were 9 deaths amongst children under one year, and 7 amongst children between 1 and 5 years. Zymotic diseases caused 6 deaths. Notifications of infectious diseases as follow were received :—Enteric fever, 23 ; erysipelas, 5 ; membranous croup, 2; scarlet fever, 1 ; total 31. The death rate is lower this month than it has been since February, and there is a great decrease in the number of cases of typhoid fever. Throe dogs suffering from rabies were destroyed. No further cases have been reported. There was one case of swine fever,the pig was destroyed.</p>
        <p>THE OAS AND WATER COMMITTEE AND THE COAL STRIKE. The Engineer reported that Thursday last completed the thirteenth week since the supplies of gas, coal and steam fuel under contract were stopped. At present there were nine days' supply in stock at the gas works, and four days' supply in stock at Netherley. The illum- inating power of the gas during the past week has been abnormally • low owing to the inferior class of coal we have been obliged to use. The last cargo of Scotch coal, besides producing gas of a very poor quality, also yielded a very low make of gas per ton of coal carboriiz-d, and, in addition to this, the coke is of inferior quality. In consequence of the scarcity of fuel and the extraordinary densand for coke, I have applied an apparatus to one of the boilers at the gas works for burning tar in place of coke. It is very effective in keeping up the steam, in fact, more so than the coke, as one boiler with the tar fuel now does the work of two under ordinary tiring. Taking this price of tar at 133. per ton, and coke at 15s. per ton the saving at present being effected is 255. per day. It the strike is ta.) continue for any length of time this system might with advantage be introduced at Netherley, as it would pay to cart the tar out rather than pay the present prices for slack.—Relief Work for the Unemployed :—The number who have so far been employed for three days each, under this system is 175. —The Committce resolved to purchase 100 tons of coal for Netherley.—Alderman Timinis moved the adoption of the minutes and explained that the Committee had not introduced the tar fuel at Netherley because they had anticipated the early conclusion of the strike and it would riot be wise to go to the expense.—The minutes were passed. WIDNES AND RUNCORN FERRY.. .. .</p>
        <p>-- . The General Purposes Committee reported that this ferry has not been in working operation by the London and North-Westera Railway Company for the last six months. The Manchester Ship Canal Company are pro- viding a ferry landing on the Runcorn side, with a floating pontoon as shown on the plan submitted to the Local Board on the 18th December. 1839. Their Engineer, Mr. Abernethy, in an interview on the 17th inst., stated that this pontoon bridge and ferry slip are ready for fixing, so that the Widnes Ferry cln be opened at an early date if required. Resolved—That this matter be referred to the Town Clerk—Alderman Wareing moved the adoption of the minutes. THE POLICE FORCE. .</p>
        <p>..... Councillor O'Donnell asked whether the regulation of the police ot the town was under the jurisdiction of the General Purposes Osmmittee, and whether the Chairman of the Committee had a scale of the various constables duties throughout the town, and whether in the event of a riot or a fire the Chairman could gather and concen- trate those constables. Also whether the Committee had any control whatever over the police. Alderman Wareing replied that the arrangement of the polling booths was in the hands of the Mayor. His answer to Councillor O'Donnell was no.—The minutes were passed, and Councillor O'Donnell gave notice that he wourd ask for a return of the information , lie required at the neat meeting.</p>
        <p>FINANCE CCDI;iIITTKE. The Accountant reported to this Committee that the following sums have been borrowed during the month, viz. :—£2oo at 3 per cent., and £1,500 at 31 per cent. I also submit a lett( r from J. W. Bennett, offering r..n be- half of the Methodist Sick and Burial Socirty, Manches- ter, the sum of £5OO at 31 per cont. Instructions should now be given to advertise for loans to meet the expen- diture on private improvements and other works, which have been ssnctioned by the Local Goverment Board. The borough fund account is still largely overdrawn at the bank, and I would suggest On t the overseer be re- quested to make another payment at an early date ;as yet the Corporation have only received £5,500, and of Ithis sum £5,000 has been piid to the School Board. The Committee resolved that the Town Clerk advertise for loans, and that the Town Clerk apply to the overseers for payment of the balance of the amount due upon the Corporation precept. The following accounts were passed for payment :—llighway. £1694 3s. 7d. ; £227 125. Id. ; Gas Works, £2,667 2s. 31. ; Water Works, £lB5 12s. 81. ; General Purposes, £B7 6s. 3. ; Finance, £1,645 15s. 6d. ; Library, £l5 2s. 61.; Techni- cal Instruction, £26Bs. 101. ; total, £6,549 3s. 83. The minutes were approved on the motion of Councillor Owens.</p>
        <p>THANKS TO HIS WORSHIP. Alderman Sailer moved that the best thanks of the meeting be given to his Worship the Mayor, for the able and imrartial manner in which he had presided over the deliberations of the Council during the past year. (Hear, hear.)—Councillßr F. Neil seconded the motion.—The motion was supported by Alderman Williamson. The Mayor thanked the members for their thanks and for the coOdence reposed in him as the first Mayor of</p>
        <p>the Borough. He gladly and thankfully took office for another year. The members then adjourned intl the Mayor's Parlour, and drunk to the health of the Mayor, to the retiring members and to prosperity to the borough.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>COAL TRADE CRISIS:</head>
        <p>THE COAL TRADE CRISIS:</p>
        <p>COLLIERS BEFORE THE . MAGISTRATES AT NEWTON-LE-WILLOWS.</p>
        <p>THE HAYDOCIL,:" RIOTS."</p>
        <p>Saturday was a very busy day at iNewton-le-Willows sessions house, owing to the large number of colliers who were summoned for various offences committed in the district under the jurisdiction of that court. Need- less to say, during the morning the streets near the Town Hall were crowded with colliers anxious to get the first news of the decision arrived at, and also be- cause the men implicated in the Haydock riots were also to undergo their adjourned trial. ' The bench was composed of Captain Withington (chairman), Colonel J. L. Wood, and Mr. Wm. Mitchell.</p>
        <p>DVIACE TO FENCES. _ The first batch of olliers who were tried were the following: Edward Clarke, John Lowe, John Valen- tine, James Tomlinson, William Cottam, Peter Jackson, Wm. Roughley, Henry Sutton, Henry ;Hart, and Jesse Kay, who were each summoned on the charge with hav- ing on Oct. 18th, broken into Haydock Park and .doing damage to the fences to the value of ss.—Mr. R. Stuart, who prosecuted in the whole of the cases,said that Thos. Martland, the keeper of Haydock Park, was on duty on the 18th Oct., when he saw the defendants in the park. He only knew one of them, and he kept them " in a parley "until a policeman was sent for. P.C. Connor, who knew the defendants came to the park and stood at the gate as they went through and took the whole of their na:nes. Mr. Stuart said that Mr. Berry, the pro- prietor of Haydock Park, did not wish to press the case unduly against the defendants. Defendants were each fined in the costs, and the damage of 53. was divided between them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>TIIEFT OF CO tL.</head>
        <p>TIIEFT OF CO tL.</p>
        <p>James Mills and Alfred Mills were each charged with stealing a quantity of coal, of the value of 6J., the pro- perty of Messrs. S. W. Pilling and Co., contractors„Hay- dock, on the 14th September. They were bound over to appear when called upon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>ALLEGED INTIMIDATION.</head>
        <p>ALLEGED INTIMIDATION.</p>
        <p>Samuel Wilding was charged with intimidating a fire- man of Messrs, Richard Evans and Co., named Thomas Davies, on the Sth Oct.—Mr. Stuart said that this was one of the unfortunate occurrences arising out of the pre- sent coal dispote. The charge against Wilding was known as "walking round." The colliers seemed to have got a notion into their mind that if a man in the employment of a firm was endeavouring to get a living, and that living did not interfere with the men's dispute at all, they would wherever they found him " walk him round." The "walking round" was a revival of the old pillory. They were going back in civilisation instead of going for- ward, because very many years ago, the man was pil- loried by the powers that were, but the colliers were the powers that be at the present time. The man Davies was an underlooker for Messrs. Richard Evans and Co.,in the (neen's pit. On the day in question, a mob pursued three firemen who took refuge in Davies' house. Wild- ing was one of the most prominent Gf the mob and he and they demanded that the throe men should be given up, but Davies refused. They thereupon resolved to " walk hi ra round." Wilding and others seized him by the col- laeand compelled him to go along the street. He was at length rescued by P.C. Richardson who was on bicycle duty on the St. Helens road, and taken to the police station for safety. Mr. Stuart contended that such acts as these tended to dela,y the settlement of the dispute. 'Davies and a number of other witnesses were called for the prosecution. Wilding said shat he was endeavonring to protect Davies, and that he was not one of the " walkers round." The bench considered the ease a bad one, and bound defendant over for three months, himself in £lO and two sureties of £5 each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>Tax ALIEORD RIOTING AT EATROCK.</head>
        <p>Tax ALIEORD RIOTING AT EATROCK.</p>
        <p>Frank Barrett, Peter Mitchell, Patrick O'Donnell, John Kenealy, John Caufield, Joseph Lynch, and Thomas Flynn, the seven men who were remanded from the Warrington Occasional Court on Monday last week to the Newton Sessions on the charge of assaulting the police at Haydock, were brought before the magistrates on the charge of rioting in Ilaydock on the 21st October. Mr. Stuart, as in the other cases, prosecuted and Mr. Arthur Browne, of Warrington, defended.—Mr. Stuart in his opening statement said that, it had been thought desirable to alter the charge of assault preferred at War- rington into one of rioting as the men had done every- thing contemplated by the word "riot." They had met together and had behaved in such a manner as to be a danger to everybody. He intended to ask their wor- ships to commit the prisoners to the Quarter Sessions or Assizes if he made out a prima facia case. On the 21st inst., about 150 people, among whom were the prisoners, congregated in Lime-st.' Havdock, and attacked a house inhabited by Aaron Litherland. Stones weighing about three-quarters of a pound were hurled at the house and also at the police. The police were compelled to use their batons, and upon this the mob fled. He (Mr. Stuart) denied that there had been any vindictiveness on the part of the police in taking the present proceedings. —Mr. Browne at this point asked that the witnesses should be ordered out of court.—An enormous number hereupon left tha court.—P.C. Appleby was the first wit- ness and said that he saw Barrett throwing stones at Litherland's house. He himself was assaulted and kicked by the crowd. He arrested Barrett but was compelled to let him ga again.—P.C. Rishton gave similar evidence of being assaulted in the mouth.—Aaron Litherland and P.C.'s Lightholder, Beaumont, Ferguson, and Roberts and Inspecter Pickaing, gave corroborative evidence.— Mr. Browne inquired how it was that the prisoners were not chargAd with riot at Warrington, to which Mr. Stuart said that it was intended to do so, but as the prisoners were remanded to Newton it was not deemed necessary to do so.—Aaron Litherland on being recalled said that the damage to the windows was Bs. 101—Mr. Browne for the defence said that the case of " riot " had not been made out. According to Inspector Pickering the crowd was swearing and quarrelling. That did riot con- stitute a riot and neither did the breaking of the windows. The men were all neighbours living in Lime-st.,and there was no intention cf assembling for unlawful purpose as they had a perfect right to be in the street at eleven o'clock at night. A quarrel between two men was the . commencement of the whole thing and the windows were accidentally broken in consequence. There were no people pursued and some of the prisoners had been found not guilty of damaging the windows at Warrington. Ile therefore considered the charge of riot had not been made out.—The magistrates however thought that the case should he Ilea'al at another Court and committed the prisoners to the Liverpool Assizes, bail being granted. ' ENORMOUS DECLINE IN RAILWAY TRAFFIC.</p>
        <p>The Midland Railway traffic returns published on Wednesday afford en indication of the serious effect which the protracted coal struggle has had upon the trade of the country. The figures for the past seventeen weeks show the decline has been continuous, and during that period amounted to £677,821.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>SETTLEMENT WITH SCOTCH MINERS.</head>
        <p>SETTLEMENT WITH SCOTCH MINERS.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the coal masters of Mid and East Lothian was held in Edinburgh to consider the demands of the men for an advance of 10 per cent. The miners were on strike on Wednesday. The result of the meeting was that a letter was sent to the miners' agent conceding the demand made by the men, and thus ending the strike. LIBERAL MEMBERS AND TRE CRISIS.</p>
        <p>A conference of Liberal members and others interested in the welfare of the miners was held on Wednesday in London. Mr. J. Hutton, chairman of the London County Council, presided, and said the lock-out had taught them two lessons—that the miners must have a living wage, and that a Board of Arbitration must be es• tablished. Among the speakers was Mr. Pickard, M.P., who referred to the dangerous calling of the miner and to the low rate of wages, and said the men were only fighting to keep body and soul together. In their struggle he had urged them to keep within the law, and he desired to avoid any utterance likely to cause discord at the coming conference. On the motion of Mr. B. Coleridge, M.P., a resllution was adopted expressing sympathy with the miners in their struggle to maintain the minimum standard of living consistent with a decent existence, and pledging the conference to support their cause in Parliament. Several other resolutions were proposed, but discussion on them was deferred till next Wednesday, when the conference will be resumed. Some of the speakers were in favour of the nationalisation of mines. A subscription for the distressed colliers was opened as practical evidence of sympathy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>AN OFFER DECLINED.</head>
        <p>AN OFFER DECLINED.</p>
        <p>The Collins Green and Bold Collieries Company, St. Helens, who employ 1,800 men, on Wednesday morning made an offer for the men to resume work :pending a general satlement at 7s. per day for colliers and 6s. per day for drawers. About 60 men waited upon Mr. Jack- son, the general manager, and he confirmed the offer,and said they would open their pits on Thursday morning at these rates. The men appeared quite satisfied, and sent messengers to find Mr. Glover, their agent. On return- ing the men reported that Mr. Glover advised them not to accept the offer, but to await the result of Friday's conference. Mr. Glover afterwards told a correspon- dent that he regarded the offer of the Collins Green Com- pany as simply a dodge to get the men to go to work. They did not intend to go back to work until there was a general settlement,or unless the old rate of wages were paid. The company was at five o'clock .informed that the men, acting on the advice of Mr. Glover, declined to resume work on the terms offered.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC NOTICE.—Not one hut lIIINDREDS of people of both sexes with wrecked and debilitated constituttons are yearly restored to sound and robust health again by Messrs. Wilkinson, 3 and 4, Fitzallan Square, Sheffield. Write to them for advice tree when suffering trom weakness, loss of flesh, appetite, disturbed rest, pimples, blotches. indiges- tion, pallid akin. nervousness, and general debility. Posi- tive cure for all Blood and Skin Diseases, acquired or consti- tutional. Sixty years' experienco. One address Os above) 1 for over 30 years. The" A 1 Mixture," 45. 91. the case. The Magic Mirror in envelope, four stamps. Sufferers a.sap pointed with Elsctrie. Belts should write to us at awe. —Adt;</p>
        <p>BRITISIE WOMEN'S TEMPERANCE • ASSOCIATION. MEETING AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The inaugural meeting of the Warrington branch of the British Women's Temperance Association was held in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-st., on Monday night. There was a very good attendance. The- chairwai occupied by Miss Bessie Dutton, and she wassupperted on the platform by Mrs. Shils- ton,.president of the Lancashire and Cheshire Union ;Miss Beightoo, secretary of the Union; Mrs. Leftwich,.secretary of the Wallasey branch ; Mrs. IL Garnett, Mrs. Starkey Stnith,Mrs. J. Yonge, the Misses Bradbury, Mrs. Gunnell, and Miss 'Roberts. SINISICIIy who was the first speaker,proceeded to give a history of the movement and formation of the British Women's Temperance Association. She said in 1873;.a lady—Mrs. Parker—went to America and at. a-temperance convention at Chicago, just Lefore ?dottier Stuart commenced the whi.ky war. Mrs. Parker was of opinion that a women's association could be formed in England,aml she invited Mother Stuart to come over, and the result of the visit was that in New- castle-on-Tyne, the British Women's Temperance Associ- ation was formed in 1874. The first president was Mrs. Parker. The nest year there was a meeting in London, In the Memorial Hall, on May 22nd, and the annual meeting has been held at that place ewer since. In 1876 John Bsight's sister became president, and she held that position until the time of her death in 1890, when Lady Henry Somerset was elected. Lady Henry Somerset de- voted nearly all her time and a great deal of her money to temperance work, and she was very anxious that a branch of the Association should be established in every town in England. • There were at present 610 branches, so that,there was plenty of work still remaining to be done. Mrs. Shilston-coneluded by moving the follow- ing resolution : " That this meeting,whilo acknowledg- ing with gratitude the value of the recognition of the importance of the question of liquor traffic legislation in the gracious speech from the throne, and heartily thank- ing the Government for Oh, introduction of the Liquor Traffic Local Control Bill on Feb. 27, expresses its great disappointment that the Government felt them- selves compelled to drop it, and calls upon them to re- introduce that measure on the earliest possible date in the sosion of 1894."</p>
        <p>Mrs. LETXWICH seconded the resolution. She felt quite certain that they would get the Veto Bill. She also thought that each woman should take a personal stand in the agitation in favour of the bill, as tl:e in- fluence of a woman went a great distance, and she hoped that in passing the resolution each one would feel her personal responsibility in the matter. Miss LEIGHTON in supperting the resolution, gave a list of the objects of the British Women's Temperance Association. Among other objects, it was in favour of the prohibition of the sale of drink to children, the abo- lition of grocers licenses, substitution of non-alcoholic wines for the Lord's Supper, Local Veto, etc. She said that in Liverpool there was a district in which there were no public-houses, and this part was inhabited by a sober, industrious people, and was an object lesson in favour of the Veto lhll. Miss Durrore hoped that they would keep "pegging away "in favour of the Veto Bill. If they did this they would attain their object. She hoped that everyone in that room would join the Association. She was sure that those present must hemn sympathy with its object, as they in some measure showed it by their presence. There was a great reason for an association similar to it in Warrington as there was one public-house to every 1200 or 251) of the population. That was out of all reason, and should not be allowed to remain as. They must do all they could to influence public opinion, and thus influence legislation. The motion on being put to the meeting was carried unanimously. During the evening, Miss Roberts sang "There is a green hilt far away," and " Tears," in an excellent manner. The usual votes of thanks closed the meeting.</p>
        <p>"YOUR LIFE IS NOT WORTH A STRAW." Nor worth a straw, eh? Then it was worth just na• thing—nothing at all. Who has not used that comrari- son a thousand times to expresF, absolute worthlessncss ? A straw ? The wind blows it away, fire burns it up, cattle tread it in the mud, it rots by the roadside. What of it? Who cares for a straw ?</p>
        <p>' Yet this is exastly what a doctor recently said to one of his patients, " Your life is not worth a straw." How much is a doctor worth who will speak so to one that trusts him, and has no hope hut in his skill ? For my part, if he were up for sale at auction, I would bid one straw for him—no more. Even if what lie said was true, he had no right to say it. Such a doctor is more likely to kill with his tongue than to cure with his ' drugs. A womaNtells the story, and she tells it well. If it doesn't sound like the truth, then I don't know what ever does. The dates and the facts are all there plain and orderly. "In the summer of 1878." she says, "I found myself feeling tired, languid, lo w-spirited, and weak. I felt as if some evil were about to happen. My appetite was poor, land after eating I had excruciating pain at my loins and sides. There was a horrible gnawing pain at the pit of my stomach, and a rising in the throat as if I should choke. My head felt as though I had a ton weight on it. Gradually I got worse, and for months could take only liquid food.. At night I lay awake for hours together. " Later on I suffered greatly from nervous prostra- tion. My legs trembled and shook so I feared to fall. If a knock came to the door I trembled from head to foot, I had frevent attacks which began with palpit- ation of the heart and sudden stoppage of the breath. At these times I was speechless and helpless. They say I looked like a corpse, cold and bloodless, my finger pails and lips having turned black. After a while this would pass off, leaving me weak and prostrate. I got so emaciated and thin that I was only a bay of bones, and so weak I had tl take hold of the furniture to steady myself as I crossed the room. As time went on the nervousness and forebodings of evil so increased that I feared I should go out of my mind. The neigh- bours said it would be a mercy if the Lord would release me from my sufferings. In this condition I continued for over four years, dur- ing which time I consulted five doctors, but nothing they gave me did any good. They all said my ailment was heart disease, and one said, ' Your life is not worth a straw."</p>
        <p>In despair I gave up taking physic, a 3 I felt that nothing would save me. In May, 18132, ten years ago, a lady (Mrs. Richardson) called at my house, told me of Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup, and strongly advised me to try it. I did sa, and felt somewhat better after the first bottle ; and by the time I had taken three bottles I was comp:etely cured. From that to this I have had no return of the attacks, and am so strong I can do any kind of work. But for Seigel's Syrup I should have been in my grave long ago. I wish others to know this, and will answer any who call or write. (Signed). Emma WICICENDEN (wife of William Wickenden, gardener), Pembroke Villas, 123, Moffat-road, Thornton Heath, March 17th, 1692.</p>
        <p>So it turned out that her life was not only worth a straw, but worth a whole golden harvest of health and better days. Yet no thanks to the doctors. Her com- plicated symptoms puzzled and alarmed them, to be sure, but why ? Is it not the doctors' duty to under- stand such things ? Most assuredly. just as a lawyer should know the law, or a pilot the rocks, tides, and lights of a coast. Had some of these medical men known that Mrs. Wickenden's malady was indigestion and dyspepsia, and not heart disease, they might possibly have relieved her. But, confused by the symptoms, they were blind to the cause. We may well wonder it there are many such doctors in England. Cases like gals show that the clear sight belonged to Mother Seigel ; and to her remedy hosts of people in this country are indebted for physical salvation, when in very truth, their lives seemed as straws. Remember this was ten years ago, and the malady has not returned, showing that the cure was a per- manent one.</p>
        <p>MRS. S. A. ALLEN'S</p>
        <p>A A T v ORLD'S</p>
        <p>•ITAIR RESTOpER.</p>
        <p>[t is not a dye.</p>
        <p>Quickly changes gray or</p>
        <p>faded hair to its natural</p>
        <p>colour. A perfect hair dresa•</p>
        <p>ing, delicately perfumed.</p>
        <p>RUNCU 11,N KURA L SANITARY AUTHORITY.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting was held at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday. Mr. J. Mclntyre presided, and there were present the Rev. J. W. N. Tanner, Rev. J. Hanby, Messrs. C. T. Whitley, J. Stokes, H. E. Heywood, E. Aaron, W. Pickering"). Weir, J. M. Williams, 8. Woodward, E. Bratherton, J. Parkin- son, J. Hall, C. Mountield, IV. Whitlow, T. Lewis, J. Brandreth, T. Baxter, C.C., Thos. Ford, J. Ashton (clerk), O. Ashton (deputy clerk), J. Farrington (nuisance inspector). AUDITOR'S CERTIFICXTES. The Clerk read the certificates of the auditor for the half-year ending Michaehnas and Lady Day hist, show- ing that thc accounts were correct. There had been no surcharges.</p>
        <p>HALTON PAROCHIAL COMMITTER. At a meeting of this Committee on October 28th, the Clerk reported that the agreement with Sir Richard M. Brooke with reference to the sewer emptying into Hal- t m Brook would be submitted to the Central Authority on Tuesday next and the luspec..or submitted tenders for the work to be executed in connecion with the said sewer. On the motion of Mr. Gamon, seconded by Mr. Looker, it was resolved that Mr. Ashley's tender be ac- cepted.—The Clerk said the agreement with Sir R. M. . Brooke was that certain sewage matter be allowed to go into Halton Brook, on the payment of 2s. 6d. a year. and that on a twelve months' notice the Authority would be bound to take out the sewage.—The agreement was signed and semed. SERIOUS OUTBREAK .OF INFECTIOUS DISEASE. The Medical Officer (Dr. .Adams) reported a case of typhoid fever at Lunt's RrINV .(back houses) Weston. The case was It very mild one but he.found that the privies and ashpits not only for this house but most in the row were ins disgraceful condition from want of effici- ent scavenging. 118- had repeatedly reported on this matter in connection with these houses. Ta o cases of scarlet fever had occurred, at the Ship Canal Huts Weston Point. The families are related and visit each other, but he (the officer) was told that there had been several cases of scarlet fever in these huts. Little, how- ever, could be done in the way of isolation. A case of diphtheria and scarlet fever had occurred at Brook Place, Halton-road, Halton. ('be disease was attributed to the open ditch or sewer which ran along the bottom of the garden, before discharging into Halton Brook. He had on a previous occasion recommended that the ditch should be piped in and discharged .into a settling tank with a tnidfeather before being allowed to join the brook. C9rtaiuly the sewage must increase in volume and offensiveness, from the new houses which drained into it. He considered that the cesspool on the premises should also be dealt with as it was also offensive. Three cases of scarlet fever had occurred at Frodsliam Bridge, Sutton. The souree of infection was doubt, ul. Seven cases of searkt fever had occurred at Kingsley, infection was attributed to Kingsley schools. Two cases of scar- let fever had occurred at Norley. The source of infec- tion was doubtful. Two cases of scarlet fever had occurred at Lower Stretton. The drainage of the house was blocked and constituted a nuisance which should be remedied. A case of typhoid fever had occurred at Appleton; tho disease was believed to have been contracted in the Isle of Man. A case AI typhoid fever had also occurred at • Bartiagton Broth. 24 vases of infectious disease had been reported during the last fortnight. He had visited all the above cases and issued the usual preventive measures. Mr. Aaron said he was in Weston Point on the previous afternoon and heard of a man repairing a road with human dung. There might well be fever in the place. It was the•first time he had. seen a street mended in such a manner.—The Medical Officer and the Nuisance Inspector said they had heard nothing of it. Mr. Aaron moved that the Inspector should go and see the place at once, and after inquiry, issue a summons against the offender and press for the inflimion of the full penalty of the low.—Mr. Parkinson seconded the motion which was carried.—'f he Medical Officer said since he had prepared his report, he had received notifications of further outbreaks of typhoid fever and scarlet fever at Preston Brook, Dutton, Kingsley, Norley and Frodsham. Mr. Woodward said wile regard to the epidemic of scarlet rover at Kingsley, that there was culpable neg- ligence of the parents irk not reporting outbreaks of the disease to medical men. He had heard of several cases of scarlet fever in houses and no doctors in attendance, and children from these places attending schools in the neighbourhood. The report that the disease hod been contracted at the schools was no doubt quite true. It ' seemed to him that something should be done to close the schools in the district,—The Clerk said if no medi- cal man was in attendance, the parent was obliged to notify the ease under a penalty.—The Chairman thought it would be well to take a case bef ire the magistrates. —Mr. Woodward thought the cases were not notified through ignorance, aed he would not take such a severe step. It was eventually decided that the Inspector , should make an inspection of the district. With regard to Halton Brook, it was stated that it had been decided to pipe the drain, and a tender accepted for the work, —Mr. Aaron said it was quite time this matter was settled. It had been recommended months ago that the ditch should be piped.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>DATE FINDING FORA HUNDRED YEARS.</head>
        <p>DATE FINDING FORA HUNDRED YEARS.</p>
        <p>To find out the day of the week, the day of the month and the year being given,tAke the last two figures of the year; add to these one fourth of their value,leavin,; out any fraction ; then add to these the date of the month ; then add the figure for the month as given in this list : —January 3,February 6 (in Leap Year s),March 6,April 2, May 4, June 0, July 2, August 5, September 1, Oct. 3, November 6, December 1. Divide the sum by 7 and the remainder will give you the number of the day of the week. When there is no remainder, the day is Saturday. Example—July 14, 1827. Last two figures of year 27 Add one fourth Add date ot month Add figure for the month 2</p>
        <p>Divide by 7-49</p>
        <p>July 14, 1827, was, therefore, a Saturday,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>RUNCORN BOAItD OF QUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOAItD OF QUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting was held at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday.. Mr. J. Mclntyre presided and there were present the Revs. J. W. N. Tanner, J. Hanby, and Dr. Gastaldi ; Messrs. S. Taylor, T. llandley, T.-Lea, A. R. Norman, D. H. Jones, J. W. 13aguley, A. Broome, C. T. Whit ley, J. Stokes, 11. E. Heywood, B. Aaron, IV . Pickering, J. Weir, J. M. Williams, 5. Woodward, B. Bratherton, J. Parkin- son, J. Hall, C. Mounfield, W. Whitlow, T. Lewis, J. Braudreth, B. Jackson, T. Baxter, C. C. Thomas Ford; J. Ashton (clerk), and G. Ashton (deputy clerk.) A CALL UPON' SURETIES. The Clerk read the certificates of Mr. Maw, the audi- tor, respecting the nemunts of the late assistant overseer of Kingsley, as follovr : "1 do hereby certify that in this accouht for the half year ended Michaelmas, 1893, ot Joseph Challtnor, late assistant overseer of the titenship of Kingsley, in the Runcorn Union, and County of Chester, the sum of £49 15L 11d, is now due to the said township."—lt was unanimously decided on the proposi- tion of Mr. Aaron, seconded by Mr. Parkinson, to call upon the sureties, who were bound in tho sum of £350 to pay the sum required. TIM RUNCORN RELIEVING OFFICER.</p>
        <p>.- 1 In accordance with the notice he had given, Mr. Parkinson moved that the salary of Mr. Thomas Cooke. relieving officer for Runcorn relief district, be increased from £lOO to £l2O. He said Mr. Cooke was a very good officer, very kind to the poor and he had saved the Union over £lOO during the past year.—Mr. Brandreth seconded the motion. Mr. Cooke was a highly efficient officer, who looked after the interest of the poor and the interest of the Union. lie weuld not allow the Guardians to be imposed upon neitlipr would lie allow the poor to suffer. —Mr. fifywood said the Committee who had had the matter in hand felt that Mr. Cooke was entitled to an advanco.—The Bey. J. Hanby said Mr. Cooke had pro. inised that should he at any time receive the appoint- ment of vaccination officer or school attendance officer, lie would relinquish the increase and Mr. Parr had said that was the fairest offer he had ever heard in his life. The fact that Mr. Parr could comfortably stop away was a point in favour of the increase. (Laughter.) Mr. Weir said this was not a time for increasing salaries. fie stated that Mr. Cooke was now doing better than the farmers in the district.—Mr. Whitlow held that Mr. Cooke should not receive more than the other relieving officers, but he pointed out that he had more work to perform. although the area of his district was not so large as Budworth or Frodsham.—Eventually Mr. Whit- low moved and Mr. Broome seconded that the salary be fixed at £ll5. The amendment was lost by 15 votes to 11 and the original motion was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>EIGIIT HOURS BILL.</head>
        <p>THE EIGIIT HOURS BILL.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Words, M.P., has addressed another letter to Mr. Gladstone on the subject of the Eight soars Bill. lie points out that the agitation in favour of the bill up to the date of its second rea3ing in Al ty had cost the miners £10,000; that the majority by which it passed its sec:ond reading was quite exceptional in regard to a private member's bill ; and that if it tailed to g( t through the Committee stage this session all the expense and laboor would be thrown away. Ile (Mr. Woods) thinks that if the rremier couid see his way to give one day either in this month or December for the passage of the bill through Committee he would do much to satisfy the miners and the supp,irters of this important measure.</p>
