      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY. NOVEMBER 4, 1893.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. T. STEAD, the energetic A ewe for Dipsomania, editor of the "Review of Re- views," believes that he. has found a remedy for dipsomania. About two months ago, Mr. STEAD made a public appeal for half-a-dozen drunkards of the most pronounced and hopeless type, upon whom he desired to test the efficacy of the new remedy. It would seem that the supply of this much-to-be-pitied elass of men is far beyond any possible demand for them, for Mr. STEAD says he was inundated with oars from all parts of the three kingdoms. From the numer- ous cases brought under his notice he selected eight, " whose record, both from the point of heredity ad of persistent hard drinking, would stand the severest test." The eight dipsomaniacs —four of whom were men, and four women— were provided with accommodation in the house of a professional mim near the British Museum, and the experiment was there carried out. Two of the men wore commercial travellers,one a solici- tor, and the other an ex-naval officer; and of the women three had been married and one was a spinster. The treatment commenced on September 11, and ended on Octo- ber 12. The result, we are told, has been an un- qualified success, the only exception being one of tbe women who, it is believed, did not conform to the regulations laid down. "For," says Mr. STEAD, "what has happened ? Seven poor miser- able wrecks, drenched- with whisky or soddened with gin, and in some eases with an additional craving for morphia and laudanum, have been in one short month so completely weaned from all desire for alcohol that they may be trusted to go through the most tavern-spotted thoroughfare without feeling the slightest inclination to resume their potations. Nay, more, not only has all crav- ing for drink boon killed in their system, but in its place there has been devel*pal an absolute hatred and loathing for intoxicants, a loathing so acute that some of our patients have walked down the middle of the street rather than use the foot- path which led them too close to the reek of the taproom." Then, as to the wonderful medicine which effects these remarkable results ? 'Unfor- tunately, upon this point information is scanty, if not altogether unsatisfactory. Mr. STEAD believes' it to be a concoction of South American origin, hut the secret of its preparation is in the possession of one man—a Mr. J. L. GLIDDON. There is, of course, no reason why at ! least ono good thing should not conic out of South America. That part of the world is, it is true, ! most famous for its frequent revolutions, and its financial crises. And, one cmnot help remend er- ing that quack nostrums are generally credited with a foreign origin—sometimes it is said they have been discovered on the prairies of North America, and again, perhaps, we are told that it is the ingenious natives of Central Africa that we have to thank for the healing compound. So far as it goes Mr. STEAD'S testimony is satisfactory enough. We do not doubt that the persons he alludes to have been, for the time being, relieved of their destructive cravings for alcohol. A ques- tion which will naturally arise in most minds will be as to the premanency of the cure. If the cure be a permanent one it is capable of bringing bless- ings into many a household. Now that Mr. STEAD has taken this matter in hand he would do</p>
        <p>well by keeping an eye upon the eight" patients" he was instrumental in securing, and reporting further as to the results of the experiment.</p>
        <p>WHAT changes have taken place The Coal Struggle. during the week in connection with</p>
        <p>the coal crisis tend on the whole towards a settlement of the dispute. An arrange- ment by which the representatives of the two contending parties can meet together in confer- ence has been come to, and that, surely, in itself may be regarded as the beginning of the end. The struggle has lasted longer than oither party expected it would at the commencement, and there are signs that both sides are longing for peace—" peace with honour" of courses It seems to be generally expected that the miners repre- sentatives will ask that the pits should,in the first instance at all events,be re-opened at the old rate of wages. They can certainly contend that even a considerable reduction upon the present price of coal would allow an ample margin for the employ- ers' profits. On the other hand some employers have placed themselves in a position which makes it difficult for them to accede to such a demand. ' One largo employer has declared that if he opened his pits at the old rates he would lose upon every ton of coal he supplied under contracts that he has accepted. This employer and many others besides have, as it has been well said, sold their workmen's labour before they had bought it. They have accepted contracts which could only be made to pay by a substantial reduction in colliers wages. The lock-out which has wrought such havoc in the country, was practically part of the bargain when these coAracts were signed. The point at issue is now really whether the miners have to suffer a reduction from their hard-earned wages, .or whether coalowners who conduct their business on speculative principles, shall suffer the penalty of a serious miscalculation. Some people seem to be in doubt as to whether the stoppage should be termed a strike or a lock-out. As a matter of fact it seems to be now taking the form of an un- doubted strike—of a strike of coalowners against carrying out the contracts they have entered into. Were these contracts out of the way, it is well-nigh certain that there would quickly be a general resumption of work. Not the least significant incident in connection with tho crisis is the meeting of Liberal members of Parliament which took place in London, on Wednesday. The sympathy of that meeting was entirely on the side of the miners. It is quite evident that the lock-out will, should it continuo much longer, develope into a political question of the first magnitude. Men who are by no means extreme in their political views, arid who are not oven in full sympathy with the miaers,nro already beginning to speak of nationalisation of the mines as the most likely way of preventing similar in- dustrial calamities in the future. This nation is dependent upon its industries, and, wealthy as it is, it cannot really afford a recurrence of strikes or lock-outs in the coal trade.</p>
        <p>ENGLISHMEN are now invited to take A '' Grea.t full credit for another " groat vio- -1 Victory.' tory "in South Africa. A largo I number of the armed employes of the S3uth African Company aro invading 'Matabeleland, and as the natives are warlike—offshoots, in fact, of the formidable Zulus—they have resented this in- trusion, and a battle has been the result. The Matabele aro, for tho most part, armed with assegais and other weapons which are very formi- dable at close quarters, but the Company's forces are equipped with all the latest " resources of civilisation "—such as Maxim guns and repeating rifles. Under these circumstances it could not be, expected that the fight Would be one of the Homeric sort—that is to say, the opposing forces never got to close quarters. The dusky warriors who own the slimy of King Lobengula—or "Le Ben " as he is called in South Africa where "niggeri " are not held in the highest esteem— rushed towards the white men in dense masses. But the Maxim guns wc re set to work, and, to- gether with the repeating rifles, they burled for- ward a perfeot storm of leaden hail, thus com- pletely decimating the ranks of the Matabele. A few attempts were made to face this destruc- tive avalanche of bullets, but in vain, and the Company's forces were left in possession of the field, and it is said, of no fewer than three thousand dead bodies. The casualties amongst the White Men were almost nil. This latest massacre of African natives, for such it really is, will, it may be expected, not make the people of this country any more satisfied with government by company promoters. A charter was granted to the South African Company by the late Tory government, and upon the strength of this the Company gas proceeded to exploit certain parts of South Africa in a way which does not bring credit upon the English name. Envoys who have been sent by native potentates have been murdered in cold blood, and other incidents have taken place whicn read more like the pro- ceedings of Cortez and Pizarro in their conquests of Mexico and Peru than the deeds of Englishmen in this nineteenth century. And then the financial transactions of this company upon which the Con- ervatives thought fib to bestow a charter have been, to say the least, of a rather remarkable character. Seeing how events have developed it is not at all unlikely that a considerable part of the ' time of Parliament, brief as is to be the autumn session, will be taken up by this Sou th African muddle.</p>
        <p>No LibEral can profess to be satis- fied with the result of the munici- Oct Veils. pal elections in Warrington on Wed- nesday. Three years ago, when the members who retired from the Town Council this year, were re- turned, the Liberals enjoyed a very notable triumph as seven out of the nine candidates elected were members of the party. This year, unfor- tunately, the position has been exactly reversed, and seven Tories have secured seats us compared with two Liberals. Such a result is all the more to he regretted, as it brin gs us one step nearer to classdomination in the town,which may be of the most offensi4e and injurious character. The liquor trade is notoriously powerful in Warring- ton, and never before has it displayed its power with greater effect than at these elections. Dur- ing the last few weeks the entire machinery of the Tory party seems to have been at its disposal, and out of the nine candidates selected to represent that party no fewer than five were directly in- ; t3rested in the' trade. As we have before ' said we have no objection—and there cni be no reasonable objection—to brewers and publicans having a fair share in the management of the town's affairs, but we do object to the attempts which are now being openly made by this one class to secure a 'controlling influence in tho Town Council. Such an attempt on the part of any class of tradesmen in the town—of grocers, butchers or bakers for instance—would be repre- hensible and would be resisted, though it is</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>• WARRINGTON UNION AS:Sri:SRN"'</head>
        <p>• WARRINGTON UNION AS:Sri:SRN"'</p>
        <p>difficult to see how the interests of these of the community could differ from the in of the general body of ratepayers. With to the publicans, however, the case is ell different. Everyone can see how the keel, public-houses may benefit at the cost of demoralization of the town. Predominance Ifl' Town Council means control of the Watch mittee and through the Watch Committee • police. Yet it is a large and most important of the duties of the police to see that pill respect the obligations that are placed upon by law. We had an example of what Is expected from a Watch Committee which is ' pletely under the thumb of the liq uor trade ' the Warrington Committee lately went out way to impede the action of the licensing)' and to try to throw discredit on the action were taking. Now that the publicans thoroughly asserted their ascendancy in tho Tory party we may expect an even more nouncel and retrograde policy on the part oi Watch Committee. " Bung " has fought and this battle and he will expect to reap the sr victory.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUA._</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Warrington of Guardians was held on WeAnesday, in the • room of the Workhouse. There were -</p>
        <p>Messrs. a Ratcliffe (chairman), R. Heat. Houghton, S. Davies, A. J. Jolley, A. Jolley., dough, Clarke, Mather, J. Houghton, G. PI Ilibbert, Smith, P. Warburton, J. Johnson. J. J. Dobson, E. Aukland, H. J. Longton, Swint J. Shaw, W. Balharry, J. C. Sutton (clerk), S. (deputy clerk), C. H. Ward (master of the and Mines (superintendent of Padgate The usual business was of a routine charai there was nothing of public interest.</p>
