se dials salary of Mrs. Mines, the Matron of Padgate Schoe°'t increased by -£5 per annum. He said that she ha° e in the service oe the B lard for so:nething like 13 9' When engaged she had been promised an increase short time, bue she had not received any up to the: sent. She only received £5 per annum more than foster mothers of the institution, although she„ many more things to do than they. She w'7l,i worst paid officer in any part of England in a s p position. She received £25 per annum, and the_s pose increase would only make it £3o.—Mr. filn; seconded the metion.—Mr. W. .HOUGHTON woved a amendment that the salary be not increased. lie give a few fieures in support of his motion. T 18 officers at Padgate,including two non-resident - and they were paid an aggregate salary of £597 annum. . There were on an average 190 children Ti institution, which n.eant that it cost £3 per chi', annum in salaries alone. In the Work home there about 400 inmates, with 15 officials with an aggl salary cf £769 63., so that was about half the 'Joni look after, the Padgate salaries were not very far the salariesof the Workhouse.At the time Mrs.MineS to Padgate, the salaries amounted to £437 11.:s. that they had considerably increased since then,alt, there were no more ehi,:dren now than then. Mr. 11(f", ton next proceeded to question the efficiency °,/el Mines as a matron. Ile also made some reinarka'm sections, and described a visit he made W F 37 schools one morning at 7.30. There wet' female attendants out of bed, and towards eight c they came down " like frightened cats." The; ren were getting their own breakfast ready,arn; in danger of being scalded to death. He main that Mrs. Mines was not entitled to an increase in E —Mr. A. J. JOLLEY seconded the amendment. Ile borated Mr. Houghton's assertions and he belir persons doiog their best to earn an increase salaries, arid not for their salaries to be inere';',l make them more diligent.—Mr. CLARKE supporn amendment.—On the amendment