        <p>COMMITTEE. t • A meeting of this Committee was held Workhouse. yesterday. There were present, Istlenl Balharry (chairman), G. Ratcliffe, J. Ilouglit°"a. Jolley, R. Heaton, J. C. Sutton (clerk), nn - Owut (valuer.) ALTERATrONS TO vartrous wormg. Mr. OWEN reported additions to Whitecroes Works, at the east of Milner-st. In this CO(' high pressure engines and five boilers had been 03 by one condensing engine, two boilers, and t" one-storey engine house bad been replaced Of °lle lofty. lie furth, r reported that MiSsrs. Crosfield and, had purchased the property known as Cartwrt' flotlle Works, which adjoined their premises. "td sent, the old tennts, Messrs. Dixon, Kenyon, I r.l, nett, were in occupation of the builoings, rn Ol-0a was not in use. The sidings had been connected thrla into the high level railway in addition to the 05 cess to the tow level railway. At present, there I. pecuniary benett to Messrs. Crosfield and ScTd, they were about to erect buildings in the new on their siding.. He could take the quettlon consideration v, hen he valued them. 'rho glletrie`:i was about to undergo alteration as well as the 10 containing it, but as it was was not yet begun, tile' ation must be postponed. • titn. There had been alterations in Mr. R. A. Naylor a in her works, in replas.ting old buildings with newt 841 ,0 0 making additional ground, which had been CO V'licitlO' to necessitate art entirely new survey lnr The survey had been made and the valuation cr forwarded to the next meeting of the Cornmitt,7l,. TIIE NEW L. I:N.-W. DEVIATION RAIL" 050% Its respect of :he adjustment required of OW curie'''. !tient of the deviation railway which was now cc d and the new station, instead of the old railway ono/ te• tion,it was necessary to ga accurately the ienth railway in the borough of Warrington, and the municipal portion. As the landmarks were t" tiej extent, destroyed by the Manchester Ship CanNr the new railway, Mr. Owen wrote to ark whe'_„anYV could have a pial