        <p>MILTON CONGREGATIONAL CHURCN, WIDNES. On Suimay :•pecial qurvices were held in behalf of the and of Hope. In the morning the Rev. R. G. Hughes Preached an eloquent sermon, and in the evening an teixocen b Ilent discourse was delivered to a crowded congrega- eveninya athe Rev. J. H. Riddett (Haydock). On Monday public meeting was held,when a miscellaneous 7htertaininent was given by a number of friends. Ma. s Lewis presided. itaoBz, ;.11 PRITCHARD, Watchmaker and Jeweller, 51, High- .. sttluorn.—Advt. AsSATMT ON A CONSTABLE AT RUNCORN. k_lin Saturday, Joseph Williams, sailor, was brought r tore Mesars. John Brundrit and Henry Reynolds, at t!llticorn Police Court, for being drunk and disorderly in er-street. on the previous night, and assaulting P.O p: the erratt. The officer stated that the prisoner struck him I' chest. and kicked him several times on the legs. had difficulty in taking him into custody. The Prisoner was fined 155. and costs, or 21 days' imprison- -I'lllTc/faun's have now stood the test for ft fty years. and quallt 41,,7 and first class workmanship are still guaranteed.</p>
        <p>RETIREMENT Or P.S. WHALLEY, FORILERLY hyda RuscoaN. ys arm at the conclusion of the fortnightly Wade of the Na7ntwich and Shavinkton sections of the Leah Constabulary at Nantwich, Superintendent anded a handsome marble clock to' Sergeant !1,,Y, who was for many years at Runcorn, where he the iespcet and good will of all classes. The clock B,4;La silver plate which was thus described. " Pre- Sergeant Whalley by the members of the Nara- aft o Division, Cheshire Constabulary, on his retirement queir,,2s years service, as a Mark of esteem and good the f A large framed phrtograph of the members of w orce accompanied the timepiece. Dtst,i2 want (1 a Cwrs and ELEGANT WEDDING or Adre.Y PREirRE g..) to PRITCHARD'S, 61. High-street.—</p>
        <p>John ,SUOOTING GAME NEAR RUNCORN. jun., was summoned before Mr. A. cou'u,all and Mr. James Handley, at Runcorn Police Muir: en Mond ly, to answer a charge of pursuing ano "g partridge on the land of the Ship Canal Corn- Inland Revenue prosecuted. John Morris, 50r,12 at Norton, on the 30th August. Mr. Moore, sorvisor of Morris, furmr, Norton, proved that on the thelk't.lg of the day in question, the defendant was on pas,„.„"tp Cinal with a gun when a covey of partridges atiru• The defendant fired and killed one of the birds __suit fell on the land belonging to Sir Richard Brooke. defer' peechley, Inland Revenue officer, said the setonlant ha I admitted to him that he had shot at some par''',ll.l,3 and sparrows but denied having shot at the and , 71"ge.—The defendant still held to this statement beer;',? was fined Ws. and costs 203. He had previously - for carrying a gun without a license. At Moms Com, STEALING AT WIDNES. Tira idnes Police Court on Monday, before Mr. T. S. ter ;Tits ard Mr. H. Wade Deacon, three boys, named Frreet arry (15), Railway-street ; William Melton Railway-street ; and Martin Doyle (13). Caroline- , were charged together with stealing 26th. of cat Lrva aggon near Widnes Dock, the property of the tt RAilway,Company. Mr.Tirainis said that coal noet,""g Was becoming a permanent offence, an•l It could tja _Z° on unchecked. The law did not allow them to 84,71PPed, so a fine of 2s. 63. would be inflicted or anrit days in default.—Jobn Butler (13), Caroline-street ra trick Tvrell (13), Margaret-street, were charged in!'" stealine 58'b. of coal from Muspratt's works the Wo°PettY of the United Alkali Co., and valued at 9d. The rd Ye were orderedLi ieceive six strokes, but Tyrell nitAi It. 6d " said her lad was subject to fits, therefore a fine was i nposed in his case, or seven days in de-</p>
        <p>RUNCORN AND DISTRICT TEACILSRS' ASSOCIATION. geeeral meeting, succeeded by a soiree, in conbeC- eveVrlth the above association, took place. on Friday th" 11! last week in Holy Trinity Infants' Schoolroom. 54 e absence of the bresident,• Mr. Jordan, the chair vas ?eeuPied by Mr. Cookson, the vice-president. There 111,4! grtod attendance. Mr. Moulton, the secretary, u,„2S read t of vice-president and treasurer for the the minutes, the meeting proceeded to the omatior an 4 d fnr thnal uatt. r eton.Us pncioetni voof Teacheis, and the gentlemen named positions were Mr. T. Ellery and Mr. Circular letters from the Union re the Science the Art Department, and claqsification in drawing ; and asselsition of the Union, with a request that district new Minns should not relinquish their efforts ta secure consirmbers whereby it would be strengthened, were ewe 'red, and resolutions passed relative thereto. A f motion was given to the effect that their meet- the e„. held quarterly instead of bi-monthly, seeing that ttm—salleialon had now been established. After the aociai nof business, the gathering became of a jycharacter, and the proceedings were greatly en-</p>
        <p>Nv DEATH OF Mn. EDWARD EOWSON. _ )zawe regret to record the death of Mr. Euwar had steLn• Lymm, from rheumatic fever. Deceasedh *re 'Ten for nearly 17 years associated with e sale e&amp; Messrs. Edwin Richardson and Son, whole- Oeveneeers, Bridge-street. Warrington, where by dear•service and kindness of heart he had tow sed himself to all. His many friends in the u car_ will be greatly shocked that so promising a er should have been so prematurely ended. Th. WVCLIFFE YOUNG PEOPLE'S SOCIETY. held e4eal weekly meeting of this society was Pre:ll Monday evening,when a good number were Drizallt The Rev. J. Yenge occupied the chair.A attbi- "8,4- competition had been arranged on the aa (3, "Philanthrophic work amongst the poor; and trw it could be best applied in Warrington,' The flenPapers sent in were read and discussed. ehairret);:n')wing took part in the discussion : The teore Messrs. T. Brown, S. Rowlands, J. Pod- -0 ttN, 4"r, and C. Taylor. A judge-was appointed ard the prizes, Wneihmt, , —orirt„, -8 At-v.,..meRED.—J. and W„ DUTTON, Sankey-3f., The TRAINING COLLEGE STUDENTS. et th,, exhibition of .£lO offered by the Committee the ter Training College to the candidate who gained 1,1011.„,5t Place in the religious knowledge examina- atieei," Woo by Miss Kate Rose,lleath-side School. ,„blent—"e has also gained a first-class in the Govern- fhetarernination, Miss Lily M. Warburton, St. School,Pendleton, Manchester, vvon an ex- tiOn 6-?1" Of X 5, because in the Government examin- tiapai: took the second place among the candidates wittb—ed for admission to the above College. Miss leci,„a:..Urton has a first-class in the religious know- L examination. 441;:t1,47j. S. Clentaa-n, Ironmonger, Warrington and</p>
        <p>Lel%.• Trot LATE Hog. L. POWYS' WILL. Ing Personally valued at £10,175 9 7d. _t3. 9 the th 4"")Pcid William Henry Fox Powys, who ed d.„418,h of July last, bequeathed to his unmarried bee; lit"s all the plate pictures, and articles of %,e-eaold rise and ornament in his residenees, 16, anellitb.erry. Place, St. Ann's-hill, Chertsey, Norton 11ni„ llaa, and Bewsey Hall, Warr;ngton and 14 to 20aTert804Y the c3olie Sue Hospital, for the Cancer I ar, n Warrington Infirmary, £2O; to his 30"tmers8, the uton. „ and Rey. lildward Victor Robert poww37 and Colonel the lion. Charles James Fox 418 sTl', X5O each; to his Lancashire agent,iso ; to ldren's nurse, £6O; and other legacies. His the le in Queentberry-place is to be sold, and from are tue,erne of the proceeds, annuities of £250 each auci t°l ue paid ta the testator's married daughters chtu remainder of the income to his unmarried Pcetvgynatjr,• The residue of his property Mr. Fox datiz Laves in trust in equal shares for all his 1940,1",e!5. but three-fourths of the income of the Wske estate are to be allowed to item:- income for twenty-one years.'one-fourth of tie beln„ With the income from the accumulations n'eanwhile paid to his dauVacra.</p>
