      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <p>A NEW FOOTBALL CLUB. An Association Football Club has been formed in connection with the Post Office at Warrington. A well attended meeting was held on Thursday night when the officers and committee were elected and rules made. Three members were also selecled to make terms and purchase the necessary require- ments. A field has been obtained and erelong the football enthusiasts of the P.O. hope to give a good account of themselves. WARRINGTON DRAMATIC SOCIETY. The dates of the two performances to be given by this Society in aid of the debt on the Gymna- sium building, are now drawing very near, and we would remind our readers that the plan is now open for the dress stalls, second seats, and balcony at Mr. R. H. Pearson's, Bold-st. Those who are de- sirous et obtaining good seats do well to book early, as large audiences are anticipated. The per- formances take place on Wednesday and Thursday, November 15th and 16th, in the Gymnasium, and a special feature is to be made of the proscenium, scenery, and stage appointments generally. We. trust that the efforts which are being made by the. Society to assist a worthy object will meet with the success they deserve. ALL HOUSEROLD LINEN, hesume I and marked, 'free of charge.—J. and W. Durroy, Sankey st.—Advt. WHEELERS ENgOY TIM/SELVES. The sixth annual soiree in connection with the above club was held in the arriugton Gymnasium, Palmyra Square. on Monday evening, when there were about 150 persons present. The interior of the spacious building had been beautifully decorated for the occasion with art curtains, plants, fairy I sinps,&amp;c. The proceedings passed off very success- fully. Dancing was kept up until 2-30, the music being supplied by Mr. R. H. Pearson's orchestral band. Mr. T. Morris and Mr. C. H. Wood acted as M.C's. and the whcle of the arrangements were carried out by Mr. E. Pickavance, the secretary of the club. The refreshments were provided by Mr., Chantry. In addition to the Warrington cyclists ' and their friends representatives from the Widnes,: St. Helens, Anfield, Bowden, Sale, Manchester, and Wallasey Wheelers Club were present. NO REASONABLE OFFER REFUSED, if made at once at 'Malan e Furniture Warehouse, Bridge Foot, Warrington, - See fuller Adv., page 4.—Advt. STEALING FOWL AT WINWICS.. At the Town Hall, Newton, on Monday morning, before Colonel J. L. Wood, John Hall, a collier, liv- ing at 109, Legh-street, Earlestown, was charged with stealing three fowl from Alder-lane farm, Winwick, sometime between Saturday night or Sunday morning, of the value of six shillings. P.C. Burns met thaprisoner in Legh-street on Sunday morning, and noticing something bulky under his coat asked him what be had got there. The pris- oner said nothing, and the constable putting his hand in his pocket felt some feathers, asked what that was the prisoner said "a pheasant," and he was then taken to the police station when the fowl were found upon him, which was afterwards identified by Mr. Longton as his property. Prisoner was remanded.—The prisoner was brought U at the 'Warrington County Petty Sessions on Wednes- day, and was fined 10s. and costs or 14 days imprisonment. STOVES.-GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, Bridge-street, Warrington. GOOD TEMPLARY AT LATCHFORD. A public entertainment in connection with the Bonehill Branch Lodge was held in the Temper- ance Hall on Monday evening. The chair was oc- cupied by Bro. A. Ford, chief templar of the lodge. The juvenile choir and the Latchford glee party were in attendance. The programme was of a very interesting character, consisting of songs, readings, recitations, and addresses. The meeting was opened by singing and prayer, after which a rous- ing address was delivered by the chairman. A humorous recitation in the Scotch dialectwas given by Bro. H. Ronald who received a hearty encore. Bros. J. Hankey, J. Ditchfield, H. Fletcher, F. Lloyd, Sis. L. Hough, and J. Scott assisted in the meeting. Bro. B. Few with his recital "Amos and Ruth " caused much •amusement. Bro. L. Ellison gave a stirring address, pressing the necessity of sobriety in Latchford. The last item—but not least—was a recitation by Miss Edith Cliffe, chief templar of Juvenile Branch, entitled." the flower girl ' fc.r which great credit was due. Several an- thems and glees were rendered by the choir. PROMOTION FOR INSPECTOR ~DOWTIt, R.S.P.C.A.—</p>
        <p>ENCOMIUMS BY THE MAGISTRATES. A case of cruelty to a horse was heard at the Warrington Police Court on Monday in which In- spector Dowty, of the R.S.P.C.A., appeared. After the case was decided, the magistrates' clerk in- formed the magistrates that the Inspector was leav- ing Warrington, and that this was his last appear- ance at the court. Mr. Monks presiding magistrate) said that the bench wished to express their ack- nowledgment of Inspector Dowty's kindness and courtesy, and of the way in which he had conducted his cases at that court. They were glad to hear that he was promoted to a higher position. They hoped that they would be as well served in the future as they had been during the time he had been in War- rington. Alderman Harrison fully concurred in the chairman's remarks. He did not think thin was any magistrate before whom Inspector Dowty had appeared more frequently than himself, and his conduct had been characterized by the greatest impartiality, and by the greatest humanity for the poor animals on whose behalf he had appeared. He richly deserved any promotion which could be given ti him. Inspector Dowty thanked the bench for their expressions. The Inspector has been promoted to the Birkenhead district and will shortly leave Warrington. SOAKING RIM PROOF OVERCOATS. yet porous. 555.. C. JACKSON HOLMES, General Outfitter. Market Gate, Warring- ton.—Adrt. THE POPITLA.R PENNY CONCERTS. Another of the Saturday night penny concerts promoted by the Warrington Social Questions Union was held on Saturday. The Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street was crowded with an appreciative audience. Mr. T. Birchen, J.P.t who occupied the ebair said he had spent the day in perhaps the most dismal town in Lancashire at the present time—St. Helens. The faces of the people really cut one up. It was pleasant to come- away to a more cheerful avnesphe -v. Much as Warrington was suffering, it was not suffering so badly as in those towns where there was a large number of colliers employed. He thought the Social Question .Union had done a wise thing in promoting these concerts. The more they could meet together for bright and cheerful pur- poses, the pleasanter life would be. He hoped they would all eecourage the Social questions Union to make the meetings successful. The programme for the evening was then proceeded with. Solos were rendered by Mr. and Ilfre. Lane, Miss Pemberton, Mr. Dell, Mr. Kerry, and the Chairman. Miss Furness exceedingly well reeited,and was rewarded with two . encores. Mr. Grounds also .gava two comic songs. Ths refreshment department which is a feature of the concerts was well managed by the ladies and their assistants. We understand ihat Dr.&amp; Smith has undertaken to provide the programme for the concert to be held to night (Sat- urday.) UMBRELLAS RE-COVERED.-1. and W. DUTTos, Sankey-at. Warrington.—Adrt. SANKEY SCHOOL BOARD. A meeting of this Board was held at the schools, 'Great Sankey, on Tuesday_ afternoon, 'when there were present—Major Ridgway (chairman), the Rev. E. Sandys Jackson. Mr. Wrighti Mr. George Clarke (headmaster), and Mr. Samuel Sutton (clerk).—The Chairman, referring to the recent examinations at the Schools, said he was not aware of them until they were over, or he should have been present to meet the inspectois. He should explain how the Mistake arose when he met them. The Clerk reported that there was a balance in hand of .£54 13s. 101. The payments during the month had been—on the 7th October.overseers' call, £BO ;10th, Mr. Clarke's feee, us. Sd. ; 25th, same account, 12s. 41. In answer to Major Ridgway the Clerk said the grants would be received next week.—Mr. Clarke reported upon the recent examinations, which he said, occupied two days. , The children had passed a satisfactory examination, and the in- spectors were pleased with everything they saw in the schools. They had no complaint to make.— The Rev. E. S. Jackson explained that he had seen Mr. Wm. Houghton who had been elected a mem- ber of the Board, 'but his business engagements would not allcw him to accept- the office.—The Chairman said he was exceedingly sorry to bear that Mr. Houghton declined to serve, as he would have made a useful member. The resignation was accepted, and itWas decided to appoint a successor at the next meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>AN EARTHQUAKE IN WALES.</head>
        <p>AN EARTHQUAKE IN WALES.</p>
        <p>DitlflCt stiocks of earthquake are reported to have been exper.enced on Thursday evening betwepn five and Six o'clock at various points along the Welsh coast. Buildings were shaken and furniture and other articles displaced. No injuries are recorded, but the inhabitants of the districts affected were greatly alarmed. At Car- ma rthen it is stated that there was a "distinct upheaval o t4e earth."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 4, 1893 WAR LtINGTON MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 4, 1893 WAR LtINGTON MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>THINGS HEARD AND SiI:EN AT</p>
        <p>ItIINCORN.</p>
        <p>BP AN OCCASIONAL CORRESPONDENT.</p>
        <p>That the country is not the elysium that some poets paint it. In our district of Cheshire, accord- ing to the report of the Medical Officer of Runcorn Rural Sanitary Authority it is at present the breed- ing place of fever. Scarlet fever, and typhoi4 fever has a bous6 in no fewer than eight townships.</p>
        <p>That it was an origins?, idea to repair a road at Weston Point, with the. •contents of an ashint, and the inventor ought to be severely punished for his originality. No better way could have been found of generating disease.</p>
        <p>That the evidence tendered at the Hospital In- quiry at Widnes, has been published and shows that the charges made against that excellent in- stitution have very small foundation in fact. As the old saying goes, some people would grumble to be hung. •</p>
        <p>That Alderman Gossage's first mayoralty Ivas been a most successful one, and it is the wish of all that the second year of office may have a similar happy ending.</p>
        <p>That the retiring Councillors were ail returned on Wednesday. The ratepayers evidently think twelve months is not sufficient• to afford a man an opportunity of displaying his metal.</p>
        <p>That the labour ticket was a little successful in Widnes as in most other municipalities- Why is it that labour candidates are far oftener at the bottom than at the top? There must be a lack of loyalty some w h ere.</p>
        <p>That Runcorn qommissioners will earn the grati- tude of the intelligent portion of -the community by deciding to erect the Technical. Institute: Future generations will rise up and call them blessed.</p>
        <p>That with the-Parish Councils Bill will come the ballot for Runcorn, and the division of the town in- to wards. The latter course was decided upon on Thursday. After a long discussion in which there was mueli beating about the bush.</p>
        <p>• ---- That Mr. Lightburn did not conceal his opinion. The ward system in his opinion is a gigantic evil. Wards will do one thins, as Mr. 'raison pointed out, they will prevent a candidate securing a seat under the wing of another candidate's popularity.</p>
        <p>had also donations promised, which made up a sum of £2,000 of a free gift to Iturreorn, if they only put them- selves in a position to receive it. The Committee had progressed step by step. By resolution the Board had adopted the Technical Instruction Act, a. site had been selected, an architect had prepared plans, and every- thing was now prepared for going on it the Board pleased. The architect, Mr. Bowden, Who was a specialist in this matter, had said when he (Mr. Wits- wall), gave him instructions to prepare plans that it was as much a secondary school as a Science and Art school. Mr. Bowden went to London to see the Science and Art Department and succeeded in designing a building and allotting rooms, upon which the fullest grants had been obtained. It was not ha reality a Tech. meal Institute, inasmuch as it was not a craft school— only big towns could do that. It was quite sufficient for the requirements of the town. Tlaey had obtained £4OO worth of models for £2OO, but they had ho proper accommodation for them. Including that expenditure, the total cost of the institution would be £4.400. To- wards this they had a grant of £l,OOO from the Science and Art Department, £440 from the Cheshire County Council, £520 from Mr. Brunner (which was almost Certain to be considerably increased), £5O from Mr. Speakman, ST.P., C.C., and £5O from the Rev. Canon Wood. ' Ile was sorry to say that the committee ap- pointed to obtain subscriptions had found that the pre- sent was not a favourable time bscauseof the stagnation of trade. It was anticipated that the County Council fund would by the end of 1894 be accumulated to £450. This would leave a balance of about £1,500 to be raised. The committee had every reafsen to believe that that sum would be forthcoming,and if it was they would have an income of £4OO for the maintenance of the building when estalidshed. Therelore there was no fear of the successful working of the institution if they only proceeded with the work.—Mr. Lightburvi seconded. He was delighted to find that the scheme was not lying dor- mant. They were agreed upon the importance that workingmen should be kept abreast of their foreign brethren, so that they might retain their position in the markets of the world. The interest taken iu scientific subjects was displayed by the crowded attendances at the Gilchrist lectirres.—Mr. Hankinson supported. The proposed institution would proves boon to Rune )rn, and he should be pleated t give his mite towards it. There was plenty of latent talent in the town, which only wanted facilities t) be brought out. He would further the institute to the utmost ,f his power.—Mr. Collier suggested that the committee might try to obtain sub- scriptions during the period over which the Gilchrist lectures extended, whilst the reople were in right vein. Mr. Wiswall remarked that the times were very bal. The motion was carried unanimously.</p>
        <p>Wednesday was an ideal first of November, that is to say, it was grim, and cold, and sloppy and wet. It was'alio an unpleasant day for those en- gaged in the hard hurrying work which the muni- cipal battle entailed. They who were compelled to " hunt" up the voters had indeed an unenviable task, and they might have been seen during the day tripping to and fro with dripping mackin- toshes and umbrellas; and muJ bespotted shoes. There was no particular excitement noticeable in any of the wards, but, as the poll shows, a good deal of unobtrusive work was done by the candi- dates and their workers in the way of bringing up voters: On the whole a considerable amount of mural and other literature had been issued during the contest and in some wards additional handbills were issued even on the day of election. In this connection it may be mentioned that several of the candidates issued their likenesses printed on cards to the electors of their respective wards, those of Mr. Pierpoint and Mr. Henry lieberts, which were issued from the " Examiner" Office, having been prepared from portraits taken by Mr. Dirties, of Sankey-at. Towards evening much more interest seemed to be taken in the result, and the polling went on steadily till the various booths were closed. DECLARATION OF THE POLL. •</p>
        <p>The polling began at the different hoots at eight o'clock in the morning, and was continued till eight o'clock at night, when the booths were closed. The ballot boxes were immediately conveyed to the Town Hall, where, in several rooms the counting was set about in the presence of the Returning Officer, the Town Clerk, the candidates, and others. There was evidently wide spread interest as to the result, for before 'nine o'clock many hundreds of persons had assembled in front of the Town nal( steps from which the announcements were made. The anxiously waited for 'results were announced at intervals, as soon as the votes given in the res- pective wards were counted. Tee results were received with a considerable amount of cheering mingled with hooting, and this latter feature was especially prominent when Alderman Francomb the returning officer for Howley ward announced that, as there had been a tie between Mr. A. Browne and Mr. A. Bennett he had given his casting vote to Mr. Bennet t. The following were the results of the poll :</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>WIDNES MUNICIPAL ELECTION.</head>
        <p>WIDNES MUNICIPAL ELECTION.</p>
        <p>- The second annual municipal elections in Widnes took place on Wednesday. There were contests in only three wards. Councillor T. Shuttleworth, Fern- worth, and Councillor W llodgkinson, West Bank, were returned unopposed, and Mr. David Wright, Halton was elected unopposed in place of Councillor Fred Neil, who retires because of an intention to reside at Southport. The contests were in Simms Cross, Victoria and Waterloo Wards, all in the centre of the town, but there was an absence of , excitement or bustle. The political parties took no I part in the election. The Mayor was the returning officer,and the returning officers for the wards were Waterloo, the Mayor, Victoria Alderman Tilley, Simms Cross, Alderman Sadler. The arrangements for polling were made by the Town Clerk,Mr.Oppenheim assisted by Mr. T. Craik and were of a complete charac- ter. For Simms Cross (888 burgeses) the polling to..utirs were in Matins Hall, the presiding officers being Mr. C. R. Lewin and Mr. T. J. Peters. In this ward the con- test was expected to be a close one as both Councillor S. Owens (L) and his opponent, Mr. A. G. Lowry (LAbour) the nominee of the Trades Council hal each prosecuted a vigorous canvass. In Victoria Ward, (881 burgesses) the polling booths were in LAcey-street schools and the presiding officers were Messrs. T. Craik and Mr. G. IL Danby. The candidates were Councillor H. Singleton (N) Mr. Michael Mannion •(Labour and Nationalist) and Mr. R. Ttbbutt, (I). In Waterloo Ward (812 burgesses) again a labour cannidate, Mr. Fred Hartland, opposed the return of the retiring member Councillor Thomas Carney. In this ward the Irish population is very large. The presiding officers were Messrs. J. W. Knowles and F.J. Poole. At 8 p.m. the ballot boxes were conveyed to the Town Hall, were the counting was expeditiously carried out by the presiding officers and their clerks. The result was declared' about nine o'clock as follows:— Simms Cross, Owens 414, Lowry 310. Victoria Single- ton 404, Tebbutt, 274, M. Mannion 82, Waterloo Ward, Carney, 369, Hartland 242. In every instance the retir- ing Councillor has been re-elected. A vote of thanks was accorded to the Mayor for his services during the election and the successful candidates came in. for hearty congratulati</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>RtNCORN BOARD OF COMMISSIONERS.</head>
        <p>RtNCORN BOARD OF COMMISSIONERS.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting was held at the Town Hall on Thursday morning; , Mr. S. Taylor presided, and there were present Messrs. Ir.WiswalL B. Imison, 11. J. Once, J.Meadowcroft, J.W. Lightburn, J. Dobson, Dr. Mangan, W. Handley, W. Collier D. Bisbrown, T. Lea, W. Hankinson, W. Leach, E. IV. Humphreys, R. Abel; V. A. Lake (law clerk), E. Marshall (sec- retary), Mr. Barker (surveyor). HOW 'Manus rEvER Is SPREAD. The Medical Officer reported that the number of deaths registered during the past month amounted to 33. Of these deaths 4 were due to heart disease, 3 to mesenteric disease, 5 to convulsions, 5 to enteric fever, 2 to whoop- ing cough, 2 to dropsy, 3 to pneumonia, and one each to phthisis peritonitis, inflammation of the lining inem- brane of the brain, premature birth, chronic kidney disease, and disease of uncertain origin. The 33 deaths showed an average death-rate for the twelve months of 213 per 1,000 of the estimated population.—Mr. Dobson said an impression prevailed amongst the working people of the town that typhoid fever was not an infec- tious disease and families in which the disease existed were visiting each other. Ile knew One instance where five persona had contracted the disease through visiting one house and there was no surprise that the disease had become epidemic. He asked the medical officer whether typhoid fever was infectious.—Dr. McDougall replied that it was very injudicious for one family to visit an- other family when there was disease of any kind exist- ing. Typhoid fever was not highly infectious but the disease was conveyed by the drink and food provided for the patient, because the persons in attendance did not take sufficient precautions in washing their hands and in disinfecting generally.—Mr. Dobsm suggested that instructions relative to the disease should be issued. —Mr. Imison said that had better be considered by the Health Committee.—Dr. McDougall pointed out that typhoid fever had been epidemic generally throughout the country. Runcorn had got a bad name' and he was sorry to say that some of the residents were disposed to give the town a bad name, but it was by no means as bad as some places.. SEMERAGH. Tho.llighWay and Building Committee recommended that pipe sewers be laid in Granville-street, aid Loch- Areet in place of the existing brick sewers as the pre- sent sewers are unfit to receive the contents at water closets which it is proposed to have substituted for toe privies at the rear of certain premises situated in these streets, and that application he made to the Local Government Board for their sanction to a loan sufficient to carry out the proposed sewerage works for 30 years; that the tender of Mr. F. T. 8e0ni..., Warrington, for work to be done in Byron-strait, Chaucer-street, Salis- bury-street, Picow-street and Picow Passages, be ac- cepted.—Mr. IViswall moved, and Mr. Lea eeconded the adoption of the minutes.—Mr. Imison'referring to thn proposed pipe sewers, said it had been found that in cer- tain parts of the town that the old brick st?wers allowei a certiin amount of liquid matter t..) escape, and tinlt probably some of the ills of the town hsiiffered from at the present time was owing to the defective condition of these sewers. He ventured to hope that this new policy of substituting pipe sewers for defective ones could soon put them face to face with a different condition of things as regarded sanitation. This expense to the Hoard would not he a heavy one if it were spread ovi r a period of 30 Nears, though it might put the owners to some little exPense to make the connections to their pro- perty, but they would derive benefit from improved sanitary conditions, and in other ways.—The minutes were adopted.</p>
        <p>WATER COIIMITTEE. The Committee recotnmended that the resoluti.lo No, 2432 be amended by altering the wage to be paid to Job Hancock from LI. 15.,t0 us. 61 per week, as the sum of El Is. referred to his services al: Il ' lncorn and the sum of 10.3.61. per week is for his work at the fialttm Pumping Station ; that the following accounts be Fad nut of water loan accour.t for services rendered in connt c- tion with the purchase &amp;e. of the Runcorn. Weston, and Halton Waterworks Underts.king t—Mr. J. L. 'reason. Accountant, £l3l 7s. 91. ; Mr.J. Newton, Engineer, £54. 103.; Mr. J. R Frith, V.tluer,l:l9 83. The minutes were adopted on the Motion of Mr. Meadoweroft, WllO in re- ply to Mr. Lea said a defect had been found in the boiler at Halton. and the Committte were in correspon- dence with the Waterworks Committee on the matter.</p>
        <p>THE TECHNICAL INSTITU rE. The Technical Instruction Committee recommended that tenders tri invited by advertisement for the erec- tion of a technical institute.—Mr. Wiswnll moved the adeption of the minutes. Ile ead the Committee had not truuhled the Board much recently with this ques- tion, but they thought the time had now arrived to pro- ceed with the work otherwise ttie grant of £I,OOO (nun the Science and Art DApartmenr. and £440 from the County Council would be jeopardised.' The Committee</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>INSLEITARY PROPERTY</head>
        <p>INSLEITARY PROPERTY</p>
        <p>The Medical Officer of Health had reported to the Health Committee that the houses known as Zion Plac,-, and numbend 1 to 13 inclusive are in a watt so danger- ous or injurious to health as to be unfit for human habit- ation it is recommended that steps be taken for the closing of the same under the provisions of the Housing , of the_ Working Classes Act, 1890, and notice served on the owners requiring them to abate the nuisances on the said premises within one month by executing such works and doing such things as may be necn.isa^y for that purpose, viz :—by closing the said houses, by pro- viding proper drainage for Zmn Place. by removing the sewage saturated soil and cleaning out the cesspool and removing the existing astipits and privies.—The minute was approved on the motion of Mr. Iminn. THE WARDS QUESTION'.</p>
        <p>Mr. Handley moved that the Board make application to the Cheshire County.; Council asking them to take such steps as may be necessary for the division of the Urban Sanitary District of Runcorn into Ward;. He also submitted a scheme which he had proposed of which the following is an outline : —Halton,raal ward ; on the south side of the Bridgewater Canal, bounded by the canal and the Big Pool, with 414 elec- tors. Mersey ward : Lying between the said canal and the river Mersey to the eastward of and including Pen- keth-lan: and Mersey-street, 632 electors. Central ward lying between the Mersey ward and Waterloo-road. bounded by the Bridgewa!er Cenal and th 3 river Mersey 745 electors. Bridgewater ward : To the westward of the Central ward known as Duke'ts-1141, and including Waterloo-road, 660 electors. Greenway ward: Lyinz on the south side of the Bridgewater Canal and docks. bounded by and including Shaw-street and Cavendish. street, 634 electors ; and Higher Runcora Ward : All Runcorn . south lying above and not in- cluded in the Greenway ward, 352 electors, Mr. Hartley drew attention to th 2 advantages which would accrue frm such a division. At present only 25 Per cent, of the voters polled, it hich was far from satis- factory, and in the interests of good government greater facilities ought to be given for according votes. In order to keep abreast with the neighbouring towns the &amp; vi- sion should be made.—Mr. Imison seconded. Runcorn seemed to be behind everybody else. Ha argued that under the ward system, a member who ha I become antagonistic to the wishes of the ratepayers could be prevented from continuing to represent them, whereas at present he could be returned wider the wing of four or five other candidates. Be contended that under the ward system, a member could better study the sanitary arrangements of his district and thus become a tn2re efficient representative.—Mr. Lightburn thought the ward principle both theoretically and nractica'ly had. It would rob the ratepayer :,f one fifth of his voting power. lie argued that it %you'd be introducing a gi-o.t..t'c evi,. ll'rarmased that the subject be referred to C committee. —Mr. Wiawall seconded. It was the meth.) lof voting which kep peop!e 'ro:n recording rhe'r V t t not the watt ot the ward :ystrrn.—Mr. Grice agreed with this sentiment as did Mr.C(.llir.—Mr.Elandley admitted that enter the present 11,A.Nia of voting, war I. would be ugelesi, but he did not intend the resolution to take effect until the passing of the Parish Councils Bill when the ballot would be iatroducerl After a somewhat lively discussion in whi lm several amendments were disposed of, the following amend- ment made by Mr. Humphreys was passed by 8 votr:s to 7 :—" That this Board being of opinion that the division of the Runcorn Urban Sanitary District into wards would greatly facilitate the recording of votes in parliamentary and local elections, and would be further instrumental in securing increased efficiency in the conducting of local affairs,hereby instructs the General Purposes and Parlia- mentary Committees, with Mr.Handley's name added, to prepare and present to the next meeting of the Board a draft application to the Cheshire County Council, to exercise the powers conferred by Section 57 of the faecal Government Act of 1838, and institute an inquire as to the advisability of such division, and to make such order , thereon as may be deemed fit; such not to come iato force until the passing of the District Councils Act."</p>
        <p>PRIVATE STREETS. The Secretary read a letter from the Local GorPrn- ment Board, sanctioning the borrowicg of £10,600 for private street works for a period of ten years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>VaRRINGTON 10311 AL SANITA.RY</head>
        <p>VaRRINGTON 10311 AL SANITA.RY</p>
        <p>AUTHORITY.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of this Authority was held yesterday •at the Board room of the Workhouse, when there were present, Rev. G. F. Roberti (chairman), Messrs. Dignum, it. Heaton, A. Jolley, J. Houghton, %V. Houghton, S. Sutton (clerk), W. Owen (surveyor), Dr. Sephton (medical offi.ter),and W. Kerfoot (nuisance inspect)r).</p>
        <p>-•- - - - THE PARISH COUNCILS BILL. The Clerk reported that he in company with tlv. chair- man (liev. (1. F. Roberts), and Messrs. Dignuni, Wi:liam Houghton, and Owen went 0 Manchesti r on Tuesday week to represent the 11,arrington Rural Sanitary Authority, at a committee formed of the various S.tni- tau, Authorities of Lancashire, 0 discuss amendments to the Parish Councils Bill. At the 'fleeting. a sub- committee WAS formed, consisting of one member for each Sanitary Authority, and the dell( of sucli author- ity. The sub-committee met on Frit.ay at Preston, and discussed amendments, and proposed further am,nd- ments, which would be put before Ws general com- mittee that afternoon, at Preston.</p>
        <p>THE 'WERTH DRAINAGE SCHEME. With regard to the Penketh drains, at Whittle tiro( k. Mr. Owen reported that as instructed by the Authority, Ito wrote t) settle the payment for wayleaves with Mr. Campbell, of St. Helens, agent for Captain Hughes for the sum of £lO. Mr. Campbell wrote to say that he ha I ; mistaken his letter, as he had asked £lOO, and not £lO, and that he could not, recommend Captain Hughes to , accept the £lOO if tenants compensation was included. The Street Committee had decided to leave the matter in the hands of the Authority. Ile (Mr. °Nen) reported , that the demand was unreasonable. The sewcr weu'd run 220 yards through Captain Hughes land ne ir to the brook where the land is l.w lying. —I.; was decided to leave the matter over until next spring. TEE MONTHLY MEDICAL REPORT. Dr. Sephton reported that for the month ending Oct. 31st, the number of births was 19, deaths 19. The death rate from all causes Was equal to 17 per 1,000. against 22.8 for the corresponding month last year. The doctor then report..d upon the cases of infectious dist ate, and la:commended that notices be served to repair or close it (mintier of dilipidated houses unfit for human habita- tion. In one ease, they ought not only to close the houscs, but pull them clown.</p>
        <p>THE BUILDING TRADE AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>AGITATION FOR INCREASED WAGES. It, is understood that the various sections of work- men in the building trades Ibf St. Helens are again agitating for an increase of wages. A letter has been sent to the masters of the brick-layers, giving six months' notice that, on and after the Ist. day of 1894, they shall require an advance upon the present rate of wages. The CA rpeOers and joiners of St. hi-tens and district have also forwarded a copy of pro- posed new working rules and regulatimis to he observed between them and the emplovers. They will come int") operation on the Ist of May, 1894. One of these rules stipulates that wages shall be paid by the hour, at the average rate of 9d. per hour, :all the year round. At present the joiners receive 84 i.ez hour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>TOWN HALL WARD.—(I,OSB Voters).</head>
