        <p>Majr,rity</p>
        <p>PRESIDING OFFICERS AND POLL CLERKS. • The following is a list of the presiding officers and poll clerks engaged at the various polling booths. Town Hall Ward: Messrs. C. J. Holmes, and E. A. While, presiding officers ; and Messrs. H. Barker, and S. 13. Chandley, poll clerics. White- cross Ward Messrs. W. 1:2. !toward, and W. J. For- shay', presiding officers, and Messrs. P. L. Speakman. end R. Denham, poll clerics. Bewsey Ward : Messrs. C. H. Unsworth, and F. It. Roberts, presiding officers, and Messrs. 3. Wellhead, and T. 11. Grain- ger, poll clerks. Orford Ward: Messrs. J. It. Wil- liams, and H. Woods, presiding officers, and Messrs. : A. W. Howard, and W. Boyle poll clerks. St. John's Ward: Messrs. G. T. Moore, T. Lythgoe, and J. W. Whittle, presiding officers, and Messrs. A. Boyle, W. T. Flood, and T. O. Grounds, poll clerks. Fairfield Ward : Messrs. E. J. Hall, and J. Longlanci, presiding officers, and Messrs. 'P. Westbrook, and F. 'limper- ley, poll clerks. Howley Ward: Messrs. John Har- rison, and James Bolton, presiding officers, and Messrs. E. J. Smith, and G. Vernon, poll clerks. Latchford Ward: Messrs. T. Cooper, and J. tiutcliffe, presiding officers, and Messrs. 11. Longdin and T. S. Steele, poll clerks.</p>
        <p>high position in the town,who in fact were looked upon as I Christian gentlemen. fie saw amongst those names that of Moßobt.Davies. (Derisive laughter.) He (the speaker) was not a teetotaller, but he did disagree with all his heart against this invasion of the Council of representa- tives of the drink traffic. Be as a magistrate was fully alive to the dreadful state of the town ; he was aware that three parts of the cases that genie before them at ;the Borough Court were those in which were concerned • the publicans' customers. And did it not strike them all that it was impossible for the strict law to be carried out, in relation to public-houses, when the proprietors of such places had to do with the administration. ("No.") Before concluding, he wished to allude to the gallant fight made in St. John's ward. Ho never remembered in his whole life such a magnificent fight made by a I young and new cnadidate, as Mr. Furness had made , against Councillor Shaw. (Applause.) It was a eplen- , did fight, and there was hope for the gallant candidate I I yet. (Loud appleeuse.) Mr. WM. Pritarmere, who was greeted with loud ap- plause, said that was not the first time that he had ap- peared in that room as a defeated candidate. (Cries of " Come to Bowsey.") But he never allowed a defeat to eause him to lose heart. (Applause.) Ho was not built that. way. (Hear, hear.) When he got hit by an op- ponent, he always tried to return the blow with one of much greater force. (Renewed applause.) He did not think that his defeat was either a disgrace to himself or to the Liberal party. ("No, no.") What he would like to say was that during that day King Grog had reigned' supreme ; and King Grog had every reason to be proud of what it had done. But Warrington had also reason to feel the odium and contumely of its -disgrace in being so fallen. (Applause.) King Grog had apparently made the first step tewards capturing the tewn,and the town would consequently render itself remarkably small in the eyes of other pieces. If the Tory leaders of the towu,from the Rector of Warrington to Mr. John White, were satisfied with their proceedings that day—well he ' would leave it to their consciences. (Hear, hear.) He heartily thanked those workers who had helped in his cause, but he also wished to express his regret that cer- tain gentlemen allied with the party who lived just out of the town, had not in any way bestirred eliemselves in the work of the day. (" Shaine.") Before concluding, he expressed Ins sincere regret that so able and trust- worthy a man as Mr. Fleming should have been dis- placed by a public:en an I an ex-policeman. He considered it was a disgrace to a ward which they had always looked moon as the senior ward, composed of respects ble trades- mereto have submitted teemselves to Bitch an Indignity. (Loud applause.) Mr. J. fl. Femmes, who was received will' vocifer- ous applause, had nothing to say except that what had been his rejection would he the town's loss—for he should now employ the time he had devoted to the town's work to his own interests. He heartily thanked those who had worked for his return. Councillor Strew GREEN who next addressed the meet- ing spoke vehemently against the methods to which the Tories had resorted—not only in tOs own, but other wards—to secure votes. He had seen things done that day that were utterly disgraceful to their opponents. They had iseued the statement " Bewsey ward can be bought," and with what he had seen, together with the lessening of his majority there was one construction which they could put upon the procedure that had been adopted by their opponents. (Loud applause.) The rinks of their party in the Council had been thinned,but nevertheless, they who still remained were true, and staunch and determined, and they should stand up for their cause with all the vigour and energy they were capable of. (Loud applause.) In concluding the speaker heartily thanked all those workers who had borne the burden and heat of his caneass. (Applause.) Mr. A. BaowNe,who was received with loud applause, in alluding to the tight contest he had fought,complained that had all who pledged for IliTIl in the canvass proved true to their Ward, he should have been in by a maprity of 30. But there was another thing. The presiding alderman of the ward, contrary to all custom, bad given in his casting vote, not to the former councillor, but to the new candidate. (Hisses and " name.") But he had only kept rip his traditional misuse of the casting votes. ("Yee," and hisses.) floweverem was consider- ing whether he should not insist upon serutiny. (Mar, lea%) He believed, a marched wom in had voted. (1 is e hear.) Ife intended they should hear of that aid some- thing else, besides. (Applause.) Mr. J. MOUNFIEID next addressed the meeting, and referred to the slanderous statements that had been made against him by the canvassers of his opponents. The meeting then broke up.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>MEETING AT THE LIBERAL CLUB.</head>
        <p>MEETING AT THE LIBERAL CLUB.</p>
        <p>After the declaration of the poll, a large body of Liberals made their way to the Liberal Club, in Bewsey- st., when an informal meeting was held. Mr. T. Birchall presided, and there were present Councillor H. Roberts, Councillor Shaw• Green. Mr. Wm. Pierpoint, Mr. J. H. Fleming, Mr. J. Mounfield, Mr. A. Browne, and others. Councillor IL Houma's, who addressed the meeting first, expressed at the outset a strong regret that the Cnairman of their Executive Committee had been un- successful in his candidature—they all regretted. it, be- cause Mr. Pierpoint was a man of whom they were justly proud. (Hear, hear.) He could not understand his defeat ; there was something in it which required in- vestigation. (" There is."). When that investigation had been made there would probably be revealed a rea- son not creditable to some of the electors of that ward, nor to the opposing party. (Hear, hear.) With respect to his own rand dature, they knew he had been suc- cessful, and for this he thanked most sincerely the woikers. Now, they knew that it had been said that certain of their party who had strong views were de- trimental to the interest of the party. He a-dted them to) look at the results of the election and Fee. (Applause.) There was no doubt about it, the time had now arrive when a dividing line ought and must be drawn for the credit of Liberalism. (Hear, hear.) As they knew it was not the Tory party they had been fighting. e" No.") They had been fighting the Tory allies, the drink trade. (" Shaine.") They had been fighting in every ward an organisation who had no interest in the welfare ef the town, an organisatioa indeed, whr se only sympathy with the woraingmen was invested in a desire to appropriate as much of his weekly wage as possible (" Shame ") I; was this influence that they would have to fight in future with all their power. (Ili 3,r hear). With respect td Councillor Fleming's defeat, the speaker said ha felt the utmost regret, fo- . as they all knew, Councillor Fleming had devoted emoiderable time and energy and labour to carrying nit betiefically the interests of the borough. (Applause.) But what had theyjgot in his place ? (" What ? ' and " Shame ") They were sending up a representative of the beer trade—a publican. (" Shatw. ') It was a fact the Lib .ral Party were not expected .to look over the hedge, but the Tory Party were allowed to teil a cow. (Hear. (ear.) It was getting time this shilly-shallying was done away with—it was getting time they came out a combined p Lay to fight the adverse combinations against them. (Hear, hear.) They would be surprised when he told tern that rule of the Liberal Alderman—the presiding Alderman of Whiteoposs Ward, who was a Liberal had , not condescended to record his vote for him(the speaker) that election. He did not intend to conceal anything from them.' Alderman Holmes, he believed, had taken this step because he disagreed with him (the speaker) in trying ta compel the Gas Committee to publish prices of tenders which they accepted. (Hear, hear.) Alderman Holmes was chairman of the Committee hut he could tell them that no matter who opposed him, if he had strength and health he should compel the publication of those tenders. (Loud applause.) Before concluding he wished to mention that he had received the support of a number of Conservatives who refused to be ruled by the institu- ti m in Sankey-street. (Bear, hear.) Be again beartilv thanked the officers and workers of his ward. BIRCHALL next addressed the tneeting. Ile said he agreed with Mr. Roberts that the time had now ar,ived when a dividing live should be drawn, when all true tnen should be brought closely together and the weak-kneed ones allowed to go their way. (Rear, hear.) The Tory party had dragged the drinli traffic through thick and thin, in a way that was an infamous disgrace to any political rarty. (Hear, hear.) fie was exceed- , ineely sorry to see that in the Town Hall ward Mr. Flem- ing had been deposed l.a the landlord of he Bay Horse I Inn—that his p!ace as an old and tried and useful ' servant of the council had been filled by a publican. (" Shaine.") Ile was also sorry tl see that Mr. Hem- jug's opponent had been prlposel by people who held a</p>
        <p>WEEKLY REPO HT ON THE HEM TII OF WARRINGTON. During the week, which Was the 43rd of the current year, the following births and deaths were registered : ‘Varrington, '25 ; Latchford, 2; t0?a1.07. Deaths: 'Warrington,l3 ; Latchford, 3 ; totitllo. The excet.s. of births over deaths was 11. The death-rate from all causes was equal to 151 per 1,000, nainst 22.5 the average of the previon4 tour weeks, and against 221 the correspottling week tt.f last year. Inn 'detail-rate from the seven chief zymotic diseases was equal to 41 per 1.000, agatnst &amp;I the average rate of the four previous weeks, and against 8.6 the corresponding week of 1892. The ages of mortality were to 1, 4 ; to 6,5 ;5 rn 15.0 ; 15 to 25, 0 • 25 io 60, 5 :60 up 2; total, Id. The deaths included-1 from enteric fever ;1 from English cholera ; trom scarlet fever; I from looping cough ; 2 from phtlitsis; 2 'mon old age ; 4 from lug disease ; I from heart disease ; 1 from brain disease. The following cases of infectious disease have been reported dur- ing the week:—scarlet fever 9: enteric fever, I. Meteorology :—The mean temmirature was on ee the generalm 2e3trd re direction 401 Fah. and therefore 18 deg. colder than the preceding week. The maximum tempera- ture 63 F., was recorded on the 22m: inst., and the minimum temperature 410 rint. .tbi nst . Thewasrainfalle N .. t nire reading .7.5 f e. denoting 1,220 miles.—JouN IL GORNALL, bledicel Officer o Health.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <p>t</p>
        <p>MEE fIN.6/ AT THE CONSERVATIVE CLUB.</p>
        <p>An enthusiastic meeting was held at the Conservative Club, after the declaration of the poll. Mr. John White , took the chair,and after reading the results of the various' wards, he said that the ciection had come up to the most sanguine expectations of the Conservatives. He pon- eiatulated and condoled with the cewly elected Coun- cillors and defeated candidates, and expressed the ut- most satisfaction that they had won Orford ward, and defeated the Chairmarrof the Liberal Association.—Mr. Robeit Pierpeint M.P. next addressed the meeting in one of those flowery speeches of his, without any particular' point in them. He congratulated the party on their suceess, and said that the Conservative party was not a party of class,but when they met together it was always " hail fellow well 'net." He considered that this was one of the causes of their success in Warrington.—Mr. H. T. Smith who neat addressed the meeting was In a very bellicose mood. Ile announced his intention of " trying again," and took great credit to himself for keeping Mr. Roberts to his own corner, and thus pre- venting him from assisting the other Liberal candidates. "This." said he, " was no doubt a great pull on the Libel als."—Mr.liaird then delivered a tuners al oration in which he thanked his workers for the work they had done for him that day, and thereby reducing the major- ity. Should occasion arise, and he were anproached, lie might consent to fight again. The successful clndidates next addressed the ineeting.—Mr. Shire ick seemed to be already thinking about t lime years t come for he annonne.ed his intention of fighting again in three years time but he perhaps meant this as a " goak."—Mr. Skelton considered that lie had gained the greatest vic- tory of the lot,as he had converted a minority of 115 two a majority of 72.—Mr. Lengton who was called upon as Councillor Longton appeared to be quite jubilant at this title, and rang the charges upon it all through his speech. —Councillor Shaw considered his maj 44 of 25 a good one after the " hard studious " 11;41it he had had.—Coun- cillor Bennett was almost ashamed of his stnall majority after hearing the large majorities of the other can- didates. However, lie supposed that it was large enough to last three years. 'Other speakers subsequently ad- dresed the meeting which broke up after a vote of thanks had been proposed to Mr. J. White for presiding.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>TH IS GS HEARD AND SEEN</head>
        <p>TH IS GS HEARD AND SEEN</p>
        <p>AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>There is, in my opinion, one lesson which ia taught by the municipal elections in Warrington, and that is, that compromises with the Tory party are a mistake. If the Liberals had fought last year, it was almost certain that they would. have- won one or two seats. They had then six Tory seats to attack and only three of their own to de- fend, but, out of consideration for the public good, they compromised, and the six Tories were allowed to return to the Council without, even being put to the trouble of soliciting a single vote. This year the tables were turned; it was the Liberals who had the six seats to defend and—well, we all know how the Tories reciprocated.</p>
        <p>Another lesson ig, that it is hard and persistent fighting that wins municipal just as it wins other battles. It was a mistake to suppose that the Tories would have any regard for the prevailing distress in the town, when they thought they !Ad a chance of increasing their majority in the Town Council, and through this mistake several of the Liberal candidates were unduly late in taking the field in real earnest. In Whitecross ward, where Councillor Roberts declined to be caught napping, the large majority by which he was returned three years ago has practically been kept intact, in spite of the desperate and not very scrupulous efforts of the Tories to bring about a change. In St. John's, ward, too, Mr. Furness fought his first battle wits an energy and" go" that may be expected to ensure victory on a second attenspt.</p>
        <p>Alderman Francomb's conduct as returning officer for Howley Ward in giving his casting vote against the old member, Mr. Browne, shows how thoroughly imbued with the narrowest party spirit the Tories are. I believe it is the almost universal practice when two candidates receive an equal number of votes, as in this case, for the returning officer to give the old member the benefit of the casting rote. As if to illustrate this practice there happened to be, on the very same day, a similar tfe at St. Helens. The retiring member there was.a Conservative, and his opponent a Liberal. It fell to the lot of the Mayor, Mr. Sinclair, a thorough gsi- ing Liberal to give the casting vote, mad how till he act? Did he, as Alderman Francomb did,. at Warrington. give the candidate of his own way of thinking in 'politics, the benefit of his deciding vote? No; he acted according to precedent, and followed the graceful custom which gives the re- tiring member the benefit of the doubt, and the Conservative was declared to be the successful can- didate.</p>
        <p>I notice from a Manchester ptper that the emin, ent musician, Dr. Hiles, who is, of course, well known in Wairington has been winning honours in London lately. An overture of his composition; entitled" Youth," Ime been rendered at the Crystal Palace, and wo are told, that "it was most enthu. elastically received by the audience and Dr. Jules was warmly congratula2ed upon all sides upon tha success his overture had met with upon its first popular performance."</p>
        <p>The Rev. John Yongeachieted quite a distinction on Sunday. He took as the lines of his discourse. in the evening, the contrasts of luxury and poverty in the town, and his words 80 mored one of hi 4 con- gregation,that he offered to pay the cost of publish- ing the whole in pamphlet form. This ie a striking; exception t the rule, earnest sermons have the re- , putation of having a rather saporilic effect up,n. the hearos. Mr. Youge's sermon was the reverse of this, being remarkably earnest and eloquent,and as it will very shortly be published, the public generally will have an opportunity of perusing it. MERLIN. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>SUPPOSED SUICIDE OF A WARRINGTON</head>
        <p>SUPPOSED SUICIDE OF A WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>WOMAN AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>On Thursday night two labourers named Bristow and Yates, while passing along the promenade near the Mersey hotel. Widnes, saw some boys kicking a woman's ulster and bonnet. On examining the articles, a letter was found attached to the bonnet. The espistle WAS of a rambling nature and the - writer implored God's forgiveness for the wrong she contemplated. The police have since ascer- tained that the clothing belonged to Miss Anderton, aged 48, housekeeper to a gentleman at Latchford, Warrington. The handwriting of the letter has also been identified. It appears that Miss Andeiton visited her sister, who lives in Violet-street, Widnes, on Tuesday and left on Wednesday morning, stat- ing that 8170 WAS returning to Warrington. It is oelieyed that she has committed suicide.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>WARRINGTON MUSEUM.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON MUSEUM.</p>
        <p>BRITISH BIRD'S EGG.</p>
        <p>The delicate colours of the eggs of birds ars well- • known to be very fugitive under the influence of sunlight, and in order to preserve them it is neces- sary to keep them n the dark. In consequence of this the two collections of eggs recently added to the Museum, one of more than 700 specimens pre- sented by Mr. henry Roberts, J.P., and the others acquired by purchase, have not hitherto been ex- hibited to the public. As a temporary arrangetient, . ' however, they are now laid out in table cases in the Bird-room, and protected from the light by covers which can be readily removed and replaced by the visitor. Eventually the collection will be placed in a cabinet of drawers so arranged, that the visitor can open and close them with perfect ease to • himself and security to the specimens. It will be found that the Museum possesses at present one or more sped. • rnens of the eggs of 162 British species of birds, includ- ing spine interesting varieties, which collectors will ' readily recognise. This does not form a complete col- lection, even as to the birds which breed in this country, and the Museum Committee will, no doubt,welcome any donations which will help in th4t direction.</p>
        <p>MASUONAL AND. Apropos of one of the questions of the day, the show case in the Lending Department, which is usually de- voted to recent additions, has been re-lahelled " South Africa " and exhibits a selection from the shelves Pf books relating to Mashonaland and the adjacent coun- tries, with a few works on the expansion of England and kindred subjects. ‘. -4- RUNCORN MUSICAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The dna public appearance of this Society was ' made on Monday evening, when a ballad concert was given in Runcorn Public Hall. There was a numerous audience. The chorus which numbered about 70 voices had not an ar !ti ma task set them. Their contribution eonsisted of three part songs, " Rest Soldier rest," " The sea bath its pearls," and Gipsy life," and directed by the baton of Mr. F. W. Crossley. the conductor, they gave a capital rendition of each item. The principals were Madame ranny Mondy, soprano, and Mr. Charles 31anners. bass. who have made name and fame in Royal Italian Opera, and Miss Lily Moody, contralto, who has also gained laurels in opera. Madame Moody was in excellent voice, and her bright and ringing tones at times enthralled her auditors. Her ex- quisite vocalisation of the "Jewel song," from Gounedts " Faust," secured her a vociferous encore, ard a like re- ' stiV followed her singing nf " Ave Maria," and the vats, song " Poor Wandering One," from " Pirates of Pen• - ' ance." In response to the last recall, Madame Mo•dy gave" Coming thro' the rye," with charming vivacity. Miss moody's c mtributions were" Angus Macdonald," " The land of yesterday," and " Ronald flay," and in e.teh her sympathetic voice was heard to advantage. In rostannse to an encore for the 13qt, named she sang " Jock of Hazeidene." To avc f rich and Pleasing quality, Mr. Manners has aildetl Vie gill of clear enunclation and- alminibll expression, and he was enc./al-eel for each of his principav cam hi ned o ,•ff..rts, namely "Sac alone charmeth," " In Cellar Cool,"- and the new humorous sonic " six husbands." He ac- knowiedzed the last recall by an enjayable rendering. o it f' ic 'Ather O'rlytm." The t sing the trio" Crows in h cornfield," whh was given with hiAl humlrnits effect+, and the audience y • persieted in a repiti- tinn. Reynolds, besides performing the onerous part of accompanist, Oared two solos on the pianoforte A Steinway grand), which were listened to with ?noel] appreciati n, and Mr. Crossley showed t.trte and ability in two performances on the vi,lin. Mrs. W. B. Kay ac- companist to the society, accompanied the chorus in h.: customary able mariner. A highly enjoyable and suc- cessful concert, was brought to a c.onclu.i.ion by thesin log of the National Anthem . The next concert will con- sist of Handel's great work "Samson."</p>
        <p>No other pianist of the century hag bad Fitch ovations l'a,derwfki,the receipts of hl4 c.oneerta having reached a record point , smnewhere about £1,200 being realised for two hours' performance. Cepttma.rians are altnoFt incariably lean people, of spare habit aml of great moderation in est;ng and drinking. Of 37 investigate.d, .tureo,.tcgek. no 4nimat cg food, four to. very little, '2O a little, trwa mndtra amount, and only one acknowited takin much. t</p>
