      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>COMMON LOT.</head>
        <p>THE COMMON LOT.</p>
        <p>However diffarently situated as regards social posi- tion, or separated as rem&amp; location, it is the common lot of all sorts and conditions of men to be liable to various ailments and diseases. All men under these Circumstances are, to a certain extent, placed on the same footing. - - -</p>
        <p>• "A fellow-feeling, makes us wondrous kind," and nothing interests a sick man more than to hear the experience of others who have suffered from the same Complaint as himself. Now, though the names of vari- ous forms of disease are legion, in this country people are subject, more or less, to one of the following form ofailraent.</p>
        <p>1. Disease of the Digestive organs, such as Indigestion, Flatulence, Headaches, Dyspepsia, Heartburn, _ . .</p>
        <p>Drowsiness, Palpita ._t ion; Sm. 2. Diseises of Liver, such as Billiousness, Jaundice, Depressed Spirits, Impure Blood, Melancholy. &amp;c. 3. Nervous Diseases, such as Fainting Fits, Near tlgia, Despondency, Nervousness, Nerve Pains, General</p>
        <p>Weakness, kc. 4. Diseases ot the Respiratory Organs, such as Com- mon Colds, Asthma, Bronchitis, Spitting of Blocd, Shortness of Breath, kc. Strictly speaking, all these ailments have a common •• •</p>
        <p>- Clergymen, prcfessional men, merchants, and others not inured to hardships, are especially liable to attacks, and we cannot give such better advice thsn to have recourse at once to a course of GWILYM EVANS' QUININE BITTERS. This excellent t mic remedy is absolutely unrivalled and the universal testimony of all who have given It a fair trial is, that, for those who suffer from any form of disease which tends to weaken the bodilv powers in any degree, the best possible thing is to take a c..urse of GIVILYM Evess' QUININE BITTERS.</p>
        <p>Gar LOOK Our Fort -COUNTHRFEITS.—As " Gmilym Evans' Quinine Bitters" have become so popular, and has gained so great a reputation as a specific for the above disorders, numerous imitations of it are being substituted and offered t 3 the public. Every- purchaser should see that the name " Gwilym Evans' is on label, stamp, and bottle.</p>
        <p>Sold by all chemists, and Patent Medicine Vendors in ls. lid., 2s. 9d., and 4s. 6d. bottles, or free by parcels post for the above ptices direct from the Proprietors. QUININE BITTERS MANUFACTURING COMPANY. LIMITED, LLANELLT, SOUTH WALES. American Depot—Mr. R. D. Williams, Plymouth, Pa.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable (Mr. Hunt) stated that the woman I went inte the shop of Mr. Longshaw's a perfect stranger. She told them that her husband lay dead at the Infirmary, and that in a few days she would be in possession of £25, and said she wanted some mourning. Gltirnatply they allowed her to take articles of the value ot 505., and she went away with them. About a week afterwards, as the woman did not appear, inquiries were made at the Infirmary, and it was found that no one had died there of the name given, and the address given was found to be wrong. The police were informed, and D.S. Monaghan made enquiries, which led him to go to another place where the prisoner had attempted to obtain goods on the same pretences. D.S. Monaghan went to Earles- town and took with him some patterns of the materials the prisoner bad obtained at Mr. Longshaw's, and visited a pawnshop there, and found that linen from Mr. Long- shaw's had been made up into aprons, and also several other articles were found in the shop. D.S. Monaghan met the prisoner in Earlestewn and took her to the !Aim station, and the other articles stolen were found at the prisoner's house. The prisoner was sent b jail , for three months hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>RI.3I.&amp;NDED ON SUSPICION'.</p>
        <p>Richard William Hicks, pleaded guilty at tho War- rington Police Court on Saturday to the charge of being drunk and disorderly on the previous evening. Inspec- tor Spinks applied for a remand owing to a large juantity of pawn tickets being found in his pockets re- lating to watches. The prisoner was remanded until Tuesday, bail being granted. He gave his address as 31, West Derby-st., Liverpool. Prisoner was again brought up before the magistrates en Tuesday and dis- missed, as it was found that he had been a traveller for a jewelry firm in London, and had the promise of his situation again. THE THEFT OF COAL Fl2Oll THE CANAL. . .</p>
        <p>Wm. Pagan, 10, Admiral-st.,Warringtoo, was brought up on remand at Daresbury Petty Sessions, on Tuesday, charged with having on the 18th October, at Appleton, stolen a lump of coal, value 51. the property of John Jackson, contractor for the Manchester Ship Canal. Mr. Wm. Fletcher, sub-agent for Mr. Jackson, said the prisoner was in their employ discharging coal. The coal prodac,ed would ha worth about 6i. Mr. Jackson did not wish to press the case. P.C. Cooper. Warring- ton, said he was at Wilderspool, when he saw the pri- soner coming towards him. He saw him lay something down, and he took the prisoner to the place, where he found the lump of coal wrapped up in a cardigan jacket. The prisoner admitted having stolen it from the Ship Canal. Be (witness) handed him over to P.C. Brew. The prisoner said the coal was not the kind of coal he was discharging, and that he picked it up on the way tome. It was a piece that had fallen off une of the waggons. As the prisoner had been in jail 13 days already on the charge and he was dismissed. IN SEARCH OF GAME AGAIN. —HEAVY PENALTY..</p>
        <p>-- At Daresbury Petty Sessions,on Tuesday, Wm. Taylor, 25, Frederick-st., Latchford, a ganger on the Ship Canal, was charged with having on the 2nd of August, at Hat- ton, been on land in the possession of Henry ‘Vhitlow, in search of game, without the license of the owner. Mr. Unsworth, solicitor, Warrington, prosecuted. The defendant did not appear, but sent his wife, who ap- peared for him at the last sessions to-answer a similar charge. Mr. Unsworth said Peter Lockett, gamekeeper, in the employ of Sir Gilbert Greenall was on some fields belonging to Mr. Whitlow when he came across the de- fendant and another man, who were accompanied by a lurcher dog, at almost one o'clock in the day time. The keeper asked the defendant for his name and address, but he refused to give it. Lockett didn't attempt to apprehend him, but it was afterwards found out that be WM the same man who was fined for a similar offence at the last Daresbury Sessions. Peter Lockett said on the defendant seeing him, he attempted to run back. On being questioned the man commenced using very abusive language. He was about 300 yards from the highway at the time. Mr. Bailey. deposed 0 seeing the defendant at Whitley on the date named. The , magis- trates fined the defendant 403. and costs,and allowed the advocate's fee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>ASSAULTING A PUBLIC AN.</head>
        <p>ASSAULTING A PUBLIC AN.</p>
        <p>Thomas Dwyer and Micriael Deer, strangers in the town, were charged at the Born.igrl Court, yesterday morning, with being drunk and disorderly in Bridge-at. the previous afternoon and also with assault,ng John Monks. Mr. A. Browne appeared to prosecute for Mr. Monks, who is landlord of the Packet Houee Inn. Mr. Browne stated that the tws prisoners were supplied with beer, but they refused to pay for it. They used very bad language to the barmaid, and Mr. Monks overheard it, sod came to the prisoners and told them that they must go out. The men refused to do so, and Mr. Monks proceeded to eject the prisoner Deer, when the other prisoner Stiuck him on the head and stunned him for some time, and he was also kicked. P.C. Barnett came up and saw one of the prisoners kick Mr. Monk, in the street. Evidence was given by Sarah Monks, daughter of the landlord, and Mr. Monks in corroboration of Mr. Browne's statement. P.C. Barnett said he was in Bridge- street when he saw a large crowd at the Packet House Inn. He saw the prisoner Dwyer kick the landlord in the abdomen. Be (witness) couldn't say whether the , prisoners were drunk or excited. P.C. Evans said he took Deer into custody. He considered the men were both drunk. The Chief Constable said both of the pris- oners had had drink, but were not what might be called drunk. The prisoners had not enough money to pay for the beer. As there was no evidence against Deer as stated in the charge, he was dismiased, and Dwyer was sent to jail for one month hard labour. Tarrr EY MEANS or A Tarot. At the Warrington Borough Court yesterday morning, Mary Kenny, Newton-le-Willows, was charged with stealing by means of a trick, from Mr. Win. Longshaw, Regent-st., on the 20th October last a number of articles of clothing valued about £2. Prisoner pleaded guilty.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>IT CONQ p 1,4 w</head>
        <p>IT CONQ p 1,4 w</p>
        <p>genie was mostly forward, and stubbornly contested until some pretty passing by Nickson and Robinson enabled Myers to contribute a very pretty run, he only only being brought down just on the line. However,he had his revenge a minute later by dropping a beautiful goal. Some pretty passing was now witnessed between the home team forwards, which enabled Myers again to score. Butterworth making a good bid for goal. Again Runcorn asserted themselves, and some tricky work be- tween Robinson and Nickson reulted in the former scoring. Butterworth failing at goal. Aided by the strong wind. Runcorn kept up the attack, and from a pass by Nickson, IV. Gayter ran in a splendid try, from which a goal was kicked by Butterworth. Half time was called with Runcorn leading by two goals and twa tries to Birkenhead Park nil. Evans re-started, and the visitors, aided by the strong wind, commenced to play up vigorously. Todd headed a beautiful dribble, which forced the homesters in their own quarters, and a free kick further enhanced their position. The Parkites were at length rewarded by a try, the result of some pretty dribbling. The shot at goal, however, was a failure. The game after this was very evenly contested, nothing further being scored. The final result was a heavy de- feat for the Park men, by two goals, three tries, to one try.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE CUP, COMPETITION. SECOND CLASS. RESULTS UP TO SATURDAY. Pld. Won. Lost. Drn. G. T. G. T. Pts. For. Apt. Leigh 5 4 1 0 6 5 ... 2 2 ... 8 St. Helens— 5 ... 4 ... 1 ... 0 ... 2 7 ... 1 2 ... 8 Ulverston 6 ... 4 ... 2 ... 0 .0.11 8 ... 3 1 ... 8 Aspnll 6 4 2 0 6 2 ... 4 2 ... 8 Widnes 11'daleSt.C11.86 ... 2 ... 2 ... 2 ... 2 4 ... 5 3 ... 6 Lancaster 6 ... 3 ... 3 ... 0 ... 4 6 ... 5 3 ... 6 Walkden 6 ... 2 ... 4 ... 0 ... 4 8 ... 4 1 ... 4 Moseley 6 1 4 1 3 1 ... 6 12 ... 3 Blackley 6 0 0 0 1 0 ...10 16 ... 0</p>
        <p>Sr. Minim v. Winice —The first meeting of these rivals at St. Helens on Saturday was rendered more exciting than usual by the exceptionally good positions held by the clubs in the competition. Widner stood at the head of the table with seven points for five matches played, their successes being against Lancaster, Moss- ley, and Ulverston, while they had, drawn with Roch- dale St, Clement's, and lost to Aspull. St. Helens was credited with six points, having played one match less than Widnes. They had beaten Aspull, Walkden, and Mossley, and last against Lancaster. The clubs had previously met seven times, the Saints having won on five occasions with a total score of five goals six tries. Last sealon Widnes were beaten twice by St. Helens, Rain, which had fallen heavily from noon, con- tinued at the time of the bicrsoff, about four thousand persons being present. When the teams appeared it was seen that Brennan and Hogan were absent from Widnes, and Wilson from St- Helens. Case played full back for the visitors. The Saints lost the toss, and Cross started against a strong wind. Even play followed. Play was stopped for a few minutes on account of a collision between Dale and'Ease. Appleton made a splendid run, and looked like scoring, but slipped and was brought down on the line. Forinrormality at scrimmage Widnes were penalized. Appleton dropped at goal, but the ball just went outside. ThxSaiuts still pressed, and some tight scrimmages were witnessed on the visitors' lines. Appleton made another cltop at goal. The kick-off brought relief to Widnes, and play was transferred to the home half. The ball was soon returned and the visitors were apparently overmatched by the good combination of the Saints. Efforts to score however, were unfruitfol, but Dale ultimately got nicely away from a scrimmage, and dribbled part Case. Rimmer was enabled to score for the Saints, but Graham failed to convert. Widnes now roused up. and gradu- ally worked the play into the Saints' twenty-five. Here a bad pass from Little to. Appleton almost let Widiaes in, but Drummond and Barber dribbling well down the • field, Foulkes effected a splendid save. Half-time, arrived with the score—St.. Helens one try, Widneo nil. After the restart the home team did moat of the pressing but Widnes displayed fine defensive tactics, and pre- vented any further scoring, the final result being. St.- Helens one try, Widnes nil. • Wipers No V. Sr. Reimers 2en.—At Widnes). on Saturday. The visitors kicked off, and immediately commeeced a well-sustained and successful pressure, which resulted in two tries being obtained before the game had been in progress a quarter of an hour. With this substantial lead they did not in the first half exert themselves to any very great degree, but were content to hold their opponents at bay. The game ended in a draw of two tries each.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN REC REATION V. Sr HELENS RECREArrowill TEATC—This matiel was played at St. Helens, on Satur. 'day. The home team had a powerful lot of forwards, and were also assisted by Hughes; of Widnes, at three- quarter. The visitors were shorn of Lightfoot at centre,. but otherwise played their usual team. After some (navy Scrimmaging work, of which the home men had the best, they managed to get over at the corner. Tho-1 at. Helens forwards, who were paying a very vigorous- game,frona a mull by the Runcorn. full back; scored an easy try. IL Hughes took a neat pass and got nicely away, but was brought up by the home full back,and at half time the score was two tries to nil in favour of the home men. On restarting, the game seeraed to be more even, and the visitors now had a brief turn of at— tacking. After this the St.Helens men completely over- played the Runcorn forwards, who could do nothing with their weighty opponents. The backs played a good defence game, but the St. Helens men succeeded in scor- ing a couple more tries,and finally won by four tries and eve minors to Runcorn Recreation Iwo minors.</p>
        <p>of scoring owing to the slippery state of theeJ After about 20 minutes play, however, C-otieh ecea( a centre from Evans. A few minutes later Evaes;',4 a second goal through off a pass from E. Pisrpole" 'te iog more was scored up to half time. The litel3„„8 s restarted and played more vigorously but only 00ep1 ceeded In getting pass the school backs,Willcocke ' ing them time after time, Wallace neatly cleared from the home right. Gornall scored a third rel.; school from a scramble in front of goal, and sec"get] wards the whistle blew leaving the Gramteet victors by 3 goals to nil.</p>
        <p>ST- IiELENS TOWN COUNCIL° THE INTRODUCTION OF ELECTRIC LIGHTING. vnLICS' THE BEHAVIOUR OF THE LIVERPOOL p 0 GRAVE INSINUATIONS.' reelothi The last meeting of the St. Helens TOwil,7,,,Lisi for the present municipal year was held ea the Mayor (Mr. Sinclair) presiding. f th`l Alderman Cook moved the confirmation c;„,) 0 minutes of the Gas Committee, which inclu";nen resolution stating that the time had coo.; ler it was desirable to seek for powers to suPP,,tYtetYs tricity for public lighting purposes, and to3llei,' Town Clerk be instrected to take all stePs ;;00 sary to secure such power under a provn'tioo order. He aaid the object was for the CorP°,r-osi to be prepared to take the matter 1.11) a" aed moment when there seemed a necessity for it, when a call was made upon them for it. CO' Mr. Hatton, when the minutes of the Watch ge: mittee were reached, asked when they were goinrhei shut of that dark cloud of policemen. It was ths`,,,,fice were sent back to Liverpool. If the St. ficierlshe'" dl5. carried on as some of them had done, they wouh-n-eeto; charged at a moment's notice. They were n0t.,,-,dAy: tatters. Who, he asked, was going to pay the £w,,-„,10 The Mayor said that, as the responsibility foe "-„jori order in the borough rested upon him, he would s-ve as Mr, Hatton. To keep order it was necessary' to 13,3,01i0 adequate body of police, and this extra body o' was obtained so that there might be no-necessitY tc)l,,es military into the borough, which would hsv6 00 terrible grief to him. On Tuesday last week b°lti there was a considerable disturbance in Farr, will,,cbt De St. Helens police were not able to quell. ThasP'sflOOle (the Mayor) calsed a letter to be sent to ta Secretary, telling him how serious the aflair 5c35,4' ;star answer by telegraph was that a repitition of the "I.p' bance must be prevented, but on that day ancitbeLtiide turbance took place, and although it was just °use&amp; the borough a good many people from Ste.alirdels'ori:landtri;