      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN T. W BANE RHA ?I Sr. .—Played at Weaverhain on Saturday. The visitors soon swarmed round the home goal, but bad shooting lost many a good chance. The game was very even, the Saints having many chances, but Carter was very safe in goal. The visitors broke away again, and the home goal-keeper failing to clear, Jones promptly put the ball through. In the second half the home team played much better down the hill, and soon equalized. The visitors next had a look in and Linton scored. The game was very fast, both ends being visited in turn. Hampson got the ball, and after tricking the home backs, centred well, and*Linton again put it through. NO further scoring took place, and the home team retired beaten after a hard fought game by three goals to one.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON GRAMMER SCROOR. v. Panic Eforrsis WIOAN.—This match was played at Wigan on Wednes- day in wretched weather. The school had it all their own way from the beginning but missed several chances</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before Mr. F. Monks (chairman), Alderman Harrison, Messrs.S. Wright, J.W.Hayward, and Coun-</p>
        <p>cillor Roberts.</p>
        <p>FINED FOR DIIIINKENNES3.—John Hill, Percivals Fold; James Hennagan, Friars Green ;Thomas Roberts, Old Road, Latchford ; Fred. Smith, Old Road, Latchford; James Clarke, Friars Green; John Smith, last from Cardiff ; Mary McKenzie, tramp; Charles Fudge, Albert Terrace, _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>THE CURLEYS Curley, 4 Berry's-yard, Was charged with assaulting his mother Bridget Curley, on Saturday night.—From the evidence it was shown that the assault was of a shocking nature. Prisoner came to his mother's house and commenced using abu- sive language. He was told to desist but he struck his mother in the face, knocked her to the ground, and commenced kicking her. P. C.,s Bruen and Corbett came upon the scene, and they found the woman's face covered with blood and she could scarcely see owing to the quantity of blood coming from a wound in the fore- head. Prisoner was sent to goal for four months. JOHN WARRINGTON RECEIVES HIS QUIRTUS.—John Warrington and John Flannaghan were each charged with being rogues and vagabonds found sleeping out in Slutchers-lane, at 1-15 on Monday morning. P. S. Taylor said that when captured Warrington had a large quant- ity of matches loose in his pockets, and Plannaghan had a pipe and tobacco.—Warrington was sent to goal for three months and Flannaghan was fined 59. and costs or seven days' hard labour. CRUELTY TO A I:loam—Joseph Jackson, hawker, 225 Winwick-road, was charged with illtreating a horse on October 24th—Detective Monaghan said that he saw the defendant hawking in Priestley-st. He noticed that his horse was in pain, and on examination found a raw wound on the back.—lnspector Dowty R.S.P.C.A., gave evidence in support, and defendant was fined 209. in- cluding costs or 14 days in default. DISORDERLY CHARACTERS. Mary Ann Ashley, 1, Buckley-st. : Alice Roberts, 23, Haydock-st. ; and Mary Bridge, 58, Turner-st. ; were each bound over for six months on the charge of fighting in Buckley-st., on Oct. 21st. P. C. Marsh proved the case.—Edward Griffin, Berry's-yard, was fined in the costs for committing a similar offence in Bank-st.,on October 23rd .P.C.Elampson proved the case.—Thomas and Mary Elealey,4, Walsh's- y ard, Mary Collins, 3,01 d-road, Margaret Collins, 2,02- road, and Kate Fallon, 5, Bakehouse-yard, were charged with being disorderly characters in Knutsford-road, on October 22nd. P.S.Taylor said he was at Knutsford-road, and observed the defendants knocking a man down and using some fearful threats. Witness blew his whistle and the defendants then ran away but witness saw them again soon afterwards raising a disturbance in anotner part of the road. Their names were taken and they were reported. F.C.'s Evans and Meacock corroborated. A witness was called to prove that Fallon was at home at the time of the alleged disturbance. The Bench dis- missed the whole of the defendants. THERE WAS NOT SUFFICIENT CREAM IN THE MILK.— Eliz tbeth Winstanley, Church-st., was charged with selling milk which had not sufficient cream in it on Oct. 4th.—lnspector Norman said that on the milk being analysed, it was found that there was a deficiency of 25 per cent, of cream. Defendant said in de- fence that the cream had all collected at the top of the milk, and that she did not stir it up when she served the milk. She was fined 53.. and costs.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED ROBBERY WIER VIOLENCE.--John Carr, a tramp, was charged with assaulting John McMarrow, in Bridge-st., on Saturday night, and with robbing him of a cotton shirt and three yards of calico, valued 33. The complainant said that.on Saturday night, he was going past the Lion Hotel,in Bridge-st.,when prisoner snatched a parcel from his pocket. Witness at once stuck to him, and while doing so he received several blows and they fell to the ground together. A by-stander named Ernest Chapman, helped witness to bold prisoner until Detec- tive Monaghan arrived, and prisoner was then taken to the police station. Witness received some injuries from falling against the curb stone. Prisoner was sent to gaol for two months.</p>
        <p>TIIESDLY.—Before Mr. R. Garnett (chairman), and Dr. Starkey Smith.</p>
        <p>THE MUNICIPAL EtEcrion.—Althsugh there was very little business at the police court on Tuesday morning, the court was crowded with policemen. About 20 of them were sworn to secrecy for the following day which was the date of the municipal elections. THE ALLEGED JEWELRY ROBBERY.—Tha two men Richard Lowe and Benjamin Willis. who were brought before the magistrates last week,on a charge of burglari- ously entering the premises of Mr. Jackson, in Church- st.,in July,and stealing a quantity of jewelry therefrom, were again brought before the magistrates. The charge was reduced to one, of unlawfully offering in pledge a gold signet ring on the 25th Oct., at the shop of Mr. Greaves, Bridge-st., there not being sufficient evidence to convict them of the other charge. The evidence of the previous week was repeated and the prisoners were fined 403. and costs or one month's imprisonment FamAy.—Before Alderman Harrison (in the chair), Dr. • Goma% Councillor H. Roberts, and mr. J. W. Hay- ward.</p>
        <p>FIGHT BETWEEN PAITPEE.4.—WiIIiam Taylor (41) and Patrick Coffey (73), paupers, were charged with being guilty of misbehaviour about 8.30 on Thursday morning, at the workhouse. Mr. Ward, workhouse master, said the two men had been fighting. Both accused the other of striking the first blow. Taylor had behaved in a very brutal manner to the old man,and he was a very violent character. The dispute had had something to do with the elections. Evidence was given by John Murphy and Martin Parkin,inmates of the workhouse, as to the fight. Taylor was considered the most to blArne,as he had been most brutal in his conduct to Coffey, who appeared with his face-in a fearful condition. Taylor was sent to jail for 21 days hard labour. Coffey was discharged. Crranozn ON Suspwrox. —Patrick Rahbitt,Warrington, was charged with being a suspicious character, found on the enclosed premises of the Bridge Inn, at 2.10 that morning,with intent to commit a felony. Evidence was given by a police officer as to hearing a noise from the direction of Mr. Ashton's house. He heard footsteps in the milkhouse. The prisoner was seen standing over a can of milk drinking it. On being arrested he picked up a stick, hut did not strike the officer. The prisoner had only recentty been discharged from the Infirmary,suffer- ing from a wound in the head, and the magistrates dis- missed the case against him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>111 E EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 4. 1893.</head>
        <p>111 E EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 4. 1893.</p>
        <p>DABESBURY PETTY SESSION.</p>
        <p>Tcreen.for.—Before Mr. W. Long (chairman), and Mr. H. Nield.</p>
        <p>DRUNK AND DISORDEELY.—For this offenco at Stretton on the Bth October, James Smith and John Miller,resid- ing at Stretton, were fined 53. including costs each. A similar penalty was imp3sed on Wm. Patin, boatman, for the same offence at Preston-o'th-Efill, on the 10th ult.</p>
        <p>_ DOG Wrnroar A LlCRWSE.—Adolphus Wilkinson, farmer, Seven Oaks, was dismissed on a charge of hay- ing a dog without a license on the 4th October. I A• SLIfHPY DRIVEM.—Win. Sant, Ellesmere-at., War- rington, was charged with having on the sth October, ! at Stretton, been asleep whilst in charge ef a dray, and having no control of the horses. He was fined ss. in- cluding costs. ASSAllLT.—Elizabeth Watton, Wilderspool, was sum- moned for having at Appleton, on the 2nd October, , assaulted and beaten George Wem, 6, Gandy's-yard, ' Warrington. From the evidence of the complainant it appeared that on the date named, he was working at the stables of the Manchester Ship Canal Co.,Wilderspool, at which the defendatWO huttbend i horselseep;, wtin tht defendant came to him and struck him in the eye, and afterwards picked up a piece of brick and said she would dash his brains out. The man who was to give evidence in corroboration of his statement, had been " sacked " by the defendant. The defendant stated that the assault was committed because the complainant owed her some money. She admitted the assault, and was willing to pay for it.. She was fined 20s. and costs or one month's imprisonment: The money was paid. .</p>
        <p>hIRSATS.—James Smith, Streiton, was charged with having oil the 22nd ult., at Whitley,used threats to John Buckley. The complainant stated that be was in bed on Sunday evening the 22nd, when the defendant came to the door and attempted to break it open. He afterwardi went to the back door and broke int) the house. He picked up the poker and said he would smash his brains in. The defendant was his son-in-law. Defendant was bound over to keep the peace for six months in a surety of £lO.</p>
        <p>M RVE 40 US RESULTS.</p>
        <p>If we said these things of ourselves thepublic might well doubt, but never has a remedy r.eceived the un- qualified endorsement that" Homocea " has received. We ask one and all to read these testimonials, and ask themselves should they be without " Homocea " in their homes.</p>
        <p>MEDICAL AND OTHER TESTIMONIES.</p>
        <p>Thirteen months under hospital .treatment. Eleven months on a water bed.</p>
        <p>hold of the greasy oval, but James Bate appeared to the best advantage in this respect, and another capital pass by that player should have been turned to good account by Potts. Donohue and Bailey dribbled well, again, and then twice in quick succession Salford were credited with minors, the result of long kicks. From this point, Warrington took the oval inside Salford territory, and there it remained until the call of "no side," when War- rington had won their third League match by a goal and a try (eight points) to nil. Teams :—Warrington : F. Heesom, back ;C. Potts, P. Carey, J. W. Evans, and H. Webster, three-quarters; John Bate and James Bate, half-backs ; Fair. Turner, J. Jolley, J. Holmes, J. Berry, J. Taylor, J. T. Thorniley, E. Bailey, and T. Donohue.— Salford : W. Manwaring, back ;F. Miles, A. Barratt,j. Smith and J. Rangeley, three-quarters; S. Walsh and France, half-backs ; T. Kent, T. King, S. Haalam, J. Brierley, A. Hindshaw, G. H. Caltwright, Horrox,and J. Tewson, forwards.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTOS A. T. SAixon A,—When time teams met last year at Salford, Warrington received a severe defeat. Warrington have not been going so strongly as last year, but notwithstanding this and the drenching rain which fell throughout the match, there was a large number of spectators on the New Barnes enclosure, when Warrington started the leather down hill. The start was a most encouraging one from the Warrington point of view, for in the first few minutes Lawless punted grandly to the line and handles rushing up scored the first try. Again the visitors attacked splea- didly,and wheeling the packs in irresistible fashion gave the Salford backs any amount cf wolk to do, but whilst the game was raging in this vicinity Coggins lost pos- session, and Ashton, by a series of kicks, propelled the oval to the Warrington end and scored a try. The War- ringtonians quickly invaded their opponents' territory, some smart passing between Clark, Mason, and Lally en- hancing the advantage gained. Walker intercepted a pass and raced clean away,but was ovcrhauled by Lally, and Phillips following up a kick grandly,and in conjunc- tion with Randles,sent the leather to the Salford citadel, but despite the grand play of the Warrington forwards the teams crossed over with the score equal. Salford restarted with only 14 men, and play located In the visitors' quarters until a brilliant dribble, beaded by Hampson, Buckley, Lawless and P,hillips was not checked until the home territory was reashed. Free kicks to either side were succeeded by Edwards, Ran- dles, Teare, and Marks rushing to the line, and a try was secured, but disallowed. At length Harwood, of Broughton Rangers fame, relieved the danger, the Sal- ford forwards carrying the ball to the visitors' 25. Sal- ford now pressed, Campbell making a splendid effort to !let through.but Phillips following up a kick broke away 3nto Salford lines, but nothing more was scored, an ex- citing game ending in a draw ef one try each.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON 2ND V.CIIESTER CoLutoe.---The first meet- ing between these two clubs took place at Wilderspool- causeway on Saturday in miserable weather. Rowe kicked off for iVarrington in a drenching shower, Ry- lance punting in touch. At length from some neat play Plinston got over, the place kick Droving a failure. From the drop out Chester pressed and some good work by Hunter and Butterworth nearly ended in the latter scoring. The borne team still kept up the attack, and Gibson was conspicuous with a nice run and kick. Shortly afterwards Griffiths got over, the kick again be- ing a failure. Resuming, Thornton was conspicuous with a neat dribble, Hunter saving. Cragg now got hold, and, passing to Hunter, this player tried a shot at goal, a minor resulting. Rylancenew secured,and run- ning strongly got in under the posts, Cragg converting. Warrington eventually won by two tries to Chester Col- lege one goal. STOCKTON HEATH V. WIDNES RANORRS.—PIayed at Widnes before a good attendance. Stockton Heath had the assistance of a strong wind at the kick off, and after a disallowed try had been scored by Baldwin, Barber obtained from a throw out, and got over no goal result- ing, which was the only point scored before the interval. The second half proved to be all in favour of the Heathens, and the game had not long been continued when Broady obtained at half-way, and after eluding all opposition gained a brilliant try, but only to see the referee ordering a five yards scrimmage. Twiss soon afterwards scored, and the referee allowing the point, Glover took the place, but just failed. Just before the call of time Broady dribbled,over the line and grounded in a favourable position, but the place was again at fault. On the call of time Stockton Heath had gained a clever victory by three tries to nil.</p>
        <p>WAHMINGTON Sr. MARY'S v. FARNWOITTN.-012 day,St. Mary's journeyed to Farnworth. The home team won the toss and played with the wind at their backs. Berl.) started the ball for the Saints, which was immedi- ately taken to the home line, where Woods wnd Welsby had hard lines for scoring. Farnworth now relieved, and the bit? travelled to midfield, neither side having the advantaxe on account of the wet state of the ground. On turning round the Saints had the best of the game, but the game looked like ending in a draw when, Hamp- son secured from a long throw out and scored, flielisby failing to improve, and St. Mary's won by one try to nil.</p>
        <p>BIIMENITRAD PA Inc v. RUNCORN.—A large gathEring turned out at the Upper Park to witness the first of the season's engagements between these Cheshire organiza- tions. Faulkner opened the gime, and after the lirst exchanges play settled down in the Park 25. Free kicks did not benefit the sides mach, when the Park forwards ' gradually got down, but on the play becoming looss•tbe Runcornians had; matters pretty much (heir own way,. and a Park backrin attempting to clear, screwed round in front of his gnat, where a fair catch was made, from which Warder easily kicked a goal. On restarting, the- Runcornians executed some nippy bits of passing, and kept their opponents extended to their lines. Fenton and Baxter eased the pressure, and the former again getting, possession, the ball' was sent one the line, Houghton conceding a minor point. Dropping out, the Runcorn- lens were again in evidence, and a smart pass was made' to W. Faulkner, vrho raced over the line, and from the place Warder registered a goal, the sides changing ends immediately afterwards, Runcorn leading by two goals to three minor points. Ow restarting, the Runcorn. players, now aided by the wind, bore down on the home defence, and, when in good position, W. Faulkner dropped and almost piloted the ball over the bar. An attempt to break away was foiled' by Warder, when Abrams dashed away from the pack, and,on nearing the line, passed to J. Faulkner,. who dashed over, but the kick at goal failed. A moment later 0. Anderson was compelled to toilets down from an attempt at a drop by Warder, and from; the kick Cut Anderson (J. D.) rushed over, but kicked too strong',. i,And Houghton enabled his side to attack with successful results, for Abrams slipped over the line; but goal-kicking was again a failure. J. Faulkner followed with another try near touch in goal, as also did W. Faulkner, with a resulting goal. Following this reverse,. the Park put on a spurt, But could not score, and the game ended—Runcotn, 3 goals and 3 tries;: Park, nit. Teams: Rungern—S. Houghton, hack;: E. J. Morris, W. Faulkner, and T. Warder, three-quarter backs; S. Abrams and F. Faul- kner, half backs; R. Reece, H. Williams, J. Davies, J. Stubbs, J. Langley,A. Taylor, H. Farmer, G. Moore,and Lightfoot, forwards. Birkenhead Park—C. Anderson, back; J. Hutchinson, A. E; Penton, and F. E. Beausire, three-quarter backs; .7. D. Anderson and M'Donald,half backs; W. T. Wood, T. W. Lockwood, A.. 7. Chadwick, J. Baxter, A. R. Spence, W. H. Wakefield, H. Smith, S. Smith, and Rev. G. D. White. forwards.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN v. BIRKENHEAD. PARK, "A "TEAMS.—The meeting of these teams took place on Saturday, en the Irwell-lane enclosure, Runcorn,before a mnderato atten. dance of spectators, owing to the inclement state of the weather. Neither organization had been beaten this season. Both teams were fairly represented. The Park started, Gayter returning to touch near centre. The</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>RUNCORN PETTY ERSSIONS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY ERSSIONS.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before Mr. A. Thomas and Mr. Jas. Handley. Drimor am) DISORDERLY.—For this offence fines were imposed upon Arthur Allaby and Patrick Harris,and for being guilty of indecent behaviour, Alice Ilampson was sent to Raol forl4 days.</p>
        <p>'ins circular; AND THIS SHIP CANAL LABOURER.—Ann Page, of Poole-lane,was summoned for assaulting a Ship Canal labourer, named Alexander Wilson. Wilson bad been lodging with the defendant for nine mouths,and on the 23rd August he went to fetch his clothes. He alleged that the woman struck him on the head with a brush, and alsa threw a bucket of water upon him. Dr. McDougall charged him ss.for dressing his wound.—The defendant said she had been compelled to turn him away because of his filthy habits. She had struck him, but it was because he had aggravated her, and had threatened to give her two black eyes.—A fine of 10s.and costs Be. Bcl., was imposed. SCHOOL BOARD CASES.—Several parents were sum- moned for not sending their children regularly to school, and the usual penalty was imposed. NON-PAYMENT OF WATER RATES.—The Runcorn, Wes- ton, and Halton Waterworks Cb. summoned 38 persons for non-payment of water rates. A number of cases had been settled out of court, and in others the justices made orders for payment. CART WITHOUT A NAME.—Wm. Bibber was summoned for using a cart without having Ws name painted there- on.—Fined is. and costs 10s. 61.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>0 FPI° E.</head>
        <p>0 FPI° E.</p>
        <p>THE NEW SECRETARY OF THE POST</p>
        <p>The Postmaster General has appointed Mr. Spencer Walpole, Lieutenant Governor of the Isle of Man, to be Secretary of the Post Office in the room of the late Sir Arthur Blackwood.</p>
        <p>In an article on the appointment the "Daily News" says : The appointment of Mr. Spencer Walpole the. younger, now lieutenant governor of the Isle of Man, to be Secr3tary of the Post Office, may be expected to provoke a good deal of comment and even surprise. Mr. Walpole is an able nian, the author of several useful and successful books, one of which, his life of Lord John Russell, seems likely to hold a permanent place in Eng- lish literature. He graduated, so to speak, in the Civil Service, and he has been justly popular among the Manxmen. Mr. Arnold Morley had a difficult task in filling the place of Sir Arthur Blackwood, whose official capacity was of a very high order indeed. But fresh blood is no bad thing In a great department, especially in one where labour is employed by the Government on so vast and varied a scale. Mr. Walpole is a sound Liberal in every sense of the term. To a thorough know- ledge of history he unites the keenest sympathy with modern ideas of social and industrial problems. He will find his work cut out for him at the Post Office, where official Conservatism has been particularly strong. The present Postmaster General has done a great deal to introduce a more tolerant and comprehensive system of administration. In carrying out the principles which he has enunciated in tho House of Commons, Mr. Morley will derive much benefit from the cc-operation of Mr. Walpole.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>COW IN A LAWYER'S OFFICE.</head>
        <p>A COW IN A LAWYER'S OFFICE.</p>
        <p>EXTRAORDINARY SCENE AT ST. HELENS..</p>
        <p>AttOui:Wcir;CicOn-it;es-cl;i-Wtein-ooii a cow ran into. the premises of Mr. Joseph Massey,solicitor, Hardshaw- st., St. Helens, and,running upstairs,broke a glass door; then, continuing upwards to the second, floor, it broke through the staircase window and went bodily on to the roof of a two-storey building. Both the animal's fore and hind legs went through the slates, and it was held a prisoner, the hind legs dropping through the plaster- ceiling above the lower landing, flow to rescue the animal became a matter of considerable perplexity. A crowd of people soon collected and the extraordinary scene of a cow fast in a roof caused great interest. TO cow had been sent for _disposal at a cattle sale in the Fleece Hotel yard, which is at the rear of the premises, and a large number of butchers and dealers were in the neighbourhood, as well as a number of men looking for a job. A dozen men, including Mr. Dean, butcher, Church-st., and Constable Smirthwaite, were soon on the roof, devising means of rescuing the animal. Ultim- ately, some ropes were brought, and passed round a chimney, which bends from the outer wall of the lower storey to the main building in the shape of- a of a semi- circle. To these ropes, pulleys and blocks were attached, and other ropes were fastened round the cow's body, and also fastened to its horns. The rafters were then sawn away, and while this was being done the animal kicked and plunged so wildly that a large number of slates were broken all round it. As soon as the aperture had been made sufficiently large the animal was lowered en to the landing below, whence it was dragged by ropes to the street. The damage done to the property of Mr. Massey is very considerable.</p>
        <p>TOUR ATTENTION, PLEASE t Sufferers from Gravel, Lumbago. Piles, Pains In the Back, Dropsy, Wind and Water Complaints. Diseases of " Kidneys, Bladder, Urinary Organs, Stone, Gleet, &amp;de ture, Sciatica, Rheumatism. and Gout, will find a positive cure in Holdroyd's Gravel Pills.- -Try a small box, and if not satisfied your money will he returned. Priee Is lid. Of all chemists, or post frte for 12 stamps from HOLD. BOYD'S Medical Hall, Cleckheaton, Yorks. Don't. be put off. If you cannot get them write to the Proprietor, and a box will be sent Next post.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>CURED IN ONE MONTH BY</head>
        <p>CURED IN ONE MONTH BY</p>
        <p>" NOMdCEA."</p>
        <p>Mitcham, S. W.</p>
        <p>'• I wish to inform you that the UM of your" Homo- cea • has produced most satisfactory results. Briefly the case is as follows :—A young woman under hospital treatment for thirteen months for disease of the hip joint, came to consult me after the splints and other appliancos had been remlved ; she kept the recumbent position for eleven months on a water bed and suffered much pain, and could neither walk nor go upstairs, without enduring the most severe agonies of pain, all apparently associated with the origin and distribution of the sciatic nerve ;in bed, also, the least movement of the affected limb gave much pain. I came to a satis- factory diagnosis that the pain was entirely of a neuralgic nature, and no longer due to the diseased hip, in which I believe that I was supported by the advisers she had at the hospital ; therefore, I at once put her on such local and internal treatment as might benefit her, but with little if any result. At last I tried your " flomoceo," and after just a month's use, the young woman is, I might sac, quite well ; the improvement, however, was very soon apparent; she can now walk comfortably, and easily ascend a flight of stairs, and does not complain of any paia whatever. Univ. Dub!, L.R.C.S.L., &amp;c.,</p>
