      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>RUGBY. WARRINGTON V. SALFORD.</head>
        <p>RUGBY. WARRINGTON V. SALFORD.</p>
        <p>These teams bad their first battle this season at the Wilderspool-road enclosure on Saturday;and the result was a surprise to the football world, coming as it did so soon after the runaway victory of Oldham over our local team the week previous. Saturday was a miserable thy, and one sadly against a true exposition of the Rugby code, rain falling nearly the whole progress of the match, rendering the leather greasy, and thus diffi- cult to hold. Nevins was absent from the home side, but a welcome return was accorded to Webster,who took his place at three-quarter for the first time this season. There would be 5,000 present when Salford, having lost the toss, started operations, defending the railway goal. With the elements in their favour, the locals soon made matters warm for the Reds, and Thorniley got neatly away from a line-out, but John Bate taking up the run- ning, an injudicious kick sent up the first minor. War- rington next had a bit of hard luck, when Holmes, in heading a rush, was foully tackled, and a sprained ankle prevented his taking any further part in the game. With fourteen men the locals more than held their own, and the Bates passed well, but Barratt floored Evans before he could get away. The two Bates were foiled by France, and altimgh Jolley was conspicuous in a line-out, the visitors again displayed neat passing, and reached Heesom, but the custodian displayed exceptional I form, and half-way only was reached. Potts kicked to Manwaring, who allowed the ball to roll between his legs, and this practically led up to Warrington's first tangible poi nt, for the forwards fairly beating their oppon- ents the leather was rushed over and Taylor scored at the corner, Fair. Turner making a capable bid for the goal. A long spell of desultory play followed, and the major portion being confined ta the forwards it was as unin- teresting as it possibly could be. John Bate accepted a Salford pass and missed a glorious chance, he only hav- ing b turn round and walk in between the posts. Evans , saved well, and Eleesorre made Tuson and Hindshaw beat a hasty retreat, whilst Donohue was applauded for a capital dribble. Salford now had a look in at the home I quarter, but Heesom was equal to any emergency, and with the aid of Carey and Evans, the Salford men were forced past the half-way. flag. Warrington were hard on the attack when the interval occurred,and were lead- ing by a try (three points) to nil. This was a very small margin in Warrington's favour, considering the strong wind which was against them in the second half. But with only 14 men and a strong wind in front of them, they were still more than a match for the Salford men. Fair. Turner's kick-off was a grand effort, landing the oval in touch a yard from the line. A determined stand was made, and though Rangeley and Barrett relieved, Heesom piloted b.sek safely, and Manwaring was busily employed in staving off the leaders. Turner and Berry were conspicuous in some loose play, and though Smith nromply neutralised this combined effort, Heesom again forced his opponents back on their lines. Carey and Webster next made a bold l.'id along the popular touch, whilst Evans and Potts tried their hands with no better luck on the other side. Evans kicked well, and Manwar- ing was well held by Potts, and after James Bate had essayed to break through, he eventually defeated Walsh, and handing over the ball to Potts, that player put the finishing touch to a clever bit of play, and Heesom kicked a goal amidet ringing cheers, Salford being denied the privilege of charging. This success of Warrington acted as a stimulus on the visitors, for after the kick Off by Kent, Walsh got clear, and running well passed to Miles, who appeared a certain scorer until Webster appeared on the scene, and brought down the Salford flyer. Ily degrees the visitors reached the Warrington goal mouth, but here the seven local forwards displayed what metal they are made of. and successfully warded off the danger. Jolley was the first to show up with a dribble, and then Donohue displayed smart footwork finally tackling Manwaring. Hindshaw and King got. away, only to be pulled up by Heesom, and for five minutes the latter was busily employed, and never at fault. It was with difficulty the players maintained a</p>
      </div>
