        <head>EARLESTOWN, NEWTON, liAIDOCK, &amp;c.,</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN, NEWTON, liAIDOCK, &amp;c.,</p>
        <p>SOCIETY.BUILDING</p>
        <p>The 30th annual meeting of the members of the above society was held in the District Infant school, Earlestown, on Thursday evening. 31r. F. Harwood, chairman of directors, presided, .and there was a fair attndanee of members, amongst others present being, Messrs. R. Davies, solicitor, T. Appleby, and T. Thompson, auditors, and P. Chandley, secretary. • The report of the directors, showed that the profits for the year amount to £1,617 12s. 5d., which with the undivided balance from last year of t 2,276 151. 6d., showed a disposable amount of £3,894 7s. 11d.' from which it is proposed to recommend the payment of a dividend at the rate of 44 per cent, per annum, absorb- ing £1,545 17s. 3d., leaving a balarice of undivided profit of £2,348 10s. Bd., leaving an increase over last year of £7l 15s. 2d. Five members have redeemed their pro- perty, representing repayments of capital to the amount of £2,065 163. sd. One small lot of property was still on hand. The number of members on the books was 412, holding 3,4484 shares. The auditors report was as follows :—Webeg to report that we have examined the books and vouchers of the Society for the, year ended 21st September, 1893. The books have been correctly kept. The mortgages of the Society have beeri inspected, and all renewal receipts for insurance of the properties have been presented to us, and the same found to be in order.—We are, yours faith- fully, Taos. Tnomrsos, Wm. APPLEBY, auditors. The Chairman said they were met there that night with a decreased dividend, and it was only right that they should know under what circumstances that had been brought about. They would remember that last year a 5 per cent, dividend was declared; they now meet with a 44, and the actual amount earned was a fraction under 41 per cent. The first imprersion of the Directors was to pay 4, but they felt that a diffidence in taking a little fr.-m the reserve fund, they therefore, after careful consideration decided to declare a dividend of 44 per cent. They would observe also that they had lent out during the year £6,560, on what your Directors considered to be good security. That was an increase over last year of £3,200, but he had to state that the greatest portion of it, perhaps two thirds, was got out during the last four months in the year, consequently they bad received little dividend from it. The benefits to be derived would come in the current years' accounts. They would observe also that they had bad a great amount in the bank during the whole of the year, an average of from £5,000 to £6,000, and they would know the bank interest was very low, and had brought them ,in very little, he theught about £lOO. He was able to assurethem, however, that those of the Directors who might be elected that night would use their best endea- vours to place that amount at more remunerative in- terest, and there was a prospect of their being able to get some portion of it out very shortly. (Hear, hear.) Taken altogether, he thought the shareholders had every reason to be well satisfied with the position of the Society. They were in the satisfactory position of being absolutely and perfectly solvent. (Applause.) He then moved the adsption of the report and balance sheet, which was seconded by 31r. Jos. Emmett and carried. , ELECTION OF OFFICERS. The election of officers was then proceeded with, and resulted as fellows :—President, Mr. F. Harwood . • direc- tors, Messrs. W. Gilmore, Thos. Haselden, John Barr, J. , Prescott, J. Walker, J. Emmett, N. Slinn, W. H. Bailey, and B. Harrison ; surveyor, Mr. John Elliott ; stewards, Messrs. T. Ormand, J. Gooding; auditors, Messrs. T. Thompson, W. Appleby ; arbitrators, Messrs. W. F. Gooch, J. Fairclough, and A. Peake. Mr. R. Davies, solicitor, after referring to the fact, that it was the 30th anniversary of the society which be had attended, expressed the hope that the society might flourish in the future as it had done in the past. With regard to the dividend, even the great railways did net pay a 44 per cent, on the shares, aud he thought that the shareholders of that society had every reason to be satisfied. They were unfortunate in having money in the bank, but that was the case with nearly all building , societies. He found that when a man wanted to build he wanted to borrow as much as he possibly could, but did not care about being in a hurry to pay it off. The thiugs which all tbeir minds were full of at the present moment was this unfortunate coalstrike. He was read- ing, as he came along to the meeting that the Midland Railway Company had lost £67;000 1::), the stoppage.He was not going to give his opinion on the subject, but they all hoped it would soon end, and be thought a satis- factory arrangement could be made between masters and men to work things smoothly in the future. (Hear, hear.) There were trades in this country, in which there had never been a dispute since certain rules, between master and man had been in operation. He was happy to think, in Earlestown they had not suffered much in , their trade, and it was a good thing. They had only one property on the hands of the society, which showed that everyone was keeping up their payments, and they were in a fortunate position la this club. (Ap- plause.) Mr. T. Appleby said it was getting monotonous to come there year by year,and never even get up the sem- blance of a row over the balance sheet, and in going through the books, they never found a single mistake, and this ought to be a great amount of satisfaction to them, 44 per cent was not a bad dividend especially when they came to consider It was not a speculative business. (Applause.) Mr. Haselden moved a vote of thanks to the Chairman,and after being seconded by Mr. Thompson it was heartily recorded. A vote Of thanks was also tendered Mr. Davies, and the auditors, and the meeting dispersed.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>TWO LIBERAL VICTORIES. LIVELY SPEECHES. There wete four municipal contests in St. Helens —VIZ, in Central Ward, 1,029 electors; North Ec- cleston Ward, 1,410 electors; Parr Ward, 1,219 electors; and West Sutton Ward, 1,164 electors. The greatest interest centred in North Eceleston Ward, where an attack had been made upon the seat of Mr. B. B. Glover (C), colliery proprietor, by Mr. I. B.ltose (L). The other candidates were as follow : —Mr. J. B. Leach (L) and Mr. M.Kinley (Lab) in , Central; Mr. E. Johnson (C) and Mr. H. Griffin i (Lab) in West Sutton; and Mr. F. J. Brown (C) and Mr. F. W. Fletcher (L) in Parr. The booths were closed at eight o'clock, and the boxes were sealed and conveyed to the Town Hall, where the Town Clerk (Mr. W.J. Jeeves) had made arrangements for a quick count. At a quarter-past nine o'clock the poll was declared as follows by the Mayor (Mr. Sinclair) to a crowd of 3,000 or 4,000 persons : Central.—Leach (L), 403 ; M'Kinley (Labour), 367 majority, 36. Parr Ward.—Fletcher (L),656 ; Brown (C),4o7—majo- rite, 249. A Liberal victory. West Sutton Ward.—Johnson (C), 407 ; Griffin (L), 406—majority, 1. The candidates tied with 406 votes each, and ties Mayor gave his casting vote in favour of the old mem- ber. North Eccleston Ward. —Rose (L), 609; Glover (C), 431—majority. 178. A Liberal victory. The Mayor having completed reading the figures,there were loud cries for the candidates. Mr. J. B. Leach was the first to come forward. He thanked the electors for again placing him at the head of the poll, and returning him to the Town Council for the third time. Mr. Veter M'Kinley,who for, the past three months had been at the head of a largo body of the u nemployed,and has requested all the public bodies to find some work for men, and who had been running for Central Ward, in which many of the unemployed reside, then mounted upon the stone balustrade, and was received with loud demonstrations of approval by many in the crowd. He said he appeared before them as a Labour candidate de- feated by only 36 votes. It had not cost him a single penny to conduct this election (cheers), and he had not done any canvassing, but by the moral support of the working classes he had beaten the best blood sucker they could bring. They would not only beat all the blood suckers, but they would beat all the top hats in St. Helens sooner or later. The votes recorded for him had been given not only by honest working men, but by starving working men, and men whose wives and children were starving (" Shame "and " Well done, Peter "). That had been a moral victory for the working classes of St. Helens. They had not returned a Labour candidate that day to the Town Council, but they would return a Labour member to Parliament (great cheers). This they would do in spite of all the money bags and mushroom gentlemen (laughter). The mounted police in the town were nothing, in_his opinion, but mongrels (" Shame"); they were neither police nor soldiers—neither fish, flesh, nor fowl. They were mongrels—something between a proper soldier ane'a policeman (renewed cries of "Shame" from the steps). They cost £2BO a week,and that was taken from the starving mem and children to keep those men feasting (cries of " Shut up"). Mr. M'Kinley (to the crowd).—I will not shut up. They don't like it (" Oh, ob." One of the candidates here said. "Shut up, shut up"). Mr. M'Kinley.—They call it a shame. D.) you eau it a shame (cries of" No, no " and" Go on ") ? Don't you endorse what I say ? All who agree with mo say "Aye" (there were loud cries of" Aye," and cheers). " lam the People's l'eter yet,"he then exclaimed,at which there was great laughter and cheers. They would see a revolu- tion in the town. They not not have coercion in the tawn as they had in Ireland. Hehoped and trusted they would watch how those top-hattpi mushroom gentlemen voted upon the labour question (a Voice : Put it in the Press, Peter," and laughter). He thanked mostsineerely all who had voted for him. 'I hough defeated that day they would gain the victory to-morrow like heroes (loud laughter and cheers.) Mr. Rose, who was received with loud cheers, said he thanked them most heartily for the honour they had done him in returning him as one of their repressn- tati yes. Mr. B. B. Glover, colliery proprietor, who had been defeated, was received with hooting by the crowd, ani could only be heard by the gentlemen and Press repre- sentatives on the steps, said he thanked the:43l bur- gesses who had recorded their votes for him that day sad aiso the geutlelata who had worked laud Ist* Lisa.</p>
        <p>He felt very sorry indeed that he had been beaten, more on account of those who had been. working than on his own account. Mr. Fletcher, the successful candidate for Parr, who on coming forward was cheered very enthusiastically, said he wished to thank them all, both ladies and gentle- men, for the kind manner in which they had worked for him. He never expected to be returned with such a majority. Mr. Brown then thanked the 407 electors who h d voted for him. Mr. Johnson was sorry so many of his friends had one against him. He had done his duty in the Council, uus his special friends had gone against him, and that was the reason of such a narrow majority. Mr. Griffin thanked them one and all, lie came be- fore them as a Labour candidate, not a Liberal or Con- servative. Mr. Johnson had fought on political lines. They had shown that day that the feeling for labour representatives was 'very strong. He had been rejected by only one vote. He was the people's man, while Mr. Johnson was the re4urning officer's man. Be hoped to come forward next year, and be returned by a large majority. (Cheers.) Mr. Leach proposed, and Mr. Rose seconded a vote of thanks to the Mayor as returning officer, which was carried with loud cheers. At the close of tho proceedings, Mr. Peter M'Kinley again mounted the balustrade, and began to harangue the crowd. The Council now consists of sixteen Liberals, fourteen, Conservatives, and six Liberal Unionists.</p>
        <p>CHARGE OF ATTEMPTED WIFE MURDER AT HAYDOCK.</p>
        <p>-- PRISONER BEFORE THE MAGISTRATE. At the Warrington Occasional Court, on Thurs- day, before Majer Pickmere (chairman), Martin King, of Haydock, was charged with having on the 14th October, at Haydock, feloniously wounded Elizabeth King, his wife, by stabbing her with a knife with intent feloniously, wilfully, and of malice aforethought to kill and murder her. Elizabeth King wife of the prisoner was first witness called and said she resided with him at 15 Millfield-lane, Haydock. About 11 o'clock on Saturday -the 14th October she went to Earlestown, on to Crow-lane to see an old woman named Mrs. Thompson, and on her way from Haydock she called at two neighbours houses in Lime-st., and also called at Mr. Melling's public-house, and had a small whiskey, and afterwards went to Mrs. Thompson's. At the Railway Hotel she had another small ishiskey when her husband came in. She asked the prisoner if he had come to meet her, and he replied " yes, I have." The prisoner had a stick in his hand, and she didn't like the looks of the stick, and she broke it. The witness here said she did not wish to give any further evidence against her husband, but the clerk (Mr. Greenall) asked the magistrate's permission to , cross-examine the witness which was granted. Witness I then admitted that she was not sober at the time, but said she had a bother with prisoner at the public house, as he was jealous of her about some other men. They were bothering the whole of the time in the public house, but she paid for a " glass" for the prisoner. They left together and afterwards went to the Newmarket Inn about eight o'clock, where they had two glasses of porter. They afterwards proceeded home, and on going through Vista- lane, she remembered calling out to the prisoner "Don't hurt me Ring.' She found out that something ailed her neck. There was nobody else in the lane except her husband, but the witness refused to give any further evidence against her husband as to how she received her injuries. The cap and knife produced belonged to her bus- band,and she was wearing the shawl produced when she left home on the day in question. She had been under the care of Dr. Hayward at the hospital, and left it on Tues- day last. She remembered meeting Edwin Adams the same night in Vista-lane, and he assisted her to her sis- ter's house in Penny-lane, Haydock. Dr. Thomas Ernest Hayward, F.R.C.S., Haydock,said that on the evening of Saturday, the 14th October,about 10 p.m., he was called to the house of Mrs. Stockton, of Penny-lane, Haydock, and there found Mrs. King lying on a couch. She was pale and faint as if she had lost some blood, but she was conscious. He found a wound on the right side of the neck, from :which bleed- ing had evidently taken place, but it had then ceased. There was also a superficial skin wound underneath it. Each of the wounds were three-quarters of an inch in length, and at the back of the left hand there was a cut about half an inch long, and on the right thumb there was another wound about an inch long. The wound in the neck would be caused by some sharp instrument as the knife produced. The wound was in a dangerous position, but the woman had made good progress to- wards recovery. Robert Watkinson, barman, at 128 Market-st., said on Saturday the 14th October, about eight o'clock, the pri- soner and his wife came into the Newmarket Inn, Earles- town, where he was barman. They went into the best room, and Mrs. King ordered two stouts, and pa,d for them. Mrs. King left the house first, and shortly after- wards the prisoner followed her. Edwin Adams, baker, New Boston, Hasidock, deposed that he was going down Vista Lane to Earlestown, on the night in question at nine o'clock, and when past the Wood Pit Colliery, a woman jumped up, and caught hold of his arm, and said "Master, master, don't leave me, some man has attacked me, and he has hurt my hand." He (witness) asked her who it was, hut she would not tell him. She said "I think I'm bleeding, let me lie down, I feel faint." Somebody came along the road with a lantern, and he then ssw that Mts. King was I covered with mud on her face, hands, and clothes. There was blood also an her face and bands. When be saw the state she was in, he assisted her to her Sister's house in Penny-lane. In the meantime he had sent for the doc- tor and police. P.C. Walbank, Haydock, and P.C. Blackburn. Hay. dock, also gave evidence. The latter said about twenty minutes to ten on Sunday, the 15th tilt., be apprehended the prisoner in a yard near his own house, in Millfield- lane, Haydock. He was then without cap, and wearing the clothes produced which were very much saturated with water and mud. He took him to Ilaydock Police Station, and there charged him with attempting to mur- der his wife by stabbing her with a knife. The prisoner made no reply. The prisoner was remanded to the next Liverpool Assizes. Bail was refused.</p>
        <p>SAD FATALITY TO A NURSE AT NEW TON-LE-WILLOWS. A fatal accident of a sad nature nefel a young lady named Clara Swift, at Newton-le-Willows station, on Monday morning. Miss Swift was head nurse at Haydock Lodge lunatic asylum, and was about 30 years of age. She left the institution on Monday morning, with the intention of proceeding to Liverpool, to bring back a patient, who happened to be a relative of hers. Arriving at the station, she purchased a ticket, and then ascended to the Man- chester platform by the subway steps. At the book stall she bought a comic illustrate paper, and a train for Bolton being in the station, she walked down the plat- form to the level crossing, to cross to the Liverpool side. It is supposed she was looking at the pictures in the paper, and after passing the end of the Bolton train was not aware of any danger. As it happened, however, the express train which leaves Manchester at 9-35 for Liverpool was coming along at a high rate of speed, and porter F. Jenkins seeing the young lady in danger, called •to her, but she evidently did not heed the warning, and before she was aware of it, the express dashed up, and the engine striking her on the left side, pitched her with tremendous force against the bridge wall. An alarm was raised, and the unfortunate victim was at once picked up and carried into a waiting room, but it was seen that slieWas then dead. Her lelt , arm was broken, and her head was horribly mutilated, death having evidently been instantaneous. The paper , she bad been reading was found close to the spot where the accident occurred. The inquest on the body was held at the Legsla Arms Hotel on Tuesday, by Mr. S. Brighouse, coroner. Mr. Fenna, Liverpool, was present on behalf of the Railway Company.—Dr. Street, of Haydock Lodge lunatic asylum, gave evidence of identification, and said the deceased had been head nurse at the asylum for two years. She was a very able servant, and had left the institution on Monday morning to go to Liverpool to bring a patient to the asylum, who happened to be a relative of hers, and he thought that fact might have been troubling her somewhat. He did latt see her again alive.—Fred. Jenkins, porter, said he saw deceased cross- ing the line and shouted to her, but she did not seem to notice him.—John Atkinson, driver of the train, said he was travelling at the usual express speed, and when passing through the station the engine whistle was sounded until he was clear. He did not see the deceased until the engine struck her. On arrival at Earlestown he told the officials that he had knocked a woman down at Newton.—Ann Williams said she searched, washed and laid cut the body, and found on the deceased a rail way ticket for Liverpool, and a pulse containing 16s. 51 —The jury returned a verdict of " Accidental death " ' and recommended that a notice be placed at the end of the platform near the crossing, warning passengers of the danger of crossing the line.</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 4, 1'93.</p>
