        <p>Warrington.</p>
        <p>Zetterfs to the tutor.</p>
        <p>THE LOCAL DISTRESS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—No one csn be brought face to face with the terrible distress which exists in our midst at the present time, without wishing to do all in his power to relive it. Will you kindly allow me to make a practical suggestion. Might not well-to-do families undertake to look after one or two needy households ? Could they not spare them a share of their own plentiful dinner, and, in ad- dition to this, give them those odds and ends of food which exist in all well-to-do households ? This would relieve the distress fund to a certain extent. I would suggest that any who do this should ascertain through the clerey that the families they adopt are deserving, and should notify the Committee of the Distress Find that they will be responsible for the relief of such and such a family. lam sure that if our dinners have to be made simpler or even less plentiful in consequence, that we shall be no losers.—l am air, yours truly, MORLEY STEVENSON. Warrington Training College.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>MUNICIPAL ELECTION.</head>
        <p>THE MUNICIPAL ELECTION.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l cannot help writing a few lines on the result of the municipal election. Being an old Warringtonian I naturally take a deep interest in all that pertains to the prosperity and interest of the town, but I blush with shame when I think that three publicans have been successful candidates. It does seem that the bung interest reigns in this old borough, and how long shall this be so ? Are the respectable and religious people satisfied ? 1 should think the clergymen, temperance reformers, and teachers of religion, who supported them will have qualms of conscience. It seems hypocritical cn their part for them to hold such positions if they are not consistent ; light and darkness cannot go together. The clergy unfortunately have been closely allied with the bung interest. The Master himself will look upon such teachers as blind guides. The alliance is an unholy alliance. Men who profess to be called of God,defenders of the church, and yet ever ready to vote for such men. The N ic' ory on Wednesday last, when publicans were returned in the place of men of position and intelligence will be only for a time. A day of reckoning will come. The publicans will be beaten with their own rod. The leaders of the Conservative party, and among them are a few respectable men, will, I am sure, on reflection, feel ashamed of their conduct in countenancing such candi- dates. Some of them admit this demon drink must be grappled with, and are willing to serve on the Licensing Committee of enquiry, and yet somehow they cannot resist helping on the interest of the publican candidates if they are true blue. Oh for more consistency on the part of men who occupy positions of trust. I would rather myself be on the side of the minority than on that side which to a large extent is responsible for all the misery and poverty that exists in many homes in War- rington to-day. We have been defeated, 'Us true, but we are not cast down. Right and truth will and must prevail.-1 remain, yours truly, AN OLD WARRINGTONIAN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>WARRINGTON A ERDLEWORK GUILD.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON A ERDLEWORK GUILD.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l shall be much obliged if you will, through the assistance of your paper, bring the above guild before the public of Warrington,</p>
        <p>List year, the comeMittee of the guild were enabled, through the kindness of those interested in the work, to distribute, through the clergy, 500 articles of clothing, which were warmly appreciated by all. This winter, owing to the prevailing distress, we feel that double the number of garments will fall short 'of the number re- quired, the poverty at present being so great. So I appeal to those interested in Warrington, and who do not already belong to our guild to join us. one shilling and two warm and inexnensiv, garments being the amount of subscription required to enable nearly every one in our town to help in a practical way those leas fortunate than themselves.</p>
        <p>alLadies with little time at their disposal, will help a double charity by purchasing garments at the parish sales held at this season, or from Mrs. Harrison,in King- at., ;who is at present assisting (through the Charity Organisation Society)' number; of women,who are thank- ful to earn a small sum by making garments most suit- able for the poor, and which can be obtained from the society mentioned above at very low prices. For men members we suggest cast off suits of clothing, clogs,and Charity blankets. Any persons willing to pin are re- quested to send in their names at once, to myself, or to any member of the committee. Circulars with names of committee can' be obtained from Mrs. James Fair- dough, hon. sec. December 12th is the date by which we hope to receive contributions :—Following parishes are those to which bundles were sent last year—The Parish, St. Paul's, St. Peter's. St. Barnabas', Holy Trinity St. Ann's, St. James', Latchford ;Christ Church, Wycliffe Congregational, St. John's Presbyteriaa, Wes- leyan, Bold-st. and Bewsey-road ; Cairo-st., St. Alban's, St. Mary's, and St. Mary's, Latchford. -A general meet- ing will be held on Wednesday next at three o'clock, at the Town Hall, when it is hoped that all who are interested in the guild will attend.—Yours truly, A. Dd. GREENALL. President Needlework Guild.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>LIQUOR TRAFFIC.</head>
        <p>THE LIQUOR TRAFFIC.</p>
        <p>Sir,—What good purpose the letter t.f " Neuter" un- der the head of" The drink traffic," will serve, I fail ti see,'and, if you will allow me to reply thereto, I shall feel greatly obliged. I grant Mr. Neuter that" nothing will be gained until the people are educated," and that plenty of entertainment (of a social character) other than the public house is a necessity in this town ; but what will deprive the people of this want more forcibly than the interests of a Town Council in the liquor traffic? Any sensible person knows lull well that those people Who require social education cannot and will not obtain it so long as there exists the depraving influence of so many drinking saloons, or hell-dens, as _are found in Warrinetrn at every few yards, and is it not appalling to think that the interests of these have indirectly served those of the majority of the Warrington town councillors I venture to assert that along with the many endeavours to relieve the prevailing distress in this town none will more permanently benefit the people in genera' than the elevation of the moral tone of the Town Council. I mean by this that men should be elected to the Council to serve the best interests of the town in which they have acquired the abundance of their prosperity in mer- chandise—not through the liquor traffic. Why do they not come forward and rend r that moral and social duty which they owe to the citizens of Warrington ? Why support men interested in the liquor traffic when we see around us on every side, poverty, misery and unem- ployed suffering through drink. Have we not a deplor- able picture of what a Town Council can do when we look at" Drunken Liverpool," and its almost dead local commerce. But then we need no comparison to give us a picture—the miserable insanitary places at Warring. ton called " home "speak for themselves, and point ti the evil—drink.</p>
        <p>Well, but it will be asked, what is the remedy,—it lies in the hands of the ministers of every denomination as well as the governing bodies of this town. It is all very well to preach social reform from the pulpit,but it is the Christian duty of all ministers to provide the want of entertainment and attraction which will meet the needs of those whose minds are dimmed by the pre- valence or drink and its surrounding influences. In a few words it iS the duty of every voter of this borough to see that all ministers and councillors do the initiation of this social reformation and give a helping trand in ac- cDrdsome with their station of life.—Yours truly. 62, Allen-street, Warrrington.</p>
        <p>F. SCIIOFiELD.</p>
        <p>THE COAL CRISIS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—What has the coal strike Cost? What will it cost? What are we paying for it? I am paying more for it than I ever expect to recover in my life time. Thous- ands say the same. Some say it is not a strike—it is a lock-out; I can see no difference. Mee have come out of pits where no reduction of wages was asked for, simply because it was the demand Of the federation. Such a demand, when both sides were satisfied, ia a piece of unmitigated tyranny and utterly inexcusable. There would be no lock-put in cases of this kind, but an unwarrirable interference with the liberty of the subject. A threat to a cod:cry manager in terms like this: " If your men continue at work I will not be responsible for what may happen." This is a threat pure and simple and a sample of unblushing impudence and self-conceit. What am we coming to? Our trade is paralized, and we are fast becoming a nation of paupers and the dis- trict swarms with organized and priOev.ed beggars,</p>
        <p>with coal coming into Manchester and Oldham from Swansea at lls, per ton, ss. for the coal and 6s. per ton freights by L. and N.-W. Railway. Scotland, burham, Staffordshire, Belgium and Germany, are all competitors with Lancashire and Yorkshire in the coal market. Let all the collieries stirt at full wages to-day and before Christmas coal will be as great a drug as it has ever been. It requires no gift of prophecy to see that. The demand will have fallen off, and leas coal than formerly used, pill be required ; and if work was limited to 3 or 4 days a week before the strike,a reduced demand means reduced work and less money in future. If it is a strike fora principle, even if successful, a terrible price will have been paid for it; if for money it will be an immense failure—end how it may. I have no hesitation in saying that the price we are paying now is extreme poverty beggary and social degradation; in the future less trade and less money, greater foreign competition, not only in our own coun- try, but among our customers in foreign countries whose trade we have lost through indulgence in the luxury of protracted strikes. If reduced wages brings less income to the shop-keeper and tradesman what does entire stop- page of the industry of the district mean 2 it means no income at all ; the begging-box man like the horse- leache's daughter cries, give, give, until the last coin is extracted and the giver and recipient are both alike, poverty-stricken and destitute.—l am, sir, yours respectfully,</p>
        <p>CYMRO.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>HOME RULE AND THE PROTESTANT MINORITY.</head>
        <p>HOME RULE AND THE PROTESTANT MINORITY.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Mr. J. C. Dalton in yours of Saturday, places before your readers a mass of untruths. History is against him, but, alas, as I take him to be a most bigot- ted Papist, therefore according to his creed, he is not allowed to read such books as Macaulay's "History of England," Addison 's MOSIIPiMS, Luther, Wycliffe, Jerome of Prague, Ridley, Knox, Lord Bacon, Camden, or even Father Paul, who wrote the " History of the Council of Trent," as all these writers, and many more are placed on the index of prohibited books, and may not be pos- sessed or read without incurring the guilt of mortal sin. Why, sir, I ask him who murdered the Protestants of Ireland in 1641? The Papists. Who unseated the mem- bers for North and South Meath last year? The Papists exclusively. Who intimidated the voters? Bishop Nulty in his pastoral, and only last week Judge Boyd in Dublin said of it, " He had never read a more shocking piece of I intimidation than the pastoral of Dr. Nulty." Mr. D. does this look like liberty even to the Papist, much less to the Protestant minority? (See Liverpool "Daily Post" and "Courier" of 25th). The Rev. Patrick O'Connell, 'preaching at Ballinabrackey, on •June 26, 1892, said that "no Parnellitte could receive the Sacrament worthily." Michael McKenna was denied absOlution by Rev. Mr. Fox. - (Welt he suffered no loss for Mr. Fox has not the power, it belongs to God only.) Mr. D. had better read "The Irish Priest in Politics, price 3d., to be got from 109, Grafton-st., Dublin. I must ask Mr. D. who massacred the Huguenots in Paris? The Papists. Who burnt the Protestants in Spain? The Papists. Who hunted the Waldenses and Albigenses like, hounds after a fox, killing them by thousands? The Papists. (See Grattan Guinness on Romanism and the Reformation.) What church is there beside Rome which issues Bulls teaching death to heretics? None. How can there be tolerance by the Papists when such a fiendish doctrine is now taught in Maynooth College— see filakeney's "Manual of Popery," page 200. By their fruits they are known. The system produces in Liver- pool 67 criminals out of 100, yet the population is only about a quarter. They get over half the benefit £3,000 out of the Liverpool Southern Hospital and don't contri- bute one hundred pounds or one-thirtieth part of what they obtain. Rome Rule is Home Rule so called. The Protestant places of worship in Rome would be closed at once if Leo XIII. only had the power; he has said so—see Times of April ilth, 1879. The present Pope wrote to King Alphonso of Spain," begging him in the name of the great Catholic country over which he reigns, to uso every effort of the civil power to banish Protes- tant missionaries and to confiscate their churches, schools, Sm. "—see monthly letter of the Protestant Alliance, Aug. 1886. Cardinal Manning in Essays on Religion and Literature, p. 403, rays: "Neither the Church nor the State, whensoever they are united on the true basis of Divine right, have any cognizance of tolerance." The modern Creed of Trent, 1564, says all are to be accursed, e., completely cursed who will not accept her dogma. It this medicine will not operate on Mr. D., I can give him a stronger dose from prescriptions by Bellarmine, Baronius, Newman and Wiseman.-7Tours truly,. Frodsham, Oct. 31, 1893.</p>
        <p>ROBERT FOIILKES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>MORALITY AND THE LOCK OUT.</head>
        <p>MORALITY AND THE LOCK OUT.</p>
        <p>Sir,—There are some points in the letter of Mr. A. Brouchemite in last issue that I should like to examine. This gentleman is an enthusiastic believer in the early advent of a labour milleniuna. Very good. But while I think it time labour had its innings, I much object to be tyrannised over, either by aristocrat or plebeian. In- deed,l think of the two,tyrannv by one is far better than tyranny by many. You might get a chance of striking the one head off, but even Hercules woult: have found it difficult to dispose of the Hydra by himself. Now, judg- ing from what has transpired in connection with this lock out, a labour tyranny is not by any means an im- probability. The miners have selected a set of men to represent them in Parliament and, as I think, to repre- sent also their ignorance and prejudices—a true repre- sentation in this case. As to the merits of the lock out, as between masters and men,l am not qualified to judge; but I hold strong opinions as to the folly and immorality of many of the actions and utterances of Mr. Brouche- mite's friends, led by Mr. S. Woods, Mr. Glover, Mr.Ald. Ben Tillett and others. When the coal owners had de- rided not to give the price demanded by the miners, the latter had nothing to do but quit and leave the ground clear for those who were willing to work. Did they do so ? No, they banded themselves together to prevent anyone working the pits they had deserted. Now, the chief use I can see of our rather expensive Government is to enforce the law and to preserve order—to safe- guard the liberties of the subject—thus enabling each and all to pursue his lawful occupation unmolested and unharmed. This salutary law the miners have seriously contravened, and the Federation which aids and abets stadi illegal practices is, to that extent, an illegal associa- tion, and a species of conspiracy against thg rights and privileges of the leiges. However, this is a matter to be settled between the Government and the miners who have so offended. But there is another count in the indictment—the mas- ters and the miners are not alone concerned in this mat- ter—the consumers are very considerably affected by this lock out. What then have they to say upon this burning question ? The Federation may be a powerful lever to move the masters—but are the consumers con- tent to act the part of fulcrum, to their damage and in- convenience ? Will they be content to hear their Gov- ernment maligned and aspersed by these labour leaders for defending their—the general public—interest in law and order? Men who have assumed the responsibility of members of Parliament ought to be the last persons to defend illegal acts—to deny their repression. In- stead of empty or interested chatterings about the res- ponsibility of owners in the matter of the riots, their plain duty was to impress upon their followers the res- ponsibility resting upon them not to interfere in any way with the choice of others working or not. In one way I am with Ben Tillett—the coal ought to be national property, that is, it cannot possibly be the property of any one man. It is absurd to assume ownership of a mineral which you have never seen, and don't know,but only infer to be there ! In assuming this the nation must not compensate for the ungot coal, which has no individual owner to be compensated, but merely for sinkage, plant, &amp;c. Royalties should share the fate of private ownership, and be abolished. For who can con- fer a share in that which is not his and which can only be obtained by the labour of ethers? As for the men who have suffered ill-usuage br work- . ..</p>
        <p>ing in tl-v? pits during the lock out—they ar-o distinctly social martyrs—martyrs in our cause—and should have had the full protection of. Imperial Government. The artificial bolstering up of prices is not in the interest of the community who have to pay the increase in shape of higher coal prices. In mining, labour as in other things, the market price should rule. Mr. Broucberoite in effect, recommends protection of miners' wages at the expense of the consuming public' and singularly enough suggests this as an ' advantage to the community. Yes! I once heard a working man auggest that, when the watermill was short of water, they should pump the water back again! I think the advantages are about equal. I am afraid your correspondent's views on the econo- mic question are unsound. but I must leave the econo- mists to deal with him in that case. I am more con- cerned in denouncing the foolish utterances of the leaders—the blind leaders of the blind, who, forgetting their own responsibilities, misplace the other responsibi- lity. fdr.Brouchemite says Mr.S. Woods, M.P.,has been giving £lO per week to the lock out fund. How much has he been giving to alleviate the distress, he and his have been causing? Ten times £lO would not have been too much for this object.—Yours truly. JOHN PEERS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>COAL TRADE CRISIS.</head>
        <p>THE COAL TRADE CRISIS.</p>
        <p>VIE CONFERENCE IN LONDON.</p>
        <p>_ At one o'clock yesterday, the joint conference of mineowners and miners opened in one of the large rooms of the Westminster Hotel, London, under the chairmanship of Mr. A. M: Chambers. Consid- erable excitement prevailed in the hotel precints. A later telegram stated that it was extremely doubtful whether any Settlement would be arrived at that day. There was much excitement among coal merchants and agents,but they- had no:informa- tion which would, in the least degree be useful to them. Public sympathy with the men was increas- ing substantially.</p>
        <p>Sir Edward Watkins has found his plan of bridging the Irish sea via the Isle of Man, impracticablc on technical grounds, but thinks the tunnel between Scotland and Ireland quite feasible at, say, the trifle of £10,000,000. Mr. Gladstone only takes 15 minutes to shake hands with 350 admirers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>EXAMINER. SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 4, 1893. NOTES ON GAMES ALD OUR LOCAL CHURCHES-WARRINGTON. ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER. SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 4, 1893. NOTES ON GAMES ALD OUR LOCAL CHURCHES-WARRINGTON. ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Yes, Warrington first team came out of tkeir shell, so to say, on Saturday. The " cocked hat" which was conferred upon them last week by Oldham, they very generously passed over to the lied Men from Salford. The latter accepted it with sufficient contriteness and humiliation—but didn't seem to care about demonstrat- ing their joy. From the very outset of the game a blind man might have seen which way play was going. It was,through the sloppy state of the ground, naturally a forward, and dirty game. And as such Warrington had all their own way—and like most people who have i their own way, they did not come to a bad end.</p>
        <p>Much of our home men's superiority was undoubtedly the outcome of a very careful course of practice which they have recently been undergoing at the " gym." Besides being well up in form, they showed a sense of combination quite uncommon to them these days. The game was unquestionably won by the forwards. During the whole of the match this part of the temp ki•pt play in the domains of the visitors. As I mentioned last week, Boscow4whs has:been out of form this season,was superceded by lieesom, who certainly showed much dashing and enthusiastic play, but was not guided,quito so much as Boscow would have been, by descretion. Then there was our old three-quarter friend, who has been missing for some time—H. Webster. He was per- suaded to grace the home ranks with his elongated self —which be did, in what might be called a " speedy " fashion. His speed in getting along was indeed many times a saving agency.</p>
        <p>During the early part ot the game, Holmes was foully tackled with the result that an injury to his ankle caused him to be carried from the field. This was the inaugura- tion of quite a series of acccidents, which, while quite serious enough to make those who sustained them mighty uncomfortable, were not bad enough to guarantee the services of the brandy bottle and stretcher. Shortly after this Potts kicked over to Manwaring,Salford's back, who fractiously allowed the ball to roll between his legs into touch, with the result that a scrimmage ensued,and Taylor crossed the line, Fair Turner failing to do the needful little kick. Well from now until half time, Sal- ford men were unable to rush Warrington from their domain.</p>
        <p>With the opening of the second half the visitors chances of scoring grew beautifully less and obscure. Their forwards all got tangled up and confused—and had it not been for Manwaring—assisted by his backs, War- rington would have pranced over the goal line uncom- monly often. Manwaring tried to do all the scoring himself towards the end. But it didn't act. Once he got the ball and had a clear course. He eluded Webster, his only obstacle and set out for the line when one of his own men valiantly ran up with the alacrity of an ante- lope, and w:th the wisdom of a Balaam's A., held his own full back till surrounded by Warringtou men. No—l could not hear what Mr. Manwaring said then. Shortly after this Potts, from a splendid pass from " Smack " Bate, got over, and Heesom kicked the goal. This was the last straw that broke the camel's back—or Salford's patience. They got into quite a frenzy and whilst strain- ing after camels got gnats. They made flying kicks at goal, and caused "line outs" or enabled Warrington to secure free kicks, and when they ran for a try, they were brought to earth before they could expose any of the fine sprints which they were said to have concealed about them.</p>
        <p>Yes, Warrington's victory of eight points to nil was indeed an encouraging one, but—oh! my prophetic uncle—who said Salford could secure that coveted tan- kard, this season. Pooh !—Commend me to the RoYing Wolves of Bewsey F.C. as a more likely team if entered as candidates.</p>
        <p>The following statistical table shows that Warrington have " clum" another rung of the tabulated ladder of results" which" as Samael Weller observed "1 begs to remark is a good phrase." Perpend : Phi. Won. Lost. Drn. GT. G.T. Pte. For. Agit. Wigan 6 . 4 . 0 . 2 . 3 5 ... 0 0.10 Swinton 6 . 4 . 0 . 2 . 5 8 ... 1 0 ...10 Oldham 6 . 4 . 1 . 1 .10 1i... 2 2 ... 9 Tyldesley Warrington 6 . 3 .. 3 . 0 ... 7 6 ... 4 7 ... 6 Broughton Rangers 6 ... 3 ... 3 ... 0 ... 6 2 ... 5 6 ... 6 Salford licehdale Hornets Barrow Broughton 6 . 0 . 6 ... 0 ... 2 1 ...16 11 ... 0</p>
        <p>The Midland Counties and Western Counties match came off on 'I uesday, at Leicester. The Midland team underwent consicleraple alteration from the advertised fifteen. A start was made at twenty minutes past three, May kicking off for the Western representatives. Alter a spell of work in front of the Midland goal,play became of a very even charactra. A splendid display of passing almost enabled the Midlanders to score near the corner. The Midland team had the best of the first half,but there was no score. Crossing over the Midlanders showed to greater advantage, but play was of a fairly interesting character. Bradshaw opened the scoring with a try from a pass by Tuke, and Byrne failed, the wind spoiling a good shot. A dashing run by Tuke took the play close to the Westerners' goal ,and Reynolds scored a try,Byrne this time placing a goal. The Midlanders, eight points to the good, played in dashing style, and Rice further augmented their total by gaining another try. The re- sult of a good game was a victory for the Midland Coun- ties by a goal and two tries to nothing.</p>
        <p>Sides :—Midland Counties : F. J. Byrne (Moseley), I,baek ; R. Hesmondhalgh (Leicester), H. T. Reynolds (Stratford-on-Avon), andtbe Rev. H. C. Wilkinson (Lei- cester), three-quarter backs ; B.H. Tuke (Coventry) and H. Simpson (Leicester), half backs ; C.Norbury (Worces- ter), G. Jones (Worcester), W. Rice (Coventry), J. R. Bradshaw (Coventry), C. J. Hill (Coventry), A .W. Gorton (Burton-on-Trent), H. W. T. Patterson (Nottingham), A. E. Cooke (Leicester), and the Rev. W. H. Carey (Leices- ter), forwards. Western Counties; P. Webber (Devon- shire), back ; C. F. Mermagen (Somerset), N. Stoyle (Devon), W. E. Bowden (Devon), and C. V. VVinsor (Devon),three-quarter backs ; .Merry(Somerset)and 11. George (Gloucester),half backs ; T.V.Davies (Gloucester) J.Leighton (Gloucester), F. H .Toller (Devon) ,G. H. Allin g- ton (Devon), A. May (Devon), E. Sloper (Devon), J. T. Tavirty (Devon), and J. Taylor (Somerset), forwards. Referee- Mr. E. 'I. Gurdon, English Rugby Union.</p>
        <p>A FESTIVE DAY AT DUTTON WORKHOUSE. A remarkably successful treat was given to the inmates of Dutton Workhouse, on Tuesday evening. After the completion of the ordinary business of the Board, a large number of the Guardians remained behind to assist in making the affair a thoroughly suc- cessful one. They began, of course, by enjoying them- selves at a substantial meat tea, but, wonderful to re- late, at their own expense, for although they sit at the board they do not board where they sit. This function over, in which Mr. Littler's glee party participated, the great event of the evening came on, and right heartily all present did their best to make it a success. the in- mates, children and all, to the number of 160 odd sat down in the dining hall and 401bs.of beef ,with chickens, sausages, &amp;c., were served up all hot, the bedridden not being forgotten, as dainty cuts from tender joints were sent to them. During the meal Mr. Littler's party dis- coursed sweet music, thus anticipating the time when— " The poor shall dine off buttered crumpets, And eat roast mutton to the sound of trumpets." At the entertainment which followed Mr. Littler's glee party rendered eight or nine pieces very efficiently, their services being highlz appreciated by inmates and visitors. The Rev. J. Hanby manipulated his lime-light apparatus exhibiting over 100 views, many of them being trans- parencies of local places and personages and were the manufacture of Mr. Heywood. The views of Mr. Parkin- son and the Rev. J. Hanby (the Gulliver and Lilliput of the Board) breaking stones, were highly amusing and amongst the best appreciated were pictures of Mr. Can- nab, Mr. Heywood, Drs. Robinson and Selby, Mr. Wil- liams, views of the children themselves, the band, a group of Mr. M'lntyre, Mr. Ashton, Mr. Farrington, and last but nGt least the Rev. J. W. N. Tanner. During the entettainment fruit was handed round to all. Amongst the visitors were Mrs. Baxter, Mrs. Parkinson, Mrs. T. Ford, Mrs. T. Lea, Mrs. A. Norman, and Mrs. Hanby, while of the Guardians present Mr. Mclntyre, chairman, (who had kindly remained to give the official stamp to the affair), and Messrs. Baxter, Heywood, Williams, Jos. Baguley, Stokes, Woodward, S. Taylor, Parkinson, Lea, Ford, Handley, Norttan, Whitlow, D. H. Jones, and Hanby. It was very much regretted that Mr. Yarwood who had worked for the entertainment as Mr. Hanby said " Lke a navvy" was absent through illness but in- mates and visitors enthusiastically authorised Mr. Hanby to send a telegram of thanks and wishes for speedy recovery. A great deal of the labour had fallen upon Mr. Lea to whom and Mr. Yarwood and Messrs. Gamon and Rowe, Halton, thanks are due for carrikge of gods from Runcorn and Halton. The Halton Wesleyan Sun- day School lent tea urns, and the bulk of the provisions were given by the Guardians, hut thanks are due to others who responded to the solicitations of Mr. Yar- wood and Mr. Hanby. During the afternoon the ladies present visited the•women's wards and were delighted with the cleanliness and order everywhere manifest and also with the bright and happy faces of the children. The behaviour of the children during the entertainment considering the extreme youth of some of thorn reflected the utmost credit upon their teachers. To see the little girls in their warm frocks, clean pinafores, and white tuckers, and the little boys with warm suits and snow white collars was ample reward for the labour involved in the affair. Special thanks were vociferously given to Mr. and Mrs. Cunnah who with hearty goodwill had given themselves to the task of making all the necessary preparationi and with thanks to all, an evening came to an and which will be long remembered.</p>
        <p>Mr. Thomas J. Lipton has obtained the highest and only award for Teas in the British Section at the Chicago Exhibition, 815 D the highest award or Coffee and Coffee Essence.</p>
        <p>ST. JOHN'S PRESBYTERIAN CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. Warnock, M.A., BD., preached on the subject of "The spirits in prison " at St. John's Presby- terian Church, Winwick-street, on Sunday evening. There was a large congregation, and the sermon was listened to with much attention. THE P.S.A. AT WYCLIFFE CHURCH. ' The usual P.S. A. meeting at Wycliffe Church on Sun- day afternoon was largely attended, the membership having now reached the total of 986. Mr. George Brown occupied the chair, .and delivered a short address on " Man" in which he pointed out the real characteristics which made true manhood. The speaker for the after- noon was Mr. Harrowsmith, of Northwich, who based his remarks on the text " Hold fast." Miss Kent was the soloist, and she sang two solos in an admirable man- ner. Mr. W. H. Payton was the accompanist. The meeting was also taken part in by the Rev. J. Yonge, Messrs. B. Farrar, and H. Barker. Mr. Yonge an- nounced that he had received Ll from the pastor of Chapel-street Congregational Church, Southport, to re- lieve the distress, which he had handed over to the tre.isurer of the benevolent fund in connection with the , brotherhood.</p>
        <p>A BAPTISMAL SERVICE AT GOLBORNE-STREET.</p>
        <p>A baptismal service was held in Golborne-street Baptist Chapel, Warrington, on Sunday evening, con- ducted by the pastor, the Rev. D. W. Roberts. There was a large attendance. Three candidates were im- mersed, and it is satisfactory to state that 25 candidates have now been immersed at this chapel during the twelve months pastorate of the Rev. D. W. Roberts. The preacher took his text from Matthew 3, 15 v. " Thus it becometh us to fulfil all righteousness." After referring to the baptisin of Christ, he said that as He was their example pertaining to all other things so He was in rela- tion to baptism. Baptism, which was immersion, was a strictly personal act, an act of personal surrender, re- solved upon and carried out by the will of the baptized, and not an act performed by one in the place of another. Christ was immersed by His own deliberate will. Some agreed with all this, but said baptism was a mere rite, an external for christianity, a nen-essential, un- important, and therefore not imperative. The preacher contended that that opinion would not bear the test supplied by their Lord's baptism. The fact that Christ Himself thought it necessary for Him to be baptised thus, ought to silence once and for all men's shallow talk that they did not need to observe this" form "of bap- tism. Special hymns were sung during the service. Mr. F. Hickman presided at the;organ.</p>
        <p>CIVIC DUTY AND 'THE MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS. Speaking in Cairo-st. Chapel on Sunday evening from the words " Thy kingdom come: Thy will be done on earth as it is in leaven," the Rev. W. H. Drummond called attention to the great awakening which was tak- ing place to the real meaning of the words of Christ. Men were feeling with the ardour of a new discovery that he meant what he said, and was dealing with real men acd real problems. He gave them, not indecd the mechanism of a new society, but an heroic standard of , public and private virtue. A stronger sense of social responsibility was one of the results of this awakening. Some blame for the injustice and ignorance which pre- vailed in any society must rest upon all its members. It would not do to lay it to the charge of particular indivi- duals or the Government, for every citizen had his share in what Ruskin called,"the invisible government . . . exercised by all energetic and intelligent men, each in his sphere regulating the inner will and secret ways of , the people, essentially forming its character and prepar- ing its fate." The preacher trieg went on to ask, "Have we as Christian citizens realized our social responsibility in Warrington ? Are we content with the scenes in its streets and the comfort and peace of its homes ? Have we been able to read the Chief Constable's yearly state- ment without a fresh access of shame that such things should be? Is this a town where men have striven to make it easy to do right and hard to do wrong ?" These were very earnest questions, which every true man, every professing Christian was bound to put to himself. It might be asked what could individuals do. Well, it was the duty of every individual to see to it that the executive government of the town was committed to the charge of men who had its moral welfare truly at heart, men who combined with business capacity and counnonsense the most enlightened views of social well- being and the noblest ideals of social righteousness. No indifference, or timidity, or party spirit should be al- lowed to interfere with this aim. The municipal elec- tion was quite as important as a Parliamentary election, and the municipal vote was fraught with sacred respon- sibility. With these principles clear before his mind, it must be left to each man's conscience how he should exercise it. In conclusion, the preacher reminded his hearers how the Seer in Patmos had seen a vision of the New Jerusalem coming down out of heaven, the city in whose streets he had walked, with whose people he had lived in daily fellowship, sanctified and sacred. Why should not they have a vision of the New Warrington, purified from " whatsoever worketh abomination or maketh a lie," adorned with beauty and justice. Let them, at any rate, keep before them a high ideal of civic duty, and a high ideal of civic attainment, and let them be prepared for the long effort, the personal sacri- fices needful to reach it. Only in this way could they rescue the great Christian prayer from a tar away dreamland, and unite it once more to the actual tasks and daily duties of men. SPECIAL SERMON AT WYCLIFFE. SOCIAL EVILS IN WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. Yonge preached a special sermon in Wyclliffe Church, on Sunday evening, on the subject " The Relation of Christianity and Churches to Social Reforms." The preacher took for his text "He beheld the city,and wept over it." Luke xix c.,41. v. Mr.Yonge said his great anxiety was to lead all whom his influence could touch in this town to the earnest and practical considera- tion for its social conditions and needs. They knew that unless they were very observant people, they did not get any accurate or impressive idea of the character of a town, or of a population, by merely passing along its thoroughfares, casting casual glances upon the people whom they met. Indeed, we got to walk the streets, especially of our own town, mechanically, far more absorbed in our own personal errands or purposes, than interested in what was passing around us. That being so, he took it for granted, that a large number of the better living people of this town, a large majority of themselves, had but the barest and feeblest suspicions of the</p>
        <p>and titled millionaire sitting with his family and fliends in cushioned, curtained luxury in the palatial hall, and on the othcr hand, the wretched slave of poverty, and drink,and dirt, crouching his family in his unwholesome abode in the ‘Varrington slum ! It was an awful reflec- tion, warranted only by too actual truth, that the slum had often helped to build the palace, and the palace often helped to continue the slum ! The greatest fortunes in this town had been made from drink. The greatest miseries of this town were</p>
        <p>CREATED BY DRINK ! He placed those utterly indisputable facts before them, and challenged their study of them. He asked them not to flinch from facing them, lie asked them to follow out their suggestiveness. What valid hope was there of improving the condition of the people, until the chief cause of popular degradation, of domestic wretehedness and squalor, of moral and physical deterioration, was shorn of its at present unhindered power ? He confessed that he couldn't comprehend the apathy of Christian people on this crying evil of the age! Least of all could he understand the apathy which was so conspicuous respecting it in this town. They possessed an unenvi- able notoriety in Warrington in this particular. Yet their own Christian people did not seem to realise it. Would they go once or twice to the Police Court, and see the daily haul of victims ? and then hear the conven- tional moralizings of justices who did everything they could to keep up the trade which manufactured the vic- tims ! Would they just study their streets on Saturday nights ? They would see, then, enough to explain, at least in dreadful measure, the awful conditions of per- sonal and family life which prevailed in hundreds of homes ! And they would find reason for Christian inter- ference and agitation and reform. But there was another giant source of evil, which was marring many lives, and destroying many homes, in this town. And so far it had been as difficult t) get at, and battle with, as the Demon of Drink. He referred now to the</p>
        <p>PREVALENCE OF GAMBLING. He had ' it on the authority of the Chief Constable that in Warrington this vice was fearfully prevalent,and increasingly so. Indeed, the evidence was overwhelm- ing that throughout the country this evil was growing by leaps and bounds. We had ruling powers in this town who would not take the steps which other towns were taking. For some reason or other,they were afraid or were unwilling to move; and so these betting and gambling shops had in Warrington free trade ! ! Here again, as a Christian and a Christian minister, interested in the morals of the town, and in the uplifting of its people—there was reason for Christian interference and agitation and reform. Mr. Yonge afterwards alludad to the formation of the Social Questions Union in the town, and stated that the object was to remove the social evils he had enumerated,and commended the union as a move- ment of the most Christian character, and of utmost soclal importance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>VOLUNTEER PRIZE DISTRIBUTION AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>VOLUNTEER PRIZE DISTRIBUTION AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Last night a good audience assembled in Runcorn Town Hall, on the occasion of the annual concert and distribution of prizes in connection with the Runcorn (F and (I) Companies ot the 2nd (Earl of Chester's) V.B. Cheshire Regiment. The concert was admirably carried out by Mr. Joseph Cantor's party which included himself, Madame Laura Smart, Mr. S. Kirkham, Mr. Ben Roberts and Mr. Frederic Owens. The volunteer band also rendered several selections. During an interval in the pro- gramme Lady Brooke, of N orton Priory, presented the prizes to the successful volunteers. As will be seen from the appended particulars, Sergeant Harvey again heads the list. He is the absolute winner of the gold medal, having secured the decoration on four occa,ions, and he is further the holder of the Lady Brooke Challenge Cup. A vote of thanks was accorded to her ladyship for her presence and for distributing the prizes. Captain D. Ashton, in the course of a speech, said he was glad to say that the I? and G Companies had kept up their strength during the past year. There were 147 efficients as compared with 148 last year, and 13 proficients as compared with 12. In camp there were 130 members of the Companies present, being a decrease of ten on last year. There were 18 marksmen this year, last year there were 21. There were no third class shots, which was a cause for congratulation. The company drills had been fairly well attended. At least fifty men were required to complete the strength of the two companies and Captain Ashton urged the young men of the town to join. They would, he said, drive great benefit from association with the volunteer movement. The following is a list of prizes :</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>DETACHMEXT CIMPETITON.</head>
