      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>EVIL CONDITIONS.</head>
        <p>EVIL CONDITIONS.</p>
        <p>in which many el their fellow townspeople lived from year to year. Most of them for example,lived in decent and comfortable dwellings, some of them might be com- paratively humble, but they were clean, orderly, and comfortable : some of them might dwell in even still better houses, and be surrounded with many luxuries. Life at home with them was, at any rate, fairly attrac- tive and congenial. They did not need to go outof doors to find the ordinary comforts of life, such as warmth, light, and congenial companionship. But, did they ever give any thought to the exactly opposite conditions which prevailed in scores and liundredsiof houses in this town ? It was an old saying that MO half the world did not know how the other half lived. It might possibly be a startling revelation if they could have a lantern exhibi- tion, here and now, of contrasting scenes taken from the same town and community ; taken from life in our own streets. Be was not sure whether it would not be a wise and Christian thing to do, if it could be done, with- out creating any personal bitterness, to present, for ex- ample, a dwelling, in Bold-street. Palniyra-square,Wilson t'atten,street or Grappenhall,contrasted with a dwelling in Dial-street, Haydock-street,Baek Stamford-street, and some of those streets at the far end of Winwick-road. Let the pictures be, not simply of external appearance. which would be toterablv diverse; but of internal cir- cumstanees ; which would be more diverse and impres- sive still. The one class of picture would present illus- trations of life itl favourable conditions, and the other would present illustrations of life in conditions which to us, at least, would appear utterly hostile to health of body, or mind, or soul. In recent weeks, some of their own ladies had come face to face with these facts ; they had</p>
        <p>„ PrNETRATED CpURTB AND SLUMS and had entered dwellings and houses, the squalid nature of which they otherwise could hardly have imagined. Some people indeed recoiled from 'the idea of any such visitation because fearful of the uncomely experiences they might have to endure. And assuredly they would meet with heartrending spectacles greatly intensified In their woelulness of the present distress, which had emptied the homes of what few comforts some of them possessed. But he was not now referring to the homes of distress and want that were so numerous just now : but to the squalid, unhealthy, miserable houses in which many poor families found a wretched shelter year in, year out. Do not run away with the idea that this town has no black spots, no insanitary areas in it. It has only too many. There were some portions of this town which had been long since CONDEMNED BY THE MEDICAL AUTHORITY as absolutely unfit for human abode; yet still these places were tenanted by people whose familiarity with squalor had made them callous to its experiesce. It would open their eyes, and stir their hearts if they would get a competent conductor, and go and examine for themselves courts, alleys, Alums in the ” good old town Of Warrington," where only dirt and drink, degra- dation, disease, and death, could find congenial dwel- ling. It might also as they enquired,surprise them to find that some of this insanitary property belonged to people who lived in the more reputable and suburban portions of the town. And further, that these property owners, some of them, either had been or were vet, occupying positions as leaders of society, members of the Corpora- tion, and worshippers in the churches They took the rents of this property, and actually thereby drew their own comforts from these miseries ! It might be rather striking if they could throw upon a screen a picture sf the landlord, and a picture of the tenant; of the land- lord's own dwelling, And the tenants's gruesome hovel, and let them look at them ! Yes, it would be powerful, surely ; almost alarming and terrorizing, if in some most public - shop in town a giant lantern show could be devised of the contrast of society! Those contrasts were something fearful, and it fright be well, as a mesns of suggestive instruc- tion, to let the populace see the picture of the weavky</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <p>DISTRESS IN WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The condition of affairs in Warrington, conse- quent upon the coal dispute, is practically the same as last week. The Relief Committee make a very earnest appeal for funds, and if the funds had not come in at aquicker rate,they would have had to close their soup kitchens altogether. Considering that about 6,000 persons are almost entirely depending upon this means of subsistence, if these fears had proved to be well grounded, the condition of affairs in the town would have been appalling, and the consequence would have been difficult to forecast. The Whitecross Wire Works have been almost" on full time " in the wire department during the present week, although the forge has not been in that happy position. Monks, Hall and Co., are still completely closed, and Houghton's Wire Works are completely stopped in the steel furnace and forge departments. Ryland's Wire Mills are in work, but Hallam Forge is only partly opened.</p>
        <p>RELIEF COMMITTEE'S WEEKLY MEETING. A MORE SATISFACTORY REPORT.</p>
        <p>The weekly meeting of the Relief Committee was held yesterday afternoon in the Council Chamber of the Town Hall, the Mayor occupying the chair. Mr. Monks said that he had a much more satisfac- tory report to give than last week. The quantities of food distributed during the week had been much the same as during the preceding week, the soup being 23,196 pints, compared with 2,3256 last week, bread 29193 lbs against 29486, tea and sugar 1279 packets against 1335. The cost this week had been £169 lls. 9d. against £lB3 us. 11d, last week. This decrease in the cost was explained by the larger amount of food and materials sent in during the present week, thus making the actual money payment less than the amount of food given away. The total ; amount subscribed since the commencement of the relief was £1391 17s. 10d. the amount expended being £1194 Bs. sd. Mr. Stanley Wilson had estimated the expendi- ture up to this week to be £1193 6.5 d., which showed how near Mr. Wilson's estimate had been to the mark. The balance now in hand was £197 93. sd. The result of the Committee's appeal at last week's meeting was, that £l6B 153. sd. had been received in subscriptions this ;week against £ll5 83. id. last week,and adding to this the weekly subscriptions of £3O this made a total of £193 153. sd. The Mayoress had also collected £l4 13s. on behalf of the distribution of tea and sugar so that the t ital amount received this week had been £213 83. 51 He had to announce that an acknowledgment had been re- ceived from the Local Government Board to theresolution passed by the Committee a fortnight ago. There was a another matter to which he wished to call attention. Ile had received a letter from Mr. Ernest Lees of the Flor- ence Mills, with reference to some remarks made at the last meeting. He said that he had seen from the War- rington papers that imputations had been made of the Florence Mills that the works were closed and that the owners had done nothing to relieve the distress occas- ioned thereby. He thought that these remarks were made in ignorance of what had been done. At the com- mencement of the strike they offered their workpeople daily, hot pots, etc., but they did not want food, and wished to work. The works were accordingly started again at a reduction of a penny in the shilling, but they could not keep the place going. They had therefore to stop the works. He had sent to Mr. Wilson £1 for the relief fund which he had not seen acknowledged. Mr. Monks said that Mr. Wilson had duly acknowledged the amount named to the firm. Mr. Lees appeared to be labouring under a misapprehension. What was said last week was that it would be a great deal cheaper for the committee to pay the extra cost of fuel rather than give relief to the,Florence Mill bands in consequence of the stoppage, but he was glad to give publicity to the letter. The executive were informed that it was very likely that a large number of men employed by the Ship Canal Co. would be out of work shortly. He would remind the secretaries of the sub-committees that the relief was for men thrown out of work by the coal dispute,and not for unemployed not connected with the strike.—On the motion of the Mayoress, seconded by Mrs. Carleton, it was resolved to continue the distribution of tea and sugar this week end.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>SANKEY BRIDGES.</head>
        <p>SANKEY BRIDGES.</p>
        <p>The following are the members who have had free teas at the Wesleyan Scheolroom, Sankey Bridges, dur- ing the present week. Saturday 150 men, Tuesday, 450 children, Wednesday, 220 women, Thursday, 480 chil- dren. After the tea on Saturday, a miscellaneous en- tertainment was given, the programme consisting of songs, speeches, &amp;c, Mr. T. Brough presiding at the piano. The tea and entertainment on Wednesday was given by and was entirely under the control of members from the Penlceth Wesleyan Chapel. The Rev. T. Gun- nell who presided at the entertainment, spoke upon tire subject of the coal dispute, and compared it with the state of England at the close of the Franco-German war, when provisions were very dear, and said that they ,were not so dear at the present time, which was a thing to be thankful for.—Mr. R. Garnett, J.P., thought that the coa.lowners had made a great blunder at the commencement of the strike, and considered that the effect of the strike would be felt for many years to come. Miss Moses next sang " In golden threshholds," and Mrs. Garnett gave a reading "Lined with gold" and also a short speech. Mrs. Cook gave an excellent ad- dress upon the subject "Elijah feeding the ravens." A vote of thanks to the chairman proposed by Mr. Brough and seconded by Mr. George, brought a very interesting meeting to a termination.</p>
        <p>THE REV. OWEN WATKINS ON THE DISTRESS.</p>
        <p>At the service on Sunday evening, at the Wesleyan Church, Bewsey-road, the pastor, the Rev. Owen Watkins, informed the congregation that it was neces- sary for him to make a special appeal. Be said that the Central Relief Committee found themselves face to</p>
        <p>face with a serious difficulty. They had spent nearly all the money that had thus far been given to them, and unless very considerable sums came to hand,it would be imperative for the committee to consider the question of reducing the quantity of provisions supplied to those in need. The amount given was not too great, being only a pound of bread and a pint of soup for each adult person per day. If it were to be reduced tq a half a pound of bread and a half a pint of soup his hearers would understand what a terrible privation that would be. To give an idea of the suffering that was going on he mentioned a case of spoor man who had lost his lost his vile and had been turned out of his lodgings,and who walked daily from Widnes for his bread and soup, a friend there having given him shelter. He gave instances of other pathetic circumstances showing how necessary it was that the fund should be maintained. lie appealed earnestly to every member of the congre- gation, who had not yet contributed, to either give a donation or make a weekly payment as long as the distress lasted.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER CONCERT IN AID OP TBE DISTRESS. The National Schools, Church-street. were well filled on Tuesday evening with an appreciative audience, the occasion being a concert in aid of the Warrington Dis- tress Fund, provided by the Clergy Daughter's School. The Rector, (Rev. F. W. Willis), was the chairman, and in the course of a few introductory remarks thanked those present fer their attendance. Referring to the pupils at the Clergy Daughters School, he said they had parted witli s mie of their sweetmeats, aid yet they hal not lost any of their sweetness. He did know these ladles had been denying themselves of their sweetness to the extent of 255, p. r week, and had handed it, through their head mistress, to the Mayor for the distress fund. The girls had denied themselves for the good of their suffering fellow creatures. (Applause.) The programme was then proceeded with ,and comprised part songs by the pupils, pianoforte and violin solos ,and duets; and songs , by the Revs. A. A. Markham, and M. Stevenson, Miss MacCarthy, and pianoforte solbs by Miss Ridgway. An attractive feature of the instrumental part of the pro- gramme was the double trio (pianoforte) by six of the pupils of the school, as was also the pianoforte, violin, and violoncello trio (Pleyell).. The Rev. Morley Steven- son was a favourite with the audience, and received two encores for his fine rendition of the songs " The Mer- maid "and "The Miller and the Maid," Miss Mac- Carthy's first song was " Funicula t Funicula " (C Deem) the refrain being taken up by the pupils, and in 'The song and the singe);" (Parker) a fine effect was produced by the double accompaniment of violin and pianoforte. Miss MacCarthy's other song was "The Trout " Schubert). The. Rev. A. A. Markham's songs were " To Althea front Prison," " Bonnie Leslie," and " Tom Bowling " and were made the most of by the. vocalist. Miss Ridgway contributed two' pianoforte solos " Maiurka " (Godard) and "Spinning song'" i(Retten). The whole of the plogramme was exceed- ngly well gone through, and reflected great credit upon all concerned. an'S24</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>DISTRESSED MINERS.</head>
        <p>THE DISTRESSED MINERS.</p>
        <p>The Trade Union Congress P-arliamentary Committee on Thursday issed a further appeal the organised workers of the United Kingdon for pronipt and generous.assis- tance to the misers. The document states that many societies have responded to the appeal from the. Belfast Congress, but the large number affected, half a million, necessitated still greater efforts. Many large firms have already conceded the old rate of wages,and it is believed that many more, if not all, will be induced to do so. The fact that the original demand for 25 per cent reduction has been reduced to 15 shows that tne course first pur- sued was not justified. The Comniittee therefore entreat that whore possible additional efforts may be made, especially for the relief of distressed wives and childrea. ' Cheques may be sent to Thomas Ashton, 925, Ashton- road, Fairfield, Manchester, payable to Enoch Edwards. The appeal is signed by John Buses. James Mawasley, William Inskip,klenry Broadbu,rstJohn 11. Wilson,David Holmes, Bea Tillett, Jame* Jack, Jahn liodgee and Charles Fsnwieli.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1893-11-04_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <p>THE LATE MR. JAMES CROSTON, J•l3.l°F MANCHESTER. At the quatterly meerin2. of the Council ol tilet Newton Division Conservative Association belddaie the Central Offices, Newton, on Nionday, "ded motion of the Chairrnan,Mr. T. If. Pearson,see°'_es by Mr. W. Valiant, the following resolution 'to adopted " That this meeting of the Council Newton Division Conservative Association desir,, to express its great regret and sorrow at the deft,t'o of James Croston, Esq., J.P., and sincerely offershy the members of his family their deepest symPat in the great loss they have sustained.'</p>
        <p>ENTERTAINMENT AT BURTONWOOD:</p>
        <p>On Monday evening a most successful entel ment was given in the church schoolroom, 1331 wood, by the friends of Mr. W. T. Sutton o James, the Less, Liverpool. Tie Rev. A. Mitchell, vicar presided, an 4 the room was croW with an enthusiastic audience. An excellen gramme was sumnitted, songs being gi ren Hetty 3lawdsley, Miss Ogley, Mr. B. Won's' Mr. W. A.Jackson gave one or two selections ° violin. The humorous portion of the enter ment was undertaken by Mr. J. McDermot,7 selections caused much merriment. blessrs, Seddon and Sutton accompanied. EARLESTOWN CONSERVATIVE amt. In the skittle alley of the above club, whit been decorated for the occasion, an entertait was given to the members and their wives,on, nesday evening by Professor J. L. Wyman. ' was a very large number present, and the e tainment which consisted of sleight of hand t.t ventriloquism and shadowgraph was much en)c Professor Wyman proved himself an adept gt juring, and his ventriloquial contribution really clever. The shadowgraphy caused el amusement, and the entertainment all throng° clever. At the close a hearty vote of thanks accorded the entertainer and Mr. Chorlten introducing him, on the motion of Major Set°,°,:i Chorlton replied hoping that would not be tn° visit Professor Wyman would pay to the club. EARLESTOWN V. }TORY/ICH llennlnnS'An• The above club met at Earlestown on Satur"l] an eight miles steeplechase. Horwich broug strong team. This being the first match of alee organized Earlestown Club, it was regarded kind of test as to their real claims to cross On°, running. The result must have caused their , porters the highest gratification as the Iturne.,,t ran wonderfully well and gained a hollow v 1) by over GO points, claiming 10 men out of the 12. The Earlestown six only totalled 23 puilqe the distance between their first man and the was under 300 yards. After a pleasant On', evening the Horwich team departed hontefti fully satisfied as to their reception if not wit“l, want of success. Next Saturday, Earlestown nay to Leigh to meet their Leigh trio&amp; t Haulgh Harriers (Bolton) in a triple c°l3 Charley Souch will tnrn out for Leigh.</p>
